helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04040000.xhp	0	help	par_id3154762	41			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"SCH:CHECKBOX:DLG_AXIS:CB_Y_SECONDARY\"\>The major axis and the secondary axis can have different scaling. For example, you can scale one axis to 2 in. and the other to 1.5 in. \</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050000.xhp	0	help	tit				0	lt	Y Error Bars				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050000.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147428	1			0	lt	\<link href=\"text/schart/01/04050000.xhp\" name=\"Y Error Bars\"\>Y Error Bars\</link\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050000.xhp	0	help	par_id3149666	2			0	lt	\<variable id=\"statistik\"\>\<ahelp hid=\".\"\>Use the \<emph\>Y Error Bars\</emph\> dialog to display error bars for 2D charts.\</ahelp\>\</variable\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050000.xhp	0	help	par_id3401287				0	lt	An error bar is an indicator line that spans over a range from y - NegativeErrorValue to y + PositiveErrorValue. In this term, y is the value of the data point. When "standard deviation" is selected, y is the mean value of the data series. NegativeErrorValue and PositiveErrorValue are the amounts calculated by the error bar function or given explicitly.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050000.xhp	0	help	par_id3153965	23			0	lt	The \<emph\>Insert - Y Error Bars\</emph\> menu command is only available for 2D charts.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050000.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150344	5			0	lt	Error category				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050000.xhp	0	help	par_id3150202	6			0	lt	In the \<emph\>Error category\</emph\> area, you can choose different ways to display the error category.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050000.xhp	0	help	hd_id3152989	7			0	lt	None				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050000.xhp	0	help	par_id3149409	8			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"SCH:RADIOBUTTON:TP_STAT:RBT_NONE\"\>Does not show any error bars.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050000.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145117	17			0	lt	Constant value				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050000.xhp	0	help	par_id3151390	18			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"SCH:METRICFIELD:TP_STAT:MTR_FLD_MINUS\"\>Displays constant values that you specify in the Parameters area.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050000.xhp	0	help	hd_id3159204	13			0	lt	Percentage				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050000.xhp	0	help	par_id3150048	14			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"SCH:METRICFIELD:TP_STAT:MTR_FLD_PERCENT\"\>Displays a percentage. The display refers to the corresponding data point. Set the percentage in the Parameters area.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050000.xhp	0	help	hd_id8977629				0	lt	Functions				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050000.xhp	0	help	par_id7109286				0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\".\"\>Select a function to calculate the error bars.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050000.xhp	0	help	par_id5154576				0	lt	Standard Error: Displays the standard error.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050000.xhp	0	help	par_id3157979	10			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\".\"\>Variance: Displays the variance calculated from the number of data points and respective values.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050000.xhp	0	help	par_id3153249	12			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\".\"\>Standard Deviation: Displays the standard deviation (square root of the variance).\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050000.xhp	0	help	par_id3149870	16			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\".\"\>Error Margin: Displays the highest error margin in percent according to the highest value of the data group. Set the percentage in the Parameters area.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050000.xhp	0	help	hd_id350962				0	lt	Cell Range				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050000.xhp	0	help	par_id6679586				0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\".\"\>Click Cell Range and then specify a cell range from which to take the positive and negative error bar values.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050000.xhp	0	help	par_id3872188				0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\".\" visibility=\"hidden\"\>Click a button to shrink the dialog, then use the mouse to select the cell range in the spreadsheet. Click the button again to restore the dialog to full size.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050000.xhp	0	help	hd_id2633747				0	lt	From Data Table				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050000.xhp	0	help	par_id6633503				0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\".\"\>For a chart with its own data, the error bar values can be entered in the chart data table. The Data Table dialog shows additional columns titled Positive Y-Error-Bars and Negative Y-Error-Bars.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050000.xhp	0	help	par_id0428200810573839				0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\".\" visibility=\"hidden\"\>Enter the value to add to the displayed value as the positive error value.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050000.xhp	0	help	par_id0428200810573862				0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\".\" visibility=\"hidden\"\>Enter the value to subtract from the displayed value as the negative error value.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050000.xhp	0	help	par_id0428200810573844				0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\".\" visibility=\"hidden\"\>Enter the address range from where to get the positive error values. Use the Shrink button to select the range from a sheet.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050000.xhp	0	help	par_id0428200810573970				0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\".\" visibility=\"hidden\"\>Enter the address range from where to get the negative error values. Use the Shrink button to select the range from a sheet.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050000.xhp	0	help	hd_id0428200810573977				0	lt	Same value for both				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050000.xhp	0	help	par_id0428200810573991				0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\".\"\>Enable to use the positive error values also as negative error values. You can only change the value of the "Positve (+)" box. That value gets copied to the "Negative (-)" box automatically.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050000.xhp	0	help	hd_id3156396	19			0	lt	Error indicator				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050000.xhp	0	help	par_id3150539	20			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_SCH_CT_INDICATE2\"\>Specifies the error indicator.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050000.xhp	0	help	hd_id0428200810574027				0	lt	Positive and Negative				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050000.xhp	0	help	par_id0428200810574039				0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\".\"\>Shows positive and negative error bars.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050000.xhp	0	help	hd_id0428200810574031				0	lt	Positive				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050000.xhp	0	help	par_id042820081057411				0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\".\"\>Shows only positive error bars.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050000.xhp	0	help	hd_id0428200810574138				0	lt	Negative				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050000.xhp	0	help	par_id0522200809110667				0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\".\"\>Shows only negative error bars.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050100.xhp	0	help	tit				0	lt	Trend Lines 				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050100.xhp	0	help	bm_id1744743				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>calculating;regression curves\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>regression curves in charts\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>trend lines in charts\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>mean value lines in charts\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050100.xhp	0	help	hd_id5409405				0	lt	\<variable id=\"regression\"\>\<link href=\"text/schart/01/04050100.xhp\"\>Trend Lines\</link\>\</variable\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050100.xhp	0	help	par_id7272255				0	lt	\<variable id=\"trendlinestext\"\>\<ahelp hid=\".\"\>Trend lines can be added to all 2D chart types except for Pie and Stock charts.\</ahelp\>\</variable\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050100.xhp	0	help	par_id143436				0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\".\" visibility=\"hidden\"\>No trend line is shown.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050100.xhp	0	help	par_id5716727				0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\".\" visibility=\"hidden\"\>A linear trend line is shown.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050100.xhp	0	help	par_id5840021				0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\".\" visibility=\"hidden\"\>A logarithmic trend line is shown.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050100.xhp	0	help	par_id9417096				0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\".\" visibility=\"hidden\"\>An exponential trend line is shown.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050100.xhp	0	help	par_id8482924				0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\".\" visibility=\"hidden\"\>A power trend line is shown.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050100.xhp	0	help	par_id8962370				0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\".\" visibility=\"hidden\"\>Shows the trend line equation next to the trend line.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050100.xhp	0	help	par_id6889858				0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\".\" visibility=\"hidden\"\>Shows the coefficient of determination next to the trend line.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050100.xhp	0	help	par_id8398998				0	lt	If you insert a trend line to a chart type that uses categories, like \<emph\>Line \</emph\>or \<emph\>Column, \</emph\>then the numbers 1, 2, 3, \<emph\>…\</emph\> are used as x-values to calculate the trend line.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050100.xhp	0	help	par_id5676747				0	lt	To insert trend lines for all data series, double-click the chart to enter edit mode. Choose \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Insert - Trend Lines\</item\>, then select the type of trend line from None, Linear, Logarithmic, Exponential, or Power trend line.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050100.xhp	0	help	par_id4349192				0	lt	To insert a trend line for a single data series, select the data series in the chart, right-click to open the context menu, and choose \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Insert - Trend Line\</item\>.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050100.xhp	0	help	par_id9337443				0	lt	To delete a single trend line or mean value line, click the line, then press the Del key.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050100.xhp	0	help	par_id4529251				0	lt	To delete all trend lines, choose \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Insert - Trend Lines\</item\>, then select \<emph\>None\</emph\>.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050100.xhp	0	help	par_id296334				0	lt	A trend line is shown in the legend automatically.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050100.xhp	0	help	par_id4072084				0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\".\"\>Mean Value Lines are special trend lines that show the mean value. Use \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Insert - Mean Value Lines\</item\> to insert mean value lines for data series.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050100.xhp	0	help	par_id9569689				0	lt	The trend line has the same color as the corresponding data series. To change the line properties, select the trend line and choose \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Format - Format Selection - Line\</item\>.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050100.xhp	0	help	par_id846888				0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\".\"\>To show the trend line equation, select the trend line in the chart, right-click to open the context menu, and choose \<emph\>Insert Trend Line Equation\</emph\>.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050100.xhp	0	help	par_id8962065				0	lt	When the chart is in edit mode, %PRODUCTNAME gives you the equation of the trend line and the coefficient of determination R². Click on the trend line to see the information in the status bar.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050100.xhp	0	help	par_id1328470				0	lt	For a category chart (for example a line chart), the trend line information is calculated using numbers 1, 2, 3, … as x-values. This is also true if your data series uses other numbers as names for the x-values. For such charts the XY chart type might be more suitable.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050100.xhp	0	help	par_id8092593				0	lt	To show the equation and the coefficient of determination, select the trend line and choose \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Format - Format Selection - Equation\</item\>.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050100.xhp	0	help	par_id7971434				0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\".\" visibility=\"hidden\"\>Enable Show equation to see the equation of the trend line.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050100.xhp	0	help	par_id558793				0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\".\" visibility=\"hidden\"\>Enable Show Coefficient of Determination to see the determination coefficient of the trend line.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050100.xhp	0	help	par_id7735221				0	lt	You can also calculate the parameters using Calc functions as follows.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050100.xhp	0	help	hd_id5744193				0	lt	The linear regression equation				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050100.xhp	0	help	par_id9251991				0	lt	The \<emph\>linear regression\</emph\> follows the equation \<item type=\"literal\"\>y=m*x+b\</item\>.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050100.xhp	0	help	par_id7951902				0	lt	m = SLOPE(Data_Y;Data_X) 				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050100.xhp	0	help	par_id6637165				0	lt	b = INTERCEPT(Data_Y ;Data_X) 				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050100.xhp	0	help	par_id7879268				0	lt	Calculate the coefficient of determination by				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050100.xhp	0	help	par_id9244361				0	lt	r² = RSQ(Data_Y;Data_X) 				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050100.xhp	0	help	par_id2083498				0	lt	Besides m, b and r² the array function \<emph\>LINEST\</emph\> provides additional statistics for a regression analysis.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050100.xhp	0	help	hd_id2538834				0	lt	The logarithm regression equation				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050100.xhp	0	help	par_id394299				0	lt	The \<emph\>logarithm regression\</emph\> follows the equation \<item type=\"literal\"\>y=a*ln(x)+b\</item\>.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050100.xhp	0	help	par_id2134159				0	lt	a = SLOPE(Data_Y;LN(Data_X)) 				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050100.xhp	0	help	par_id5946531				0	lt	b = INTERCEPT(Data_Y ;LN(Data_X)) 				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050100.xhp	0	help	par_id5649281				0	lt	r² = RSQ(Data_Y;LN(Data_X)) 				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050100.xhp	0	help	hd_id7874080				0	lt	The exponential regression equation				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050100.xhp	0	help	par_id4679097				0	lt	 For exponential trend lines a transformation to a linear model takes place. The optimal curve fitting is related to the linear model and the results are interpreted accordingly. 				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050100.xhp	0	help	par_id9112216				0	lt	The exponential regression follows the equation \<item type=\"literal\"\>y=b*exp(a*x)\</item\> or \<item type=\"literal\"\>y=b*m^x\</item\>, which is transformed to \<item type=\"literal\"\>ln(y)=ln(b)+a*x\</item\> or \<item type=\"literal\"\>ln(y)=ln(b)+ln(m)*x\</item\> respectively.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050100.xhp	0	help	par_id4416638				0	lt	a = SLOPE(LN(Data_Y);Data_X) 				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050100.xhp	0	help	par_id1039155				0	lt	The variables for the second variation are calculated as follows:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050100.xhp	0	help	par_id7184057				0	lt	m = EXP(SLOPE(LN(Data_Y);Data_X)) 				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050100.xhp	0	help	par_id786767				0	lt	b = EXP(INTERCEPT(LN(Data_Y);Data_X)) 				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050100.xhp	0	help	par_id7127292				0	lt	Calculate the coefficient of determination by				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050100.xhp	0	help	par_id5437177				0	lt	r² = RSQ(LN(Data_Y);Data_X) 				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050100.xhp	0	help	par_id6946317				0	lt	Besides m, b and r² the array function LOGEST provides additional statistics for a regression analysis.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050100.xhp	0	help	hd_id6349375				0	lt	The power regression equation				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050100.xhp	0	help	par_id1857661				0	lt	 For \<emph\>power regression\</emph\> curves a transformation to a linear model takes place. The power regression follows the equation \<item type=\"literal\"\>y=b*x^a\</item\> , which is transformed to \<item type=\"literal\"\>ln(y)=ln(b)+a*ln(x)\</item\>.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050100.xhp	0	help	par_id8517105				0	lt	a = SLOPE(LN(Data_Y);LN(Data_X)) 				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050100.xhp	0	help	par_id9827265				0	lt	b = EXP(INTERCEPT(LN(Data_Y);LN(Data_X)) 				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050100.xhp	0	help	par_id2357249				0	lt	r² = RSQ(LN(Data_Y);LN(Data_X)) 				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050100.xhp	0	help	hd_id9204077				0	lt	Constraints				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050100.xhp	0	help	par_id7393719				0	lt	 The calculation of the trend line considers only data pairs with the following values:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050100.xhp	0	help	par_id7212744				0	lt	logarithm regression: only positive x-values are considered,				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050100.xhp	0	help	par_id1664479				0	lt	exponential regression: only positive y-values are considered,				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050100.xhp	0	help	par_id8734702				0	lt	power regression: only positive x-values and positive y-values are considered.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050100.xhp	0	help	par_id181279				0	lt	You should transform your data accordingly; it is best to work on a copy of the original data and transform the copied data.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050100.xhp	0	help	hd_id7907040				0	lt	The polynomial regression equation				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050100.xhp	0	help	par_id8918729				0	lt	A \<emph\>polynomial regression\</emph\> curve cannot be added automatically. You must calculate this curve manually. 				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050100.xhp	0	help	par_id33875				0	lt	Create a table with the columns x, x², x³, … , xⁿ, y up to the desired degree n. 				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050100.xhp	0	help	par_id8720053				0	lt	Use the formula \<item type=\"literal\"\>=LINEST(Data_Y,Data_X)\</item\> with the complete range x to xⁿ (without headings) as Data_X. 				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050100.xhp	0	help	par_id5068514				0	lt	The first row of the LINEST output contains the coefficients of the regression polynomial, with the coefficient of xⁿ at the leftmost position.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050100.xhp	0	help	par_id8202154				0	lt	The first element of the third row of the LINEST output is the value of r². See the \<link href=\"text/scalc/01/04060107.xhp#Section8\"\>LINEST\</link\> function for details on proper use and an explanation of the other output parameters.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050100.xhp	0	help	par_id4562211				0	lt	\<link href=\"text/schart/01/04050000.xhp\"\>Y Error Bars tab page\</link\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04060000.xhp	0	help	tit				0	lt	Options				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04060000.xhp	0	help	bm_id3149400				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>aligning; 2D charts\</bookmark_value\>         \<bookmark_value\>charts; aligning\</bookmark_value\>         \<bookmark_value\>pie charts;options\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04060000.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149400	1			0	lt	\<link href=\"text/schart/01/04060000.xhp\" name=\"Options\"\>Options\</link\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04060000.xhp	0	help	par_id3155067	2			0	lt	Use this dialog to define some options that are available for specific chart types. The contents of the Options dialog vary with the chart type.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04060000.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150043	9			0	lt	Align data series to:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04060000.xhp	0	help	par_id3145228	10			0	lt	In this area you can choose between two Y axis scaling modes. The axes can only be scaled and given properties separately.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04060000.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147346	4			0	lt	Primary Y axis				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04060000.xhp	0	help	par_id3147005	15			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"SCH:RADIOBUTTON:TP_OPTIONS:RBT_OPT_AXIS_1\"\>This option is active as default. All data series are aligned to the primary Y axis.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04060000.xhp	0	help	hd_id3143221	5			0	lt	Secondary Y axis				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04060000.xhp	0	help	par_id3154656	11			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"SCH:RADIOBUTTON:TP_OPTIONS:RBT_OPT_AXIS_2\"\>Changes the scaling of the Y axis. This axis is only visible when at least one data series is assigned to it and the axis view is active.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04060000.xhp	0	help	hd_id3166423	6			0	lt	Settings				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04060000.xhp	0	help	par_id3150365	12			0	lt	Define the settings for a bar chart in this area. Any changes apply to all data series of the chart, not to the selected data only.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04060000.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145584	7			0	lt	Spacing				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04060000.xhp	0	help	par_id3155376	13			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"SCH:METRICFIELD:TP_OPTIONS:MT_GAP\"\>Defines the spacing between the columns in percent.\</ahelp\> The maximal spacing is 600%.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04060000.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145384	8			0	lt	Overlap				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04060000.xhp	0	help	par_id3156447	14			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"SCH:METRICFIELD:TP_OPTIONS:MT_OVERLAP\"\>Defines the necessary settings for overlapping data series.\</ahelp\> You can choose between -100 and +100%.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04060000.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153305	16			0	lt	Connection Lines				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04060000.xhp	0	help	par_id3148868	17			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"SCH:CHECKBOX:TP_OPTIONS:CB_CONNECTOR\"\>For "stacked" and "percent" column (vertical bar) charts, mark this check box to connect the column layers that belong together with lines.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04060000.xhp	0	help	hd_id9842219				0	lt	Show bars side by side				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04060000.xhp	0	help	par_id9800103				0	lt	If two axes are shown in a bar chart, and some data series are attached to the first axis, while some other data series are attached to the second axis, then both sets of data series are shown independently, overlapping each other.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04060000.xhp	0	help	par_id2144535				0	lt	As a result, bars attached to the first y-axis are partly or completely hidden by bars attached to the second y-axis. To avoid this, enable the option to display bars side by side. \<ahelp hid=\".\"\>The bars from different data series are shown as if they were attached only to one axis.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04060000.xhp	0	help	hd_id24414				0	lt	Clockwise direction				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04060000.xhp	0	help	par_id2527237				0	lt	Available for pie and donut charts. \<ahelp hid=\".\"\>The default direction in which the pieces of a pie chart are ordered is counterclockwise. Enable the \<emph\>Clockwise direction\</emph\> checkbox to draw the pieces in opposite direction.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04060000.xhp	0	help	hd_id401013				0	lt	Starting angle				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04060000.xhp	0	help	par_id761131				0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\".\"\>Drag the small dot along the circle or click any position on the circle to set the starting angle of a pie or donut chart. The starting angle is the mathematical angle position where the first piece is drawn. The value of 90 degrees draws the first piece at the 12 o'clock position. A value of 0 degrees starts at the 3 o'clock position.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04060000.xhp	0	help	par_id553910				0	lt	In 3D pie and donut charts that were created with older versions of the software, the starting angle is 0 degrees instead of 90 degrees. For old and new 2D charts the default starting angle is 90 degrees.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04060000.xhp	0	help	par_id1414838				0	lt	When you change the starting angle or the direction, only current versions of the software show the changed values. Older versions of the software display the same document using the default values: Always counterclockwise direction and a starting value of 90 degrees (2D pie charts) or 0 degrees (3D pie charts).				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04060000.xhp	0	help	hd_id3179723				0	lt	Degrees				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04060000.xhp	0	help	par_id2164067				0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\".\"\>Enter the starting angle between 0 and 359 degrees. You can also click the arrows to change the displayed value.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04060000.xhp	0	help	hd_id0305200910524613				0	lt	Plot missing values				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04060000.xhp	0	help	par_id0305200910524650				0	lt	Sometimes values are missing in a data series that is shown in a chart. You can select from different options how to plot the missing values. The options are available for some chart types only.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04060000.xhp	0	help	hd_id0305200910524823				0	lt	Leave gap				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04060000.xhp	0	help	par_id0305200910524811				0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\".\"\>For a missing value, no data will be shown. This is the default for chart types Column, Bar, Line, Net.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04060000.xhp	0	help	hd_id0305200910524811				0	lt	Assume zero				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04060000.xhp	0	help	par_id030520091052489				0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\".\"\>For a missing value, the y-value will be shown as zero. This is the default for chart type Area.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04060000.xhp	0	help	hd_id0305200910524837				0	lt	Continue line				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04060000.xhp	0	help	par_id0305200910524938				0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\".\"\>For a missing value, the interpolation from the neighbor values will be shown. This is the default for chart type XY.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04060000.xhp	0	help	hd_id0305200910524937				0	lt	Include values from hidden cells				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04060000.xhp	0	help	par_id030520091052494				0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\".\"\>Check to also show values of currently hidden cells within the source cell range.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04070000.xhp	0	help	tit				0	lt	Grids				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04070000.xhp	0	help	bm_id3147434				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>axes; inserting grids\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>grids; inserting in charts\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04070000.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147434	1			0	lt	Grids				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04070000.xhp	0	help	par_id3146974	2			0	lt	\<variable id=\"gitter\"\>\<ahelp hid=\".\"\>You can divide the axes into sections by assigning gridlines to them. This allows you to get a better overview of the chart, especially if you are working with large charts.\</ahelp\>\</variable\> The Y axis major grid is activated by default.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04070000.xhp	0	help	hd_id3156286	3			0	lt	Major grids				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04070000.xhp	0	help	par_id3154511	4			0	lt	Defines the axis to be set as the major grid.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04070000.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149400	5			0	lt	X axis				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04070000.xhp	0	help	par_id3150749	6			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"SCH:CHECKBOX:DLG_GRID:CB_X_MAIN\"\>Adds gridlines to the X axis of the chart.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04070000.xhp	0	help	par_id3154754	7			0	lt	\<variable id=\"sytextxgitter\"\>\<ahelp hid=\".uno:ToggleGridHorizontal\"\>The \<emph\>Horizontal Grid On/Off\</emph\> icon on the \<emph\>Formatting\</emph\> bar toggles the visibility of the grid display for the X axis. Note: This only works if the \<emph\>Minor grid\</emph\> check boxes in \<emph\>Insert - Grids\</emph\> are cleared.\</ahelp\>\</variable\> Otherwise, the minor grid remains visible when the major grid is turned off.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04070000.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145228	8			0	lt	Y axis				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04070000.xhp	0	help	par_id3147004	9			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"SCH:CHECKBOX:DLG_GRID:CB_Y_MAIN\"\>Adds gridlines to the Y axis of the chart.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04070000.xhp	0	help	par_id3150344	10			0	lt	\<variable id=\"sytextygitter\"\>\<ahelp hid=\".uno:ToggleGridVertical\"\>The \<emph\>Vertical Grid On/Off\</emph\> icon on the \<emph\>Formatting\</emph\> bar toggles the visibility of the grid display for the Y axis. Note: This only works if the X-axis \<emph\>Minor grid\</emph\> is not selected in \<emph\>Insert - Grids\</emph\>.\</ahelp\>\</variable\> Otherwise, the minor grid remains visible when the major grid is turned off.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04070000.xhp	0	help	hd_id3166430	11			0	lt	Z axis				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04070000.xhp	0	help	par_id3155378	12			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"SCH:CHECKBOX:DLG_GRID:CB_Z_MAIN\"\>Adds gridlines to the Z axis of the chart.\</ahelp\> This option is only available if you're working with 3D charts.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04070000.xhp	0	help	hd_id3146978	13			0	lt	Minor grids				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04070000.xhp	0	help	par_id3156449	14			0	lt	Use this area to assign a minor grid for each axis. Assigning minor grids to the axis reduces the distance between the major grids.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04070000.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153308	15			0	lt	X axis				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04070000.xhp	0	help	par_id3148704	16			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"SCH:CHECKBOX:DLG_GRID:CB_X_HELP\"\>Adds gridlines that subdivide the X axis into smaller sections.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04070000.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153917	17			0	lt	Y axis				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04070000.xhp	0	help	par_id3154536	18			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"SCH:CHECKBOX:DLG_GRID:CB_Y_HELP\"\>Adds gridlines that subdivide the Y axis into smaller sections.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04070000.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148607	19			0	lt	Z axis				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04070000.xhp	0	help	par_id3153247	20			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"SCH:CHECKBOX:DLG_GRID:CB_Z_HELP\"\>Adds gridlines that subdivide the Z axis into smaller sections.\</ahelp\> This option is only available if you're working with 3D charts.				20130618 17:22:18
