helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03090700.xhp	0	help	par_id3155394	48			0	lt	For alignment, the \<emph\>alignl, alignc\</emph\> and \<emph\>alignr\</emph\> commands are especially effective, if you are 				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03090700.xhp	0	help	par_id3151009	49			0	lt	aligning numerators and denominators, for example \<emph\>{alignl a}over{b+c}\</emph\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03090700.xhp	0	help	par_id3148812	50			0	lt	constructing binomials or stacks, for example \<emph\>binom{2*n}{alignr k}\</emph\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03090700.xhp	0	help	par_id3154360	51			0	lt	aligning the elements in a matrix, for example \<emph\>matrix{alignr a#b+2##c+1/3#alignl d}\</emph\> and				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03090700.xhp	0	help	par_id3155946	52			0	lt	beginning a new line, for example \<emph\>a+b-c newline alignr x/y\</emph\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03090700.xhp	0	help	par_id3154621	53			0	lt	When using the align instructions, note that				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03090700.xhp	0	help	par_id3147382	54			0	lt	they can only placed at the beginning of expressions and can only occur once. Therefore you can type \<emph\>a+b alignr c\</emph\>, but not \<emph\>a+alignr b\</emph\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03090700.xhp	0	help	par_id3154004	55			0	lt	they affect each other, which means that typing \<emph\>{alignl{alignr a}}over{b+c}\</emph\> aligns \<emph\>a\</emph\> on the right.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03090700.xhp	0	help	hd_id8036133				0	lt	To align using the "matrix" command				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03090700.xhp	0	help	par_idN10F70				0	lt	Aligning to the left				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03090700.xhp	0	help	par_id3149645	56			0	lt	If a line or an expression begins with text, it is aligned on the left by default. You can change this with any of the \<emph\>align\</emph\> commands. An example is \<emph\>stack{a+b-c*d#alignr "text"}\</emph\>, where "text" appears aligned to the right. Note that text must always be surrounded by quotation marks.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03090700.xhp	0	help	par_id3149966	57			0	lt	The standard centralized formulas can be aligned to the left without using the \<emph\>Format - Alignment\</emph\> menu. To do this, place an empty character string, that is, the inverted commas which surround any text "", before the section of formula that you want to align. For example, typing \<emph\>"" a+b newline "" c+d\</emph\> results in both equations being left-aligned instead of centered.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03090700.xhp	0	help	par_id3145654	46			0	lt	When typing information in the Commands window, note that some formats require spaces for the correct structure. This is especially true when entering values (for example, a lsup{3}) instead of placeholders.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03090700.xhp	0	help	par_id3148708	47			0	lt	Click \<link href=\"text/smath/01/03091100.xhp\" name=\"Brackets and Grouping\"\>Brackets and Grouping\</link\> for more information about formatting in \<emph\>$[officename] Math\</emph\>.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03090700.xhp	0	help	par_id3155340	31			0	lt	Useful information about \<link href=\"text/smath/01/03091200.xhp\" name=\"Indexes and Exponents\"\>Indexes and Exponents\</link\> and \<link href=\"text/smath/01/03091400.xhp\" name=\"Scaling\"\>Scaling\</link\>, helps you organize your document in the best possible way.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03090800.xhp	0	help	tit				0	lt	Set Operations				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03090800.xhp	0	help	bm_id3156318				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>set operations in $[officename]Math\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>sets of numbers\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>included in set operator\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>not included in set operator\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>owns command\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>includes set operator\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>empty set\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>intersection of sets\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>union of sets\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>difference set operator\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>quotient set\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>cardinal numbers\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>subset set operators\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>superset set operators\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>not subset set operators\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>not superset set operators\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>natural numbers\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>whole numbers\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>real numbers\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>complex numbers; set\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>rational numbers\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03090800.xhp	0	help	hd_id3156318	1			0	lt	\<link href=\"text/smath/01/03090800.xhp\" name=\"Set Operations\"\>Set Operations\</link\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03090800.xhp	0	help	par_id3154641	2			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_SMA_SETOPERATIONS_CAT\"\>Assign different set operators to the characters in your \<emph\>$[officename] Math\</emph\> formula. The individual operators are shown in the lower section of the Elements window\</ahelp\>. Call the \<link href=\"text/shared/00/00000001.xhp#kontextmenue\" name=\"context menu\"\>context menu\</link\> in the \<emph\>Commands\</emph\> window to see an identical list of the individual functions. Any operators not found in the Elements window have to be entered directly in the Commands window. You can also directly insert other parts of the formula even if symbols already exist for them.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03090800.xhp	0	help	par_id3149290	3			0	lt	After clicking the \<emph\>Set Operations\</emph\> icon in the Elements window additional icons will be shown in the lower part of this window. Simply click a symbol to incorporate the operator in the formula being edited in the Commands window.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03090800.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147258	4			0	lt	The set operations in detail:				20130618 17:22:18
