helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01010210.xhp	0	help	bm_id4488967				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>fundamentals\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>subroutines\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>variables;global and local\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>modules;subroutines and functions\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01010210.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154927	1			0	lt	\<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/01010210.xhp\" name=\"Basics\"\>Basics\</link\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01010210.xhp	0	help	par_id3156023	14			0	lt	This section provides the fundamentals for working with $[officename] Basic.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01010210.xhp	0	help	par_id3147560	2			0	lt	$[officename] Basic code is based on subroutines and functions that are specified between \<emph\>sub...end sub\</emph\> and \<emph\>function...end function\</emph\> sections. Each Sub or Function can call other Subs and Functions. If you take care to write generic code for a Sub or Function, you can probably re-use it in other programs. See also \<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/01020300.xhp\" name=\"Procedures and Functions\"\>Procedures and Functions\</link\>.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01010210.xhp	0	help	par_id314756320				0	lt	Some restrictions apply for the names of your public variables, subs, and functions. You must not use the same name as one of the modules of the same library.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01010210.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150398	3			0	lt	What is a Sub?				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01010210.xhp	0	help	par_id3148797	4			0	lt	\<emph\>Sub\</emph\> is the short form of \<emph\>subroutine\</emph\>, that is used to handle a certain task within a program. Subs are used to split a task into individual procedures. Splitting a program into procedures and sub-procedures enhances readability and reduces the error-proneness. A sub possibly takes some arguments as parameters but does not return any values back to the calling sub or function, for example:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01010210.xhp	0	help	par_id3150868	15			0	lt	DoSomethingWithTheValues(MyFirstValue,MySecondValue)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01010210.xhp	0	help	hd_id3156282	5			0	lt	What is a Function?				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01010210.xhp	0	help	par_id3156424	6			0	lt	A \<emph\>function\</emph\> is essentially a sub, which returns a value. You may use a function at the right side of a variable declaration, or at other places where you normally use values, for example:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01010210.xhp	0	help	par_id3146985	7			0	lt	MySecondValue = myFunction(MyFirstValue)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01010210.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153364	8			0	lt	Global and local variables				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01010210.xhp	0	help	par_id3151112	9			0	lt	Global variables are valid for all subs and functions inside a module. They are declared at the beginning of a module before the first sub or function starts.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01010210.xhp	0	help	par_id3154012	10			0	lt	Variables that you declare within a sub or function are valid only inside this sub or function. These variables override global variables with the same name and local variables with the same name coming from superordinate subs or functions.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01010210.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150010	11			0	lt	Structuring				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01010210.xhp	0	help	par_id3153727	12			0	lt	After separating your program into procedures and functions (Subs and Functions), you can save these procedures and functions as files for reuse in other projects. $[officename] Basic supports \<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/01020500.xhp\" name=\"Modules and Libraries\"\>Modules and Libraries\</link\>. Subs and functions are always contained in modules. You can define modules to be global or part of a document. Multiple modules can be combined to a library.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01010210.xhp	0	help	par_id3152578	13			0	lt	You can copy or move subs, functions, modules and libraries from one file to another by using the \<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/01/06130000.xhp\" name=\"Macro\"\>Macro\</link\> dialog.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020000.xhp	0	help	tit				0	lt	Syntax				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020000.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148946	1			0	lt	\<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/01020000.xhp\" name=\"Syntax\"\>Syntax\</link\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020000.xhp	0	help	par_id3150793	2			0	lt	This section describes the basic syntax elements of $[officename] Basic. For a detailed description please refer to the $[officename] Basic Guide which is available separately.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	tit				0	lt	Using Variables				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	bm_id3149346				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>names of variables\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>variables; using\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>types of variables\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>declaring variables\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>values;of variables\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>constants\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>arrays;declaring\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>defining;constants\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149346	1			0	lt	\<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/01020100.xhp\" name=\"Using Variables\"\>Using Variables\</link\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154346	3			0	lt	The following describes the basic use of variables in $[officename] Basic.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153361	4			0	lt	Naming Conventions for Variable Identifiers				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3148797	5			0	lt	A variable name can consist of a maximum of 255 characters. The first character of a variable name \<emph\>must\</emph\> be a letter A-Z or a-z. Numbers can also be used in a variable name, but punctuation symbols and special characters are not permitted, with exception of the underscore character ("_"). In $[officename] Basic variable identifiers are not case-sensitive. Variable names may contain spaces but must be enclosed in square brackets if they do.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3156422	6			0	lt	Examples for variable identifiers:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3163798	7			0	lt	MyNumber=5				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3156441	126			0	lt	Correct				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3147317	8			0	lt	MyNumber5=15				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149664	127			0	lt	Correct				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3145364	9			0	lt	MyNumber_5=20				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3146119	128			0	lt	Correct				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154729	10			0	lt	My Number=20				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153876	11			0	lt	Not valid, variable with space must be enclosed in square brackets				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3147126	14			0	lt	[My Number]=12				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154510	15			0	lt	Correct				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153708	12			0	lt	DéjàVu=25				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150330	129			0	lt	Not valid, special characters are not allowed				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3155443	13			0	lt	5MyNumber=12				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154254	130			0	lt	Not valid, variable may not begin with a number				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3147345	16			0	lt	Number,Mine=12				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149256	131			0	lt	Not valid, punctuation marks are not allowed				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3146317	17			0	lt	Declaring Variables				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150299	18			0	lt	In $[officename] Basic you don't need to declare variables explicitly. A variable declaration can be performed with the \<emph\>Dim\</emph\> statement. You can declare more than one variable at a time by separating the names with a comma. To define the variable type, use either a type-declaration sign after the name, or the appropriate key word. 				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154118	140			0	lt	Examples for variable declarations:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150090	19			0	lt	DIM a$				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150982	132			0	lt	Declares the variable "a" as a String				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149531	20			0	lt	DIM a As String				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150343	133			0	lt	Declares the variable "a" as a String				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149036	21			0	lt	DIM a$, b As Integer				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3155507	22			0	lt	Declares one variable as a String and one as an Integer				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_idN10854				0	lt	DIM c As Boolean				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_idN10859				0	lt	Declares c as a Boolean variable that can be TRUE or FALSE				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150519	23			0	lt	It is very important when declaring variables that you use the type-declaration character each time, even if it was used in the declaration instead of a keyword. Thus the following statements are invalid:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3152985	24			0	lt	DIM a$				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154527	134			0	lt	Declares "a" as a String				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3148599	25			0	lt	a="TestString"				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153064	135			0	lt	Type-declaration missing: "a$="				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3144770	26			0	lt	Once you have declared a variable as a certain type, you cannot declare the variable under the same name again as a different type!				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149331	27			0	lt	Forcing Variable Declarations				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149443	28			0	lt	To force declaration of variables, use the following command:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3152869	29			0	lt	OPTION EXPLICIT				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3155072	30			0	lt	The \<emph\>Option Explicit\</emph\> statement has to be the first line in the module, before the first SUB. Generally, only arrays need to be declared explicitly. All other variables are declared according to the type-declaration character, or - if omitted - as the default type \<emph\>Single\</emph\>.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154614	34			0	lt	Variable Types				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3155383	35			0	lt	$[officename] Basic supports four variable classes:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153972	36			0	lt	\<emph\>Numeric\</emph\> variables can contain number values. Some variables are used to store large or small numbers, and others are used for floating-point or fractional numbers. 				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3159226	37			0	lt	\<emph\>String\</emph\> variables contain character strings.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3145217	38			0	lt	\<emph\>Boolean\</emph\> variables contain either the TRUE or the FALSE value.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154762	39			0	lt	\<emph\>Object\</emph\> variables can store objects of various types, like tables and documents within a document.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153805	40			0	lt	Integer Variables				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3146966	41			0	lt	Integer variables range from -32768 to 32767. If you assign a floating-point value to an integer variable, the decimal places are rounded to the next integer. Integer variables are rapidly calculated in procedures and are suitable for counter variables in loops. An integer variable only requires two bytes of memory. "%" is the type-declaration character.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153810	43			0	lt	Dim Variable%				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153556	44			0	lt	Dim Variable As Integer				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147546	45			0	lt	Long Integer Variables				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3151193	46			0	lt	Long integer variables range from -2147483648 to 2147483647. If you assign a floating-point value to a long integer variable, the decimal places are rounded to the next integer. Long integer variables are rapidly calculated in procedures and are suitable for counter variables in loops for large values. A long integer variable requires four bytes of memory. "&" is the type-declaration character.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154708	48			0	lt	Dim Variable&				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3156365	49			0	lt	Dim Variable as Long				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	hd_id7596972				0	lt	Decimal Variables				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id2649311				0	lt	Decimal variables can take positive or negative numbers or zero. Accuracy is up to 29 digits.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id7617114				0	lt	You can use plus (+) or minus (-) signs as prefixes for decimal numbers (with or without spaces).				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id1593676				0	lt	If a decimal number is assigned to an integer variable, %PRODUCTNAME Basic rounds the figure up or down.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147500	50			0	lt	Single Variables				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153070	51			0	lt	Single variables can take positive or negative values ranging from 3.402823 x 10E38 to 1.401298 x 10E-45. Single variables are floating-point variables, in which the decimal precision decreases as the non-decimal part of the number increases. Single variables are suitable for mathematical calculations of average precision. Calculations require more time than for Integer variables, but are faster than calculations with Double variables. A Single variable requires 4 bytes of memory. The type-declaration character is "!".				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149875	52			0	lt	Dim Variable!				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153302	53			0	lt	Dim Variable as Single				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155753	54			0	lt	Double Variables				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150953	55			0	lt	Double variables can take positive or negative values ranging from 1.79769313486232 x 10E308 to 4.94065645841247 x 10E-324. Double variables are floating-point variables, in which the decimal precision decreases as the non-decimal part of the number increases. Double variables are suitable for precise calculations. Calculations require more time than for Single variables. A Double variable requires 8 bytes of memory. The type-declaration character is "#".				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150431	56			0	lt	Dim Variable#				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154406	57			0	lt	Dim Variable As Double				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155747	95			0	lt	Currency Variables				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153337	96			0	lt	Currency variables are internally stored as 64-bit numbers (8 Bytes) and displayed as a fixed-decimal number with 15 non-decimal and 4 decimal places. The values range from -922337203685477.5808 to +922337203685477.5807. Currency variables are used to calculate currency values with a high precision. The type-declaration character is "@".				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3147296	97			0	lt	Dim Variable@				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150391	98			0	lt	Dim Variable As Currency				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148742	58			0	lt	String Variables				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3151393	59			0	lt	String variables can hold character strings with up to 65,535 characters. Each character is stored as the corresponding Unicode value. String variables are suitable for word processing within programs and for temporary storage of any non-printable character up to a maximum length of 64 Kbytes. The memory required for storing string variables depends on the number of characters in the variable. The type-declaration character is "$".				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3166467	60			0	lt	Dim Variable$				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153027	61			0	lt	Dim Variable As String				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150534	62			0	lt	Boolean Variables				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3145632	63			0	lt	Boolean variables store only one of two values: TRUE or FALSE. A number 0 evaluates to FALSE, every other value evaluates to TRUE.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3147615	64			0	lt	Dim Variable As Boolean				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149722	65			0	lt	Date Variables				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3159116	66			0	lt	Date variables can only contain dates and time values stored in an internal format. Values assigned to Date variables with \<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/03030101.xhp\" name=\"Dateserial\"\>\<emph\>Dateserial\</emph\>\</link\>, \<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/03030102.xhp\" name=\"Datevalue\"\>\<emph\>Datevalue\</emph\>\</link\>, \<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/03030205.xhp\" name=\"Timeserial\"\>\<emph\>Timeserial\</emph\>\</link\> or \<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/03030206.xhp\" name=\"Timevalue\"\>\<emph\>Timevalue\</emph\>\</link\> are automatically converted to the internal format. Date-variables are converted to normal numbers by using the \<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/03030103.xhp\" name=\"Day\"\>\<emph\>Day\</emph\>\</link\>, \<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/03030104.xhp\" name=\"Month\"\>\<emph\>Month\</emph\>\</link\>, \<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/03030106.xhp\" name=\"Year\"\>\<emph\>Year\</emph\>\</link\> or the \<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/03030201.xhp\" name=\"Hour\"\>\<emph\>Hour\</emph\>\</link\>, \<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/03030202.xhp\" name=\"Minute\"\>\<emph\>Minute\</emph\>\</link\>, \<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/03030204.xhp\" name=\"Second\"\>\<emph\>Second\</emph\>\</link\> function. The internal format enables a comparison of date/time values by calculating the difference between two numbers. These variables can only be declared with the key word \<emph\>Date\</emph\>.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150462	67			0	lt	Dim Variable As Date				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148732	68			0	lt	Initial Variable Values				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154549	69			0	lt	As soon as the variable has been declared, it is automatically set to the "Null" value. Note the following conventions:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3143222	70			0	lt	\<emph\>Numeric\</emph\> variables are automatically assigned the value "0" as soon as they are declared.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150693	71			0	lt	\<emph\>Date variables\</emph\> are assigned the value 0 internally; equivalent to converting the value to "0" with the \<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/03030103.xhp\" name=\"Day\"\>\<emph\>Day\</emph\>\</link\>, \<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/03030104.xhp\" name=\"Month\"\>\<emph\>Month\</emph\>\</link\>, \<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/03030106.xhp\" name=\"Year\"\>\<emph\>Year\</emph\>\</link\> or the \<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/03030201.xhp\" name=\"Hour\"\>\<emph\>Hour\</emph\>\</link\>, \<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/03030202.xhp\" name=\"Minute\"\>\<emph\>Minute\</emph\>\</link\>, \<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/03030204.xhp\" name=\"Second\"\>\<emph\>Second\</emph\>\</link\> function.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154807	72			0	lt	\<emph\>String variables\</emph\> are assigned an empty-string ("") when they are declared.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153936	83			0	lt	Arrays				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3148736	84			0	lt	$[officename] Basic knows one- or multi-dimensional arrays, defined by a specified variable type. Arrays are suitable for editing lists and tables in programs. Individual elements of an array can be addressed through a numeric index.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149546	85			0	lt	Arrays \<emph\>must\</emph\> be declared with the \<emph\>Dim\</emph\> statement. There are several ways to define the index range of an array:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150143	86			0	lt	DIM text$(20)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154567	136			0	lt	21 elements numbered from 0 to 20				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3145596	125			0	lt	DIM text$(5,4)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154397	137			0	lt	30 elements (a matrix of 6 x 5 elements)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149185	87			0	lt	DIM text$(5 to 25)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149690	138			0	lt	21 elements numbered from 5 to 25				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3155950	88			0	lt	DIM text$(-15 to 5)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153113	89			0	lt	21 elements (including 0), numbered from -15 to 5				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153005	90			0	lt	The index range can include positive as well as negative numbers. 				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154507	91			0	lt	Constants				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3156357	92			0	lt	Constants have a fixed value. They are only defined once in the program and cannot be redefined later:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153203	93			0	lt	CONST ConstName=Expression				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020200.xhp	0	help	tit				0	lt	Using Objects				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020200.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145645	1			0	lt	\<variable id=\"01020200\"\>\<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/01020200.xhp\"\>Using the Object Catalog\</link\>\</variable\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020200.xhp	0	help	par_id3153707	76			0	lt	The object catalog provides an overview of all modules and dialogs you have created in $[officename].				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020200.xhp	0	help	par_id3147346	78			0	lt	Click the \<emph\>Object Catalog\</emph\> icon \<image id=\"img_id3147341\" src=\"res/commandimagelist/sc_objectcatalog.png\" width=\"0.564cm\" height=\"0.564cm\"\>\<alt id=\"alt_id3147341\"\>Icon\</alt\>\</image\> in the Macro toolbar to display the object catalog.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020200.xhp	0	help	par_id3155114	79			0	lt	The dialog shows a list of all existing objects in a hierarchical representation. Double-clicking a list entry opens its subordinate objects.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020200.xhp	0	help	par_id3150786	83			0	lt	To display a certain module in the Editor or to position the cursor in a selected SUB or FUNCTION, select the corresponding entry and click the \<emph\>Show\</emph\> icon \<image id=\"img_id3149527\" src=\"basctl/res/im01.png\" width=\"0.564cm\" height=\"0.564cm\"\>\<alt id=\"alt_id3149527\"\>Icon\</alt\>\</image\>.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020200.xhp	0	help	par_id3153266	81			0	lt	Click the (X) icon in the title bar to close the object catalog.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	tit				0	lt	Using Procedures and Functions				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	bm_id3149456				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>procedures\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>functions;using\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>variables;passing to procedures and functions\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>parameters;for procedures and functions\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>parameters;passing by reference or value\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>variables;scope\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>scope of variables\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>GLOBAL variables\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>PUBLIC variables\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>PRIVATE variables\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>functions;return value type\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>return value type of functions\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149456	1			0	lt	\<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/01020300.xhp\"\>Using Procedures and Functions\</link\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3150767	2			0	lt	The following describes the basic use of procedures and functions in $[officename] Basic.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3151215	56			0	lt	When you create a new module, $[officename] Basic automatically inserts a SUB called "Main". This default name has nothing to do with the order or the starting point of a $[officename] Basic project. You can also safely rename this SUB.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id314756320				0	lt	Some restrictions apply for the names of your public variables, subs, and functions. You must not use the same name as one of the modules of the same library.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3154124	3			0	lt	Procedures (SUBS) and functions (FUNCTIONS) help you maintaining a structured overview by separating a program into logical pieces.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3153193	4			0	lt	One benefit of procedures and functions is that, once you have developed a program code containing task components, you can use this code in another project.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153770	26			0	lt	Passing Variables to Procedures (SUB) and Functions (FUNCTION)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3155414	27			0	lt	Variables can be passed to both procedures and functions. The SUB or FUNCTION must be declared to expect parameters:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3163710	28			0	lt	SUB SubName(\<emph\>Parameter1 As Type, Parameter2 As Type,...\</emph\>)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3151114	29			0	lt	Program code				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3146975	30			0	lt	END SUB				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3152577	31			0	lt	The SUB is called using the following syntax:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3159154	32			0	lt	SubName(Value1, Value2,...)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3147124	33			0	lt	The parameters passed to a SUB must fit to those specified in the SUB declaration.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3147397	34			0	lt	The same process applies to FUNCTIONS. In addition, functions always return a function result. The result of a function is defined by assigning the return value to the function name:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3149412	35			0	lt	FUNCTION FunctionName(Parameter1 As Type, Parameter2 As Type,...) As Type				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3156284	36			0	lt	Program code				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3145799	37			0	lt	\<emph\>FunctionName=Result\</emph\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3150716	38			0	lt	End Function				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3153839	39			0	lt	The FUNCTION is called using the following syntax:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3146914	40			0	lt	Variable=FunctionName(Parameter1, Parameter2,...)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_idN107B3				0	lt	You can also use the fully qualified name to call a procedure or function:\<br/\>\<item type=\"literal\"\>Library.Module.Macro()\</item\>\<br/\> For example, to call the Autotext macro from the Gimmicks library, use the following command:\<br/\>\<item type=\"literal\"\>Gimmicks.AutoText.Main()\</item\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	hd_id3156276	45			0	lt	Passing Variables by Value or Reference				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3155765	47			0	lt	Parameters can be passed to a SUB or a FUNCTION either by reference or by value. Unless otherwise specified, a parameter is always passed by reference. That means that a SUB or a FUNCTION gets the parameter and can read and modify its value.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3145640	53			0	lt	If you want to pass a parameter by value insert the key word "ByVal" in front of the parameter when you call a SUB or FUNCTION, for example:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3150042	54			0	lt	Result = Function(\<emph\>ByVal\</emph\> Parameter)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3149258	55			0	lt	In this case, the original content of the parameter will not be modified by the FUNCTION since it only gets the value and not the parameter itself.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150982	57			0	lt	Scope of Variables				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3149814	58			0	lt	A variable defined within a SUB or FUNCTION, only remains valid until the procedure is exited. This is known as a "local" variable. In many cases, you need a variable to be valid in all procedures, in every module of all libraries, or after a SUB or FUNCTION is exited.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154186	59			0	lt	Declaring Variables Outside a SUB or FUNCTION				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3150208	111			0	lt	GLOBAL VarName As TYPENAME				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3145258	112			0	lt	The variable is valid as long as the $[officename] session lasts.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3153198	60			0	lt	PUBLIC VarName As TYPENAME				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3150088	61			0	lt	The variable is valid in all modules.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3158212	62			0	lt	PRIVATE VarName As TYPENAME				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3152994	63			0	lt	The variable is only valid in this module.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3150886	64			0	lt	DIM VarName As TYPENAME				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3150368	65			0	lt	The variable is only valid in this module.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	hd_id5097506				0	lt	Example for private variables				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id8738975				0	lt	Enforce private variables to be private across modules by setting CompatibilityMode(true).				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id146488				0	lt	REM ***** Module1 *****				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id2042298				0	lt	Private myText As String				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id2969756				0	lt	Sub initMyText				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id9475997				0	lt	myText = "Hello"				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id6933500				0	lt	print "in module1 : ", myText				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id631733				0	lt	End Sub				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id8234199				0	lt	REM ***** Module2 *****				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id6969512				0	lt	'Option Explicit				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id1196935				0	lt	Sub demoBug				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id1423993				0	lt	CompatibilityMode( true )				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id6308786				0	lt	initMyText				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id4104129				0	lt	' Now returns empty string				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id7906125				0	lt	' (or rises error for Option Explicit)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id8055970				0	lt	print "Now in module2 : ", myText				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id2806176				0	lt	End Sub				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154368	66			0	lt	Saving Variable Content after Exiting a SUB or FUNCTION				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3156288	67			0	lt	STATIC VarName As TYPENAME				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3154486	68			0	lt	The variable retains its value until the next time the FUNCTION or SUB is entered. The declaration must exist inside a SUB or a FUNCTION.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155809	41			0	lt	Specifying the Return Value Type of a FUNCTION				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3149404	42			0	lt	As with variables, include a type-declaration character after the function name, or the type indicated by "As" and the corresponding key word at the end of the parameter list to define the type of the function's return value, for example:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3152899	43			0	lt	Function WordCount(WordText as String) \<emph\>as Integer\</emph\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020500.xhp	0	help	tit				0	lt	Libraries, Modules and Dialogs				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020500.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147317	1			0	lt	\<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/01020500.xhp\" name=\"Libraries, Modules and Dialogs\"\>Libraries, Modules and Dialogs\</link\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020500.xhp	0	help	par_id3147427	2			0	lt	The following describes the basic use of libraries, modules and dialogs in $[officename] Basic.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020500.xhp	0	help	par_id3146120	3			0	lt	$[officename] Basic provides tools to help you structuring your projects. It supports various "units" which enable you to group individual SUBS and FUNCTIONS in a Basic project.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020500.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148575	5			0	lt	Libraries				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020500.xhp	0	help	par_id3150011	6			0	lt	Libraries serve as a tool for organizing modules, and can either be attached to a document or a template. When the document or a template is saved, all modules contained in the library are automatically saved as well.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020500.xhp	0	help	par_id3151112	7			0	lt	A library can contain up to 16,000 modules.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020500.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149262	8			0	lt	Modules				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020500.xhp	0	help	par_id3156441	9			0	lt	A module contains SUBS and FUNCTIONS along with variable declarations. The length of the program that can be saved in a module is limited to 64 KB. If more space is required you can divide a $[officename] Basic project among several modules, and then save them in a single library.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020500.xhp	0	help	hd_id3152577	11			0	lt	Dialog Modules				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020500.xhp	0	help	par_id3149377	12			0	lt	Dialog modules contain dialog definitions, including the dialog box properties, the properties of each dialog element and the events assigned. Since a dialog module can only contain a single dialog, they are often referred to as "dialogs".				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030000.xhp	0	help	tit				0	lt	Integrated Development Environment (IDE)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030000.xhp	0	help	bm_id3145090				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>Basic IDE;Integrated Development Environment\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>IDE;Integrated Development Environment\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030000.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145090	1			0	lt	\<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/01030000.xhp\" name=\"Integrated Development Environment (IDE)\"\>Integrated Development Environment (IDE)\</link\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030000.xhp	0	help	par_id3146795	2			0	lt	This section describes the Integrated Development Environment for $[officename] Basic.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030100.xhp	0	help	tit				0	lt	IDE Overview				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147291	1			0	lt	\<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/01030100.xhp\" name=\"IDE Overview\"\>IDE Overview\</link\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030100.xhp	0	help	par_id3156344	3			0	lt	The \<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/main0211.xhp\" name=\"Macro Toolbar\"\>\<emph\>Macro Toolbar\</emph\>\</link\> in the IDE provides various icons for editing and testing programs.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030100.xhp	0	help	par_id3151210	4			0	lt	In the \<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/01030200.xhp\" name=\"Editor window\"\>\<emph\>Editor window\</emph\>\</link\>, directly below the Macro toolbar, you can edit the Basic program code. The column on the left side is used to set breakpoints in the program code.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154686	5			0	lt	The \<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/01050100.xhp\" name=\"Watch\"\>\<emph\>Watch window\</emph\>\</link\> (observer) is located below the Editor window at the left, and displays the contents of variables or arrays during a single step process.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030100.xhp	0	help	par_id3145787	8			0	lt	The \<emph\>Call Stack\</emph\> window to the right provides information about the call stack of SUBS and FUNCTIONS when a program runs.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030100.xhp	0	help	par_id3147434	6			0	lt	\<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/01050000.xhp\" name=\"Basic IDE\"\>Basic IDE\</link\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030200.xhp	0	help	tit				0	lt	The Basic Editor				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030200.xhp	0	help	bm_id3148647				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>saving;Basic code\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>loading;Basic code\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>Basic editor\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>navigating;in Basic projects\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>long lines;in Basic editor\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>lines of text;in Basic editor\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>continuation;long lines in editor\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030200.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147264	1			0	lt	\<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/01030200.xhp\" name=\"The Basic Editor\"\>The Basic Editor\</link\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030200.xhp	0	help	par_id3145069	3			0	lt	The Basic Editor provides the standard editing functions you are familiar with when working in a text document. It supports the functions of the \<emph\>Edit\</emph\> menu (Cut, Delete, Paste), the ability to select text with the Shift key, as well as cursor positioning functions (for example, moving from word to word with \<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>Command\</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Ctrl\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\> and the arrow keys).				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030200.xhp	0	help	par_id3154686	31			0	lt	Long lines can be split into several parts by inserting a space and an underline character _ as the last two characters of a line. This connects the line with the following line to one logical line. (If "Option Compatible" is used in the same Basic module, the line continuation feature is also valid for comment lines.)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030200.xhp	0	help	par_id3151042	32			0	lt	If you press the \<emph\>Run BASIC\</emph\> icon on the \<emph\>Macro\</emph\> bar, program execution starts at the first line of the Basic editor. The program executes the first Sub or Function and then program execution stops. The "Sub Main" does not take precedence on program execution.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030200.xhp	0	help	par_id59816				0	lt	Insert your Basic code between the Sub Main and End Sub lines that you see when you first open the IDE. Alternatively, delete all lines and then enter your own Basic code.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030200.xhp	0	help	hd_id3125863	4			0	lt	Navigating in a Project				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030200.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145785	6			0	lt	The Library List				20130618 17:22:18
