helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	hd_id3159176	8			0	lt	Parameters:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3149415	9			0	lt	\<emph\>Number:\</emph\> Numeric expression that you want to convert to a formatted string.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3147531	10			0	lt	\<emph\>Format:\</emph\> String that specifies the format code for the number. If \<emph\>Format\</emph\> is omitted, the Format function works like the \<emph\>Str\</emph\> function.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147561	47			0	lt	Formatting Codes				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3147265	11			0	lt	The following list describes the codes that you can use for formatting a number:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3153380	12			0	lt	\<emph\>0:\</emph\> If \<emph\>Number\</emph\> has a digit at the position of the 0 in the format code, the digit is displayed, otherwise a zero is displayed.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3151210	13			0	lt	If \<emph\>Number\</emph\> has fewer digits than the number of zeros in the format code, (on either side of the decimal), leading or trailing zeros are displayed. If the number has more digits to the left of the decimal separator than the amount of zeros in the format code, the additional digits are displayed without formatting.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3151176	14			0	lt	Decimal places in the number are rounded according to the number of zeros that appear after the decimal separator in the \<emph\>Format \</emph\>code.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3154123	15			0	lt	\<emph\>#:\</emph\> If \<emph\>Number\</emph\> contains a digit at the position of the # placeholder in the \<emph\>Format\</emph\> code, the digit is displayed, otherwise nothing is displayed at this position.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3148452	16			0	lt	This symbol works like the 0, except that leading or trailing zeroes are not displayed if there are more # characters in the format code than digits in the number. Only the relevant digits of the number are displayed.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3159150	17			0	lt	\<emph\>.:\</emph\> The decimal placeholder determines the number of decimal places to the left and right of the decimal separator.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3159252	18			0	lt	If the format code contains only # placeholders to the left of this symbol, numbers less than 1 begin with a decimal separator. To always display a leading zero with fractional numbers, use 0 as a placeholder for the first digit to the left of the decimal separator.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3153368	19			0	lt	\<emph\>%:\</emph\> Multiplies the number by 100 and inserts the percent sign (%) where the number appears in the format code.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3149481	20			0	lt	\<emph\>E- E+ e- e+ :\</emph\> If the format code contains at least one digit placeholder (0 or #) to the right of the symbol E-, E+, e-, or e+, the number is formatted in the scientific or exponential format. The letter E or e is inserted between the number and the exponent. The number of placeholders for digits to the right of the symbol determines the number of digits in the exponent.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3149262	21			0	lt	If the exponent is negative, a minus sign is displayed directly before an exponent with E-, E+, e-, e+. If the exponent is positive, a plus sign is only displayed before exponents with E+ or e+.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3148617	23			0	lt	The thousands delimiter is displayed if the format code contains the delimiter enclosed by digit placeholders (0 or #).				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3163713	29			0	lt	The use of a period as a thousands and decimal separator is dependent on the regional setting. When you enter a number directly in Basic source code, always use a period as decimal delimiter. The actual character displayed as a decimal separator depends on the number format in your system settings.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3152887	24			0	lt	\<emph\>- + $ ( ) space:\</emph\> A plus (+), minus (-), dollar ($), space, or brackets entered directly in the format code is displayed as a literal character.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3148576	25			0	lt	To display characters other than the ones listed here, you must precede it by a backslash (\\), or enclose it in quotation marks (" ").				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3153139	26			0	lt	\\ : The backslash displays the next character in the format code.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3153366	27			0	lt	Characters in the format code that have a special meaning can only be displayed as literal characters if they are preceded by a backslash. The backslash itself is not displayed, unless you enter a double backslash (\\\\) in the format code.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3155411	28			0	lt	Characters that must be preceded by a backslash in the format code in order to be displayed as literal characters are date- and time-formatting characters (a, c, d, h, m, n, p, q, s, t, w, y, /, :), numeric-formatting characters (#, 0, %, E, e, comma, period), and string-formatting characters (@, &, <, >, !).				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3145749	30			0	lt	You can also use the following predefined number formats. Except for "General Number", all of the predefined format codes return the number as a decimal number with two decimal places.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3150113	31			0	lt	If you use predefined formats, the name of the format must be enclosed in quotation marks.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149377	32			0	lt	Predefined format				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3154730	33			0	lt	\<emph\>General Number:\</emph\> Numbers are displayed as entered.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3153158	34			0	lt	\<emph\>Currency:\</emph\> Inserts a dollar sign in front of the number and encloses negative numbers in brackets.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3154490	35			0	lt	\<emph\>Fixed:\</emph\> Displays at least one digit in front of the decimal separator.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3153415	36			0	lt	\<emph\>Standard:\</emph\> Displays numbers with a thousands separator.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3150715	37			0	lt	\<emph\>Percent:\</emph\> Multiplies the number by 100 and appends a percent sign to the number.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3153836	38			0	lt	\<emph\>Scientific:\</emph\> Displays numbers in scientific format (for example, 1.00E+03 for 1000).				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3153707	39			0	lt	A format code can be divided into three sections that are separated by semicolons. The first part defines the format for positive values, the second part for negative values, and the third part for zero. If you only specify one format code, it applies to all numbers.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149019	40			0	lt	Example:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3156054	41			0	lt	Sub ExampleFormat				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3148993	42			0	lt	MsgBox Format(6328.2, "##,##0.00")				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_idN107A2				0	lt	REM always use a period as decimal delimiter when you enter numbers in Basic source code.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3147339	46			0	lt	REM displays for example 6,328.20 in English locale, 6.328,20 in German locale.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3156382	43			0	lt	End Sub				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120302.xhp	0	help	tit				0	lt	LCase Function [Runtime]				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120302.xhp	0	help	bm_id3152363				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>LCase function\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120302.xhp	0	help	hd_id3152363	1			0	lt	\<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/03120302.xhp\" name=\"LCase Function [Runtime]\"\>LCase Function [Runtime]\</link\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120302.xhp	0	help	par_id3145609	2			0	lt	Converts all uppercase letters in a string to lowercase.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120302.xhp	0	help	par_id3154347	3			0	lt	See also: \<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/03120310.xhp\" name=\"UCase\"\>UCase\</link\> Function				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120302.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149456	4			0	lt	Syntax:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120302.xhp	0	help	par_id3150791	5			0	lt	LCase (Text As String)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120302.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154940	6			0	lt	Return value:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120302.xhp	0	help	par_id3144760	7			0	lt	String				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120302.xhp	0	help	hd_id3151043	8			0	lt	Parameters:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120302.xhp	0	help	par_id3153193	9			0	lt	\<emph\>Text:\</emph\> Any string expression that you want to convert.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120302.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148451	10			0	lt	Example:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120302.xhp	0	help	par_id3149203	11			0	lt	Sub ExampleLUCase				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120302.xhp	0	help	par_id3150440	12			0	lt	Dim sVar As String				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120302.xhp	0	help	par_id3153367	13			0	lt	sVar = "Las Vegas"				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120302.xhp	0	help	par_id3146121	14			0	lt	Print LCase(sVar) REM Returns "las vegas"				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120302.xhp	0	help	par_id3146986	15			0	lt	Print UCase(sVar) REM Returns "LAS VEGAS"				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120302.xhp	0	help	par_id3153575	16			0	lt	end Sub				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120303.xhp	0	help	tit				0	lt	Left Function [Runtime]				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120303.xhp	0	help	bm_id3149346				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>Left function\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120303.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149346	1			0	lt	\<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/03120303.xhp\" name=\"Left Function [Runtime]\"\>Left Function [Runtime]\</link\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120303.xhp	0	help	par_id3147242	2			0	lt	Returns the number of leftmost characters that you specify of a string expression.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120303.xhp	0	help	hd_id3156153	3			0	lt	Syntax:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120303.xhp	0	help	par_id3150771	4			0	lt	Left (Text As String, n As Long)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120303.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153824	5			0	lt	Return value:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120303.xhp	0	help	par_id3147530	6			0	lt	String				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120303.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148946	7			0	lt	Parameters:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120303.xhp	0	help	par_id3148552	8			0	lt	\<emph\>Text:\</emph\> Any string expression that you want to return the leftmost characters from.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120303.xhp	0	help	par_id3149456	9			0	lt	\<emph\>n:\</emph\> Numeric expression that specifies the number of characters that you want to return. If \<emph\>n\</emph\> = 0, a zero-length string is returned. The maximum allowed value is 65535.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120303.xhp	0	help	par_id3150791	10			0	lt	The following example converts a date in YYYY.MM.DD format to MM/DD/YYYY format.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120303.xhp	0	help	hd_id3125863	11			0	lt	Example:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120303.xhp	0	help	par_id3144761	12			0	lt	Sub ExampleUSDate				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120303.xhp	0	help	par_id3153194	13			0	lt	Dim sInput As String				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120303.xhp	0	help	par_id3154217	14			0	lt	Dim sUS_date As String				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120303.xhp	0	help	par_id3150448	15			0	lt	sInput = InputBox("Please input a date in the international format 'YYYY-MM-DD'")				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120303.xhp	0	help	par_id3149203	16			0	lt	sUS_date = Mid(sInput, 6, 2)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120303.xhp	0	help	par_id3150439	17			0	lt	sUS_date = sUS_date & "/"				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120303.xhp	0	help	par_id3153770	18			0	lt	sUS_date = sUS_date & Right(sInput, 2)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120303.xhp	0	help	par_id3161833	19			0	lt	sUS_date = sUS_date & "/"				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120303.xhp	0	help	par_id3147215	20			0	lt	sUS_date = sUS_date & Left(sInput, 4)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120303.xhp	0	help	par_id3149666	21			0	lt	MsgBox sUS_date				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120303.xhp	0	help	par_id3153138	22			0	lt	End Sub				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120304.xhp	0	help	tit				0	lt	LSet Statement [Runtime]				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120304.xhp	0	help	bm_id3143268				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>LSet statement\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120304.xhp	0	help	hd_id3143268	1			0	lt	\<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/03120304.xhp\" name=\"LSet Statement [Runtime]\"\>LSet Statement [Runtime]\</link\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120304.xhp	0	help	par_id3155419	2			0	lt	Aligns a string to the left of a string variable, or copies a variable of a user-defined type to another variable of a different user-defined type.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120304.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145317	3			0	lt	Syntax:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120304.xhp	0	help	par_id3150984	4			0	lt	LSet Var As String = Text or LSet Var1 = Var2				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120304.xhp	0	help	hd_id3143271	5			0	lt	Parameters:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120304.xhp	0	help	par_id3145610	6			0	lt	\<emph\>Var:\</emph\> Any String variable that contains the string that you want align to the left.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120304.xhp	0	help	par_id3154346	7			0	lt	\<emph\>Text:\</emph\> String that you want to align to the left of the string variable.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120304.xhp	0	help	par_id3151054	8			0	lt	\<emph\>Var1:\</emph\> Name of the user-defined type variable that you want to copy to.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120304.xhp	0	help	par_id3153361	9			0	lt	\<emph\>Var2:\</emph\> Name of the user-defined type variable that you want to copy from.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120304.xhp	0	help	par_id3154686	10			0	lt	If the string is shorter than the string variable, \<emph\>LSet\</emph\> left-aligns the string within the string variable. Any remaining positions in the string variable are replaced by spaces. If the string is longer than the string variable, only the leftmost characters up to the length of the string variable are copied. With the \<emph\>LSet\</emph\> statement, you can also copy a user-defined type variable to another variable of the same type.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120304.xhp	0	help	hd_id3156282	11			0	lt	Example:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120304.xhp	0	help	par_id3153193	12			0	lt	Sub ExampleRLSet				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120304.xhp	0	help	par_id3150768	13			0	lt	Dim sVar As String				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120304.xhp	0	help	par_id3150447	14			0	lt	Dim sExpr As String				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120304.xhp	0	help	par_id3149561	16			0	lt	sVar = String(40,"*")				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120304.xhp	0	help	par_id3153768	17			0	lt	sExpr = "SBX"				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120304.xhp	0	help	par_id3152940	18			0	lt	REM Align "SBX" within the 40-character reference string				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120304.xhp	0	help	par_id3148647	19			0	lt	REM Replace asterisks with spaces				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120304.xhp	0	help	par_id3146119	20			0	lt	RSet sVar = sExpr				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120304.xhp	0	help	par_id3153365	21			0	lt	Print ">"; sVar; "<"				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120304.xhp	0	help	par_id3149260	23			0	lt	sVar = String(5,"*")				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120304.xhp	0	help	par_id3147436	24			0	lt	sExpr = "123457896"				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120304.xhp	0	help	par_id3146923	25			0	lt	RSet sVar = sExpr				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120304.xhp	0	help	par_id3151114	26			0	lt	Print ">"; sVar; "<"				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120304.xhp	0	help	par_id3155855	28			0	lt	sVar = String(40,"*")				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120304.xhp	0	help	par_id3145253	29			0	lt	sExpr = "SBX"				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120304.xhp	0	help	par_id3151075	30			0	lt	REM Left-align "SBX" within the 40-character reference string				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120304.xhp	0	help	par_id3147126	31			0	lt	LSet sVar = sExpr				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120304.xhp	0	help	par_id3154792	32			0	lt	Print ">"; sVar; "<"				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120304.xhp	0	help	par_id3154942	34			0	lt	sVar = String(5,"*")				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120304.xhp	0	help	par_id3155603	35			0	lt	sExpr = "123456789"				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120304.xhp	0	help	par_id3150716	36			0	lt	LSet sVar = sExpr				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120304.xhp	0	help	par_id3146969	37			0	lt	Print ">"; sVar; "<"				20130618 17:22:18
