helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\borders.xhp	0	help	par_id4511551				0	lt	\<image id=\"img_id8139591\" src=\"res/helpimg/border_ca_4.png\" width=\"1.2602in\" height=\"1.5937in\"\>\<alt id=\"alt_id8139591\"\>borders with a block selected\</alt\>\</image\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\borders.xhp	0	help	par_id5383465				0	lt	You cannot apply borders to multiple selections.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\borders.xhp	0	help	hd_id7790154				0	lt	Default Settings				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\borders.xhp	0	help	par_id2918485				0	lt	Click one of the \<emph\>Default\</emph\> icons to set or reset multiple borders.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\borders.xhp	0	help	par_id1836909				0	lt	The thin gray lines inside an icon show the borders that will be reset or cleared.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\borders.xhp	0	help	par_id5212561				0	lt	The dark lines inside an icon show the lines that will be set using the selected line style and color.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\borders.xhp	0	help	par_id4818872				0	lt	The thick gray lines inside an icon show the lines that will not be changed.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\borders.xhp	0	help	hd_id8989226				0	lt	Examples				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\borders.xhp	0	help	par_id622577				0	lt	Select a block of about 8x8 cells, then choose \<emph\>Format - Cells - Borders\</emph\>.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\borders.xhp	0	help	par_id8119754				0	lt	\<image id=\"img_id7261268\" src=\"res/helpimg/border_ca_5.png\" width=\"1.0937in\" height=\"0.2189in\"\>\<alt id=\"alt_id7261268\"\>default icon row of Borders tab page\</alt\>\</image\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\borders.xhp	0	help	par_id8964201				0	lt	Click the left icon to clear all lines. This removes all outer borders, all inner lines, and all diagonal lines.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\borders.xhp	0	help	par_id6048463				0	lt	Click the second icon from the left to set an outer border and to remove all other lines.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\borders.xhp	0	help	par_id1495406				0	lt	Click the rightmost icon to set an outer border. The inner lines are not changed, except the diagonal lines, which will be removed.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\borders.xhp	0	help	par_id9269386				0	lt	Now you can continue to see which lines the other icons will set or remove.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\borders.xhp	0	help	hd_id3593554				0	lt	User Defined Settings				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\borders.xhp	0	help	par_id4018066				0	lt	In the \<emph\>User defined\</emph\> area, you can click to set or remove individual lines. The preview shows lines in three different states. 				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\borders.xhp	0	help	par_id8004699				0	lt	Repeatedly click an edge or a corner to switch through the three different states.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\borders.xhp	0	help	par_id8037659				0	lt	Line types				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\borders.xhp	0	help	par_id2305978				0	lt	Image				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\borders.xhp	0	help	par_id8716086				0	lt	Meaning				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\borders.xhp	0	help	par_id3978087				0	lt	A black line				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\borders.xhp	0	help	par_id4065065				0	lt	\<image id=\"img_id9379863\" src=\"res/helpimg/border_ca_7.png\" width=\"1.2602in\" height=\"1.1252in\"\>\<alt id=\"alt_id9379863\"\>solid line for user defined border\</alt\>\</image\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\borders.xhp	0	help	par_id6987823				0	lt	A black line sets the corresponding line of the selected cells. The line is shown as a dotted line when you choose the 0.05 pt line style. Double lines are shown when you select a double line style.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\borders.xhp	0	help	par_id1209143				0	lt	A gray line				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\borders.xhp	0	help	par_id6653340				0	lt	\<image id=\"img_id6972563\" src=\"res/helpimg/border_ca_gray.png\" width=\"1.2602in\" height=\"1.1252in\"\>\<alt id=\"alt_id6972563\"\>gray line for user defined border\</alt\>\</image\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\borders.xhp	0	help	par_id2278817				0	lt	A gray line is shown when the corresponding line of the selected cells will not be changed. No line will be set or removed at this position.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\borders.xhp	0	help	par_id5374919				0	lt	A white line				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\borders.xhp	0	help	par_id52491				0	lt	\<image id=\"img_id3801080\" src=\"res/helpimg/border_ca_white.png\" width=\"1.2602in\" height=\"1.1252in\"\>\<alt id=\"alt_id3801080\"\>white line for user defined border\</alt\>\</image\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\borders.xhp	0	help	par_id372325				0	lt	A white line is shown when the corresponding line of the selected cells will be removed.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\borders.xhp	0	help	hd_id7282937				0	lt	Examples				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\borders.xhp	0	help	par_id4230780				0	lt	Select a single cell, then choose \<emph\>Format - Cells - Borders\</emph\>.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\borders.xhp	0	help	par_id1712393				0	lt	Click the lower edge to set a very thin line as a lower border. All other lines will be removed from the cell.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\borders.xhp	0	help	par_id5149693				0	lt	\<image id=\"img_id9467452\" src=\"res/helpimg/border_ca_6.png\" width=\"1.2602in\" height=\"1.1252in\"\>\<alt id=\"alt_id9467452\"\>setting a thin lower border\</alt\>\</image\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\borders.xhp	0	help	par_id5759453				0	lt	Choose a thicker line style and click the lower edge. This sets a thicker line as a lower border.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\borders.xhp	0	help	par_id6342051				0	lt	\<image id=\"img_id7431562\" src=\"res/helpimg/border_ca_7.png\" width=\"1.2602in\" height=\"1.1252in\"\>\<alt id=\"alt_id7431562\"\>setting a thick line as a border\</alt\>\</image\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\borders.xhp	0	help	par_id5775322				0	lt	Click the second \<emph\>Default\</emph\> icon from the left to set all four borders. Then repeatedly click the lower edge until a white line is shown. This removes the lower border.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\borders.xhp	0	help	par_id2882778				0	lt	\<image id=\"img_id8155766.00000001\" src=\"res/helpimg/border_ca_8.png\" width=\"1.2602in\" height=\"1.1252in\"\>\<alt id=\"alt_id8155766.00000001\"\>removing lower border\</alt\>\</image\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\borders.xhp	0	help	par_id8102053				0	lt	You can combine several line types and styles. The last image shows how to set thick outer borders (the thick black lines), while any diagonal lines inside the cell will not be touched (gray lines).				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\borders.xhp	0	help	par_id2102420				0	lt	\<image id=\"img_id5380718\" src=\"res/helpimg/border_ca_9.png\" width=\"1.2602in\" height=\"1.1252in\"\>\<alt id=\"alt_id5380718\"\>advanced example for cell borders\</alt\>\</image\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_date.xhp	0	help	tit				0	lt	Calculating With Dates and Times				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_date.xhp	0	help	bm_id3146120				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>dates; in cells\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>times; in cells\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>cells;date and time formats\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>current date and time values\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_date.xhp	0	help	hd_id3146120	11			0	lt	\<variable id=\"calc_date\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/calc_date.xhp\" name=\"Calculating With Dates and Times\"\>Calculating With Dates and Times\</link\>\</variable\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_date.xhp	0	help	par_id3154320	12			0	lt	In $[officename] Calc, you can perform calculations with current date and time values. As an example, to find out exactly how old you are in seconds or hours, follow the following steps:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_date.xhp	0	help	par_id3150750	13			0	lt	In a spreadsheet, enter your birthday in cell A1.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_date.xhp	0	help	par_id3145642	14			0	lt	Enter the following formula in cell A3: \<item type=\"literal\"\>=NOW()-A1\</item\>            				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_date.xhp	0	help	par_id3149020	52			0	lt	After pressing the \<item type=\"keycode\"\>Enter\</item\> key you will see the result in date format. Since the result should show the difference between two dates as a number of days, you must format cell A3 as a number.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_date.xhp	0	help	par_id3155335	53			0	lt	Place the cursor in cell A3, right-click to open a context menu and choose \<emph\>Format Cells\</emph\>.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_date.xhp	0	help	par_id3147343	54			0	lt	The \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Format Cells\</item\> dialog appears. On the \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Numbers\</item\> tab, the "Number" category will appear already highlighted. The format is set to "General", which causes the result of a calculation containing date entries to be displayed as a date. To display the result as a number, set the number format to "-1,234" and close the dialog with the \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>OK\</item\> button.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_date.xhp	0	help	par_id3147001	15			0	lt	The number of days between today's date and the specified date is displayed in cell A3.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_date.xhp	0	help	par_id3150304	16			0	lt	Experiment with some additional formulas: in A4 enter =A3*24 to calculate the hours, in A5 enter =A4*60 for the minutes, and in A6 enter =A5*60 for seconds. Press the \<item type=\"keycode\"\>Enter\</item\> key after each formula.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_date.xhp	0	help	par_id3149207	17			0	lt	The time since your date of birth will be calculated and displayed in the various units. The values are calculated as of the exact moment when you entered the last formula and pressed the \<item type=\"keycode\"\>Enter\</item\> key. This value is not automatically updated, although "Now" continuously changes. In the \<emph\>Tools\</emph\> menu, the menu item \<emph\>Cell Contents - AutoCalculate\</emph\> is normally active; however, automatic calculation does not apply to the function NOW. This ensures that your computer is not solely occupied with updating the sheet.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	tit				0	lt	Automatically Calculating Series				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	bm_id3150769				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>series; calculating\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>calculating; series\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>linear series\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>growth series\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>date series\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>powers of 2 calculations\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>cells; filling automatically\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>automatic cell filling\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>AutoFill function\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>filling;cells, automatically\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150769	6			0	lt	\<variable id=\"calc_series\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/calc_series.xhp\" name=\"Automatically Calculating Series\"\>Automatically Filling in Data Based on Adjacent Cells\</link\>\</variable\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	par_idN106A8				0	lt	You can automatically fill cells with data with the AutoFill command or the Series command.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	par_idN106D3				0	lt	Using AutoFill				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	par_idN106D7				0	lt	AutoFill automatically generates a data series based on a defined pattern.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	par_id3154319	7			0	lt	On a sheet, click in a cell, and type a number.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	par_idN106CB				0	lt	Click in another cell and then click back in the cell where you typed the number.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	par_id3145272	16			0	lt	Drag the fill handle in the bottom right corner of the cell across the cells that you want to fill, and release the mouse button.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	par_id3145801	17			0	lt	The cells are filled with ascending numbers.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	par_idN106EE				0	lt	To quickly create a list of consecutive days, enter \<item type=\"literal\"\>Monday\</item\> in a cell, and drag the fill handle.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	par_id9720145				0	lt	Hold down \<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>Command \</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Ctrl \</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\>if you do not want to fill the cells with different values.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	par_id3154490	18			0	lt	If you select two or more adjacent cells that contain different numbers, and drag, the remaining cells are filled with the arithmetic pattern that is recognized in the numbers. The AutoFill function also recognizes customized lists that are defined under \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>\<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>%PRODUCTNAME - Preferences\</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Tools - Options\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\> - %PRODUCTNAME Calc - Sort Lists\</item\>.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	par_idN10737				0	lt	You can double-click the fill handle to automatically fill all empty columns of the current data block. For example, first enter Jan into A1 and drag the fill handle down to A12 to get the twelve months in the first column. Now enter some values into B1 and C1. Select those two cells, and double-click the fill handle. This fills automatically the data block B1:C12.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	par_idN10713				0	lt	Using a Defined Series				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	par_id3150749	9			0	lt	Select the cell range in the sheet that you want to fill.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	par_id3154754	19			0	lt	Choose \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Edit - Fill - Series\</item\>.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	par_idN10716				0	lt	Select the parameters for the series. 				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	par_idN10731				0	lt	If you select a \<emph\>linear\</emph\> series, the increment that you enter is \<emph\>added\</emph\> to each consecutive number in the series to create the next value.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	par_idN1073C				0	lt	If you select a \<emph\>growth\</emph\> series, the increment that you enter is \<emph\>multiplied\</emph\> by each consecutive number to create the next value.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	par_idN10747				0	lt	If you select a \<emph\>date\</emph\> series, the increment that you enter is added to the time unit that you specify.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	par_id3159173	20			0	lt	\<link href=\"text/shared/optionen/01060400.xhp\" name=\"Sort lists\"\>Sort lists\</link\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_timevalues.xhp	0	help	tit				0	lt	Calculating Time Differences				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_timevalues.xhp	0	help	bm_id3150769				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>calculating;time differences\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>time differences\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_timevalues.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150769	53			0	lt	\<variable id=\"calc_timevalues\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/calc_timevalues.xhp\" name=\"Calculating Time Differences\"\>Calculating Time Differences\</link\>\</variable\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_timevalues.xhp	0	help	par_id3149263	54			0	lt	If you want to calculate time differences, for example, the time between 23:30 and 01:10 in the same night, use the following formula:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_timevalues.xhp	0	help	par_id3159153	55			0	lt	=(B2<A2)+B2-A2				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_timevalues.xhp	0	help	par_id3152598	56			0	lt	The later time is B2 and the earlier time is A2. The result of the example is 01:40 or 1 hour and 40 minutes.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_timevalues.xhp	0	help	par_id3145271	57			0	lt	In the formula, an entire 24-hour day has a value of 1 and one hour has a value of 1/24. The logical value in parentheses is 0 or 1, corresponding to 0 or 24 hours. The result returned by the formula is automatically issued in time format due to the sequence of the operands.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calculate.xhp	0	help	tit				0	lt	Calculating in Spreadsheets				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calculate.xhp	0	help	bm_id3150791				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>spreadsheets; calculating\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>calculating; spreadsheets\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>formulas; calculating\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calculate.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150791				0	lt	\<variable id=\"calculate\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/calculate.xhp\" name=\"Calculating in Spreadsheets\"\>Calculating in Spreadsheets\</link\>\</variable\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calculate.xhp	0	help	par_id3146120				0	lt	The following is an example of a calculation in $[officename] Calc.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calculate.xhp	0	help	par_id3153951				0	lt	Click in a cell, and type a number				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calculate.xhp	0	help	par_idN10656				0	lt	Press Enter.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calculate.xhp	0	help	par_idN1065D				0	lt	The cursor moves down to the next cell.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calculate.xhp	0	help	par_id3155064				0	lt	Enter another number.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calculate.xhp	0	help	par_idN1066F				0	lt	Press the Tab key.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calculate.xhp	0	help	par_idN10676				0	lt	The cursor moves to the right into the next cell.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calculate.xhp	0	help	par_id3154253				0	lt	Type in a formula, for example, \<item type=\"literal\"\>=A3 * A4 / 100.\</item\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calculate.xhp	0	help	par_idN1068B				0	lt	 Press Enter.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calculate.xhp	0	help	par_id3147343				0	lt	The result of the formula appears in the cell. If you want, you can edit the formula in the input line of the Formula bar.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calculate.xhp	0	help	par_id3155378				0	lt	When you edit a formula, the new result is calculated automatically.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_enter.xhp	0	help	tit				0	lt	Entering Values				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_enter.xhp	0	help	bm_id3150868				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>values; inserting in multiple cells\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>inserting;values\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>cell ranges;selecting for data entries\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>areas, see also cell ranges\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_enter.xhp	0	help	hd_id3405255				0	lt	\<variable id=\"cell_enter\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/cell_enter.xhp\"\>Entering Values\</link\>\</variable\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_enter.xhp	0	help	par_id7147129				0	lt	Calc can simplify entering data and values into multiple cells. You can change some settings to conform to your preferences.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_enter.xhp	0	help	hd_id5621509				0	lt	To Enter Values Into a Range of Cells Manually				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_enter.xhp	0	help	par_id8200018				0	lt	There are two features that assist you when you enter a block of data manually.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_enter.xhp	0	help	hd_id1867427				0	lt	Area Detection for New Rows				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_enter.xhp	0	help	par_id7908871				0	lt	In the row below a heading row, you can advance from one cell to the next with the Tab key. After you enter the value into the last cell in the current row, press Enter. Calc positions the cursor below the first cell of the current block.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_enter.xhp	0	help	par_id6196783				0	lt	\<image id=\"img_id6473586\" src=\"res/helpimg/area1.png\" width=\"4.8335in\" height=\"1.5937in\"\>\<alt id=\"alt_id6473586\"\>area detection\</alt\>\</image\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_enter.xhp	0	help	par_id8118839				0	lt	In row 3, press Tab to advance from cell B3 to C3, D3, and E3. Then press Enter to advance to B4.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_enter.xhp	0	help	hd_id3583788				0	lt	Area Selection				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_enter.xhp	0	help	par_id2011780				0	lt	Use drag-and-drop to select the area where you want to input values. But start dragging from the last cell of the area and release the mouse button when you have selected the first cell. Now you can start to input values. Always press the Tab key to advance to the next cell. You will not leave the selected area.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_enter.xhp	0	help	par_id7044282				0	lt	\<image id=\"img_id2811365\" src=\"res/helpimg/area2.png\" width=\"4.8335in\" height=\"1.5937in\"\>\<alt id=\"alt_id2811365\"\>area selection\</alt\>\</image\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_enter.xhp	0	help	par_id3232520				0	lt	Select the area from E7 to B3. Now B3 is waiting for your input. Press Tab to advance to the next cell within the selected area.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_enter.xhp	0	help	hd_id8950163				0	lt	To Enter Values to a Range of Cells Automatically				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_enter.xhp	0	help	par_id633869				0	lt	See \<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/calc_series.xhp\"\>Automatically Filling in Data Based on Adjacent Cells\</link\>.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_protect.xhp	0	help	tit				0	lt	Protecting Cells from Changes				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_protect.xhp	0	help	bm_id3146119				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>protecting;cells and sheets\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>cells; protecting\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>cell protection; enabling\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>sheets; protecting\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>documents; protecting\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>cells; hiding for printing\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>changing; sheet protection\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>hiding;formulas\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>formulas;hiding\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_protect.xhp	0	help	hd_id3146119	5			0	lt	\<variable id=\"cell_protect\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/cell_protect.xhp\" name=\"Protecting Cells from Changes\"\>Protecting Cells from Changes\</link\>\</variable\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_protect.xhp	0	help	par_id3153368	17			0	lt	In \<item type=\"productname\"\>%PRODUCTNAME\</item\> Calc you can protect sheets and the document as a whole. You can choose whether the cells are protected against accidental changes, whether the formulas can be viewed from within Calc, whether the cells are visible or whether the cells can be printed.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_protect.xhp	0	help	par_id3145261	18			0	lt	Protection can be provided by means of a password, but it does not have to be. If you have assigned a password, protection can only be removed once the correct password has been entered.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_protect.xhp	0	help	par_id3148576	19			0	lt	Note that the cell protection for cells with the \<emph\>Protected\</emph\> attribute is only effective when you protect the whole sheet. In the default condition, every cell has the \<emph\>Protected\</emph\> attribute. Therefore you must remove the attribute selectively for those cells where the user may make changes. You then protect the whole sheet and save the document.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_protect.xhp	0	help	par_id5974303				0	lt	These protection features are just switches to prevent accidental action. The features are not intended to provide any secure protection. For example, by exporting a sheet to another file format, a user may be able to surpass the protection features. There is only one secure protection: the password that you can apply when saving an OpenDocument file. A file that has been saved with a password can be opened only with the same password.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_protect.xhp	0	help	par_idN1066B				0	lt	Select the cells that you want to specify the cell protection options for.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_protect.xhp	0	help	par_id3149019	7			0	lt	Choose \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Format - Cells\</item\> and click the \<emph\>Cell Protection\</emph\> tab.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_protect.xhp	0	help	par_id3152985	9			0	lt	Select the protection options that you want. All options will be applied only after you protect the sheet from the Tools menu - see below.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_protect.xhp	0	help	par_id31529866655				0	lt	Uncheck \<emph\>Protected\</emph\> to allow the user to change the currently selected cells.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_protect.xhp	0	help	par_id3152898	10			0	lt	Select \<emph\>Protected\</emph\> to prevent changes to the contents and the format of a cell. 				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_protect.xhp	0	help	par_idN1069A				0	lt	Select \<emph\>Hide formula\</emph\> to hide and to protect formulas from changes.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_protect.xhp	0	help	par_idN106A1				0	lt	Select \<emph\>Hide when printing\</emph\> to hide protected cells in the printed document. The cells are not hidden onscreen.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_protect.xhp	0	help	par_id3152872	11			0	lt	Click \<emph\>OK\</emph\>.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_protect.xhp	0	help	par_id3145362	12			0	lt	Apply the protection options.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_protect.xhp	0	help	par_idN106C0				0	lt	To protect the cells from being changed / viewed / printed according to your settings in the \<emph\>Format - Cells\</emph\> dialog, choose \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Tools - Protect Document - Sheet\</item\>.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_protect.xhp	0	help	par_idN106C7				0	lt	To protect the structure of the document, for example the count, \<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/rename_table.xhp\"\>names\</link\>, and order of the sheets, from being changed, choose \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Tools - Protect Document - Document\</item\>.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_protect.xhp	0	help	par_idN106CF				0	lt	(Optional) Enter a password.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_protect.xhp	0	help	par_idN106D2				0	lt	If you forget your password, you cannot deactivate the protection. If you only want to protect cells from accidental changes, set the sheet protection, but do not enter a password.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_protect.xhp	0	help	par_id3153810	13			0	lt	Click \<emph\>OK\</emph\>. 				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_protect.xhp	0	help	par_idN10B8C				0	lt	            \<embedvar href=\"text/shared/guide/digital_signatures.xhp#digital_signatures\"/\>         				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_unprotect.xhp	0	help	tit				0	lt	Unprotecting Cells				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_unprotect.xhp	0	help	bm_id3153252				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>cell protection; unprotecting\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>protecting; unprotecting cells\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>unprotecting cells\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_unprotect.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153252	14			0	lt	\<variable id=\"cell_unprotect\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/cell_unprotect.xhp\" name=\"Unprotecting Cells\"\>Unprotecting Cells\</link\> \</variable\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_unprotect.xhp	0	help	par_id3151112	15			0	lt	Click the sheet for which you want to cancel the protection.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_unprotect.xhp	0	help	par_id3149656	16			0	lt	Select \<emph\>Tools - Protect Document\</emph\>, then choose \<emph\>Sheet\</emph\> or \<emph\>Document\</emph\> to remove the check mark indicating the protected status.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_unprotect.xhp	0	help	par_id3145171	17			0	lt	If you have assigned a password, enter it in this dialog and click \<emph\>OK\</emph\>.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_unprotect.xhp	0	help	par_id3153771	18			0	lt	The cells can now be edited, the formulas can be viewed, and all cells can be printed until you reactivate the protection for the sheet or document.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellcopy.xhp	0	help	tit				0	lt	Only Copy Visible Cells				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellcopy.xhp	0	help	bm_id3150440				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>cells; copying/deleting/formatting/moving\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>rows;visible and invisible\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>copying; visible cells only\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>formatting;visible cells only\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>moving;visible cells only\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>deleting;visible cells only\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>invisible cells\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>filters;copying visible cells only\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>hidden cells\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellcopy.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150440	1			0	lt	\<variable id=\"cellcopy\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/cellcopy.xhp\" name=\"Only Copy Visible Cells\"\>Only Copy Visible Cells\</link\>\</variable\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellcopy.xhp	0	help	par_id3148577	2			0	lt	Assume you have hidden a few rows in a cell range. Now you want to copy, delete, or format only the remaining visible rows.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellcopy.xhp	0	help	par_id3154729	3			0	lt	$[officename] behavior depends on how the cells were made invisible, by a filter or manually.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellcopy.xhp	0	help	par_id3155603	4			0	lt	Method and Action				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellcopy.xhp	0	help	par_id3150751	5			0	lt	Result				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellcopy.xhp	0	help	par_id3149018	6			0	lt	Cells were filtered by AutoFilters, standard filters or advanced filters.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellcopy.xhp	0	help	par_id3150044	7			0	lt	Copy, delete, move, or format a selection of currently visible cells.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellcopy.xhp	0	help	par_id3146918	8			0	lt	Only the visible cells of the selection are copied, deleted, moved, or formatted.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellcopy.xhp	0	help	par_id3166427	12			0	lt	Cells were hidden using the \<emph\>Hide\</emph\> command in the context menu of the row or column headers, or through an \<link href=\"text/scalc/01/12080000.xhp\" name=\"outline\"\>outline\</link\>.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellcopy.xhp	0	help	par_id3152990	13			0	lt	Copy, delete, move, or format a selection of currently visible cells.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellcopy.xhp	0	help	par_id3154371	14			0	lt	All cells of the selection, including the hidden cells, are copied, deleted, moved, or formatted.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellreference_dragdrop.xhp	0	help	tit				0	lt	Referencing Cells by Drag-and-Drop				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellreference_dragdrop.xhp	0	help	bm_id3154686				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>drag and drop; referencing cells\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>cells; referencing by drag and drop \</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>references;inserting by drag and drop\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>inserting;references, by drag and drop\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellreference_dragdrop.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154686	16			0	lt	\<variable id=\"cellreference_dragdrop\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/cellreference_dragdrop.xhp\" name=\"Referencing Cells by Drag-and-Drop\"\>Referencing Cells by Drag-and-Drop\</link\>\</variable\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellreference_dragdrop.xhp	0	help	par_id3156444	17			0	lt	With the help of the Navigator you can reference cells from one sheet to another sheet in the same document or in a different document. The cells can be inserted as a copy, link, or hyperlink. The range to be inserted must be defined with a name in the original file so that it can be inserted in the target file.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellreference_dragdrop.xhp	0	help	par_id3152576	25			0	lt	Open the document that contains the source cells.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellreference_dragdrop.xhp	0	help	par_id3154011	26			0	lt	To set the source range as the range, select the cells and choose \<emph\>Insert - Names - Define\</emph\>. Save the source document, and do not close it.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellreference_dragdrop.xhp	0	help	par_id3151073	18			0	lt	Open the sheet in which you want to insert something.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellreference_dragdrop.xhp	0	help	par_id3154732	19			0	lt	Open the \<link href=\"text/scalc/01/02110000.xhp\" name=\"Navigator\"\>Navigator\</link\>. In the lower box of the Navigator select the source file.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellreference_dragdrop.xhp	0	help	par_id3150752	22			0	lt	In the Navigator, the source file object appears under "Range names".				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellreference_dragdrop.xhp	0	help	par_id3154754	27			0	lt	Using the \<emph\>Drag Mode\</emph\> icon in Navigator, choose whether you want the reference to be a hyperlink, link, or copy.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellreference_dragdrop.xhp	0	help	par_id3154256	23			0	lt	Click the name under "Range names" in the Navigator, and drag into the cell of the current sheet where you want to insert the reference.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellreference_dragdrop.xhp	0	help	par_id3149565	24			0	lt	This method can also be used to insert a range from another sheet of the same document into the current sheet. Select the active document as source in step 4 above.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellreferences.xhp	0	help	tit				0	lt	Referencing a Cell in Another Document				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellreferences.xhp	0	help	bm_id3147436				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>sheet references\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>references; to cells in other sheets/documents\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>cells; operating in another document\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>documents;references\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellreferences.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147436	9			0	lt	\<variable id=\"cellreferences\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/cellreferences.xhp\" name=\"Referencing Other Sheets\"\>Referencing Other Sheets\</link\>\</variable\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellreferences.xhp	0	help	par_id9663075				0	lt	In a sheet cell you can show a reference to a cell in another sheet.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellreferences.xhp	0	help	par_id1879329				0	lt	In the same way, a reference can also be made to a cell from another document provided that this document has already been saved as a file.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellreferences.xhp	0	help	hd_id7122409				0	lt	To Reference a Cell in the Same Document				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellreferences.xhp	0	help	par_id2078005				0	lt	Open a new, empty spreadsheet.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellreferences.xhp	0	help	par_id4943693				0	lt	By way of example, enter the following formula in cell A1 of Sheet1:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellreferences.xhp	0	help	par_id9064302				0	lt	               \<item type=\"literal\"\>=Sheet2.A1\</item\>            				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellreferences.xhp	0	help	par_id7609790				0	lt	Click the \<emph\>Sheet 2\</emph\> tab at the bottom of the spreadsheet. Set the cursor in cell A1 there and enter text or a number.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellreferences.xhp	0	help	par_id809961				0	lt	If you switch back to Sheet1, you will see the same content in cell A1 there. If the contents of Sheet2.A1 change, then the contents of Sheet1.A1 also change.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellreferences.xhp	0	help	hd_id9209570				0	lt	To Reference a Cell in Another Document				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellreferences.xhp	0	help	par_id5949278				0	lt	Choose \<emph\>File - Open\</emph\>, to load an existing spreadsheet document.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellreferences.xhp	0	help	par_id8001953				0	lt	Choose \<emph\>File - New\</emph\>, to open a new spreadsheet document. Set the cursor in the cell where you want to insert the external data and enter an equals sign to indicate that you want to begin a formula.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellreferences.xhp	0	help	par_id8571123				0	lt	Now switch to the document you have just loaded. Click the cell with the data that you want to insert in the new document.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellreferences.xhp	0	help	par_id8261665				0	lt	Switch back to the new spreadsheet. In the input line you will now see how $[officename] Calc has added the reference to the formula for you. 				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellreferences.xhp	0	help	par_id5888241				0	lt	The reference to a cell of another document contains the name of the other document in single inverted commas, then a hash #, then the name of the sheet of the other document, followed by a point and the name of the cell.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellreferences.xhp	0	help	par_id7697683				0	lt	Confirm the formula by clicking the green check mark.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellreferences.xhp	0	help	par_id7099826				0	lt	If you drag the box in the lower right corner of the active cell to select a range of cells, $[officename] automatically inserts the corresponding references in the adjacent cells. As a result, the sheet name is preceded with a "$" sign to designate it as an absolute reference.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellreferences.xhp	0	help	par_id674459				0	lt	If you examine the name of the other document in this formula, you will notice that it is written as a \<link href=\"text/shared/00/00000002.xhp#url\" name=\"URL\"\>URL\</link\>. This means that you can also enter a URL from the Internet.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellreferences_url.xhp	0	help	tit				0	lt	References to Other Sheets and Referencing URLs				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellreferences_url.xhp	0	help	bm_id3150441				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>HTML; in sheet cells\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>references; URL in cells\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>cells; Internet references\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>URL; in Calc\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellreferences_url.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150441	15			0	lt	\<variable id=\"cellreferences_url\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/cellreferences_url.xhp\" name=\"Referencing URLs\"\>Referencing URLs\</link\>\</variable\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellreferences_url.xhp	0	help	par_id1955626				0	lt	For example, if you found an Internet page containing current stock exchange information in spreadsheet cells, you can load this page in $[officename] Calc by using the following procedure:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellreferences_url.xhp	0	help	par_id3152993	39			0	lt	In a $[officename] Calc document, position the cursor in the cell into which you want to insert the external data.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellreferences_url.xhp	0	help	par_id3145384	40			0	lt	Choose \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Insert - Link to External Data\</item\>. The \<link href=\"text/scalc/01/04090000.xhp\" name=\"External Data\"\>\<item type=\"menuitem\"\>External Data\</item\>\</link\> dialog appears.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellreferences_url.xhp	0	help	par_id3152892	41			0	lt	Enter the URL of the document or Web page in the dialog. The URL must be in the format: http://www.my-bank.com/table.html. The URL for local or local area network files is the path seen in the \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>File - Open\</item\> dialog.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellreferences_url.xhp	0	help	par_id3153068	42			0	lt	$[officename] loads the Web page or file in the "background", that is, without displaying it. In the large list box of the \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>External Data\</item\> dialog, you can see the name of all the sheets or named ranges you can choose from.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellreferences_url.xhp	0	help	par_id3153914	43			0	lt	Select one or more sheets or named ranges. You can also activate the automatic update function every "n" seconds and click \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>OK\</item\>.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellreferences_url.xhp	0	help	par_id3157979	44			0	lt	The contents will be inserted as a link in the $[officename] Calc document.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellreferences_url.xhp	0	help	par_id3144768	30			0	lt	Save your spreadsheet. When you open it again later, $[officename] Calc will update the linked cells following an inquiry.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellreferences_url.xhp	0	help	par_id3159204	38			0	lt	Under \<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>%PRODUCTNAME - Preferences\</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Tools - Options\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\> - \<link href=\"text/shared/optionen/01040900.xhp\" name=\"Spreadsheet - General\"\>\<item type=\"menuitem\"\>%PRODUCTNAME Calc - General\</item\>\</link\> you can choose to have the update, when opened, automatically carried out either always, upon request or never. The update can be started manually in the dialog under \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Edit - Links\</item\>.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_by_formula.xhp	0	help	tit				0	lt	Assigning Formats by Formula				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_by_formula.xhp	0	help	bm_id3145673				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>formats; assigning by formulas\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>cell formats; assigning by formulas\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>STYLE function example\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>cell styles;assigning by formulas\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>formulas;assigning cell formats\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_by_formula.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145673	13			0	lt	\<variable id=\"cellstyle_by_formula\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/cellstyle_by_formula.xhp\" name=\"Assigning Formats by Formula\"\>Assigning Formats by Formula\</link\> \</variable\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_by_formula.xhp	0	help	par_id3150275	14			0	lt	The STYLE() function can be added to an existing formula in a cell. For example, together with the CURRENT function, you can color a cell depending on its value. The formula =...+STYLE(IF(CURRENT()>3; "Red"; "Green")) applies the cell style "Red" to cells if the value is greater than 3, otherwise the cell style "Green" is applied.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_by_formula.xhp	0	help	par_id3151385	15			0	lt	If you would like to apply a formula to all cells in a selected area, you can use the\<emph/\>         \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Find & Replace\</item\> dialog.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_by_formula.xhp	0	help	par_id3149456	16			0	lt	Select all the desired cells.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_by_formula.xhp	0	help	par_id3148797	17			0	lt	Select the menu command \<emph\>Edit - Find & Replace\</emph\>.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_by_formula.xhp	0	help	par_id3150767	18			0	lt	For the \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Search for\</item\> term, enter: .\<item type=\"literal\"\>*\</item\>            				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_by_formula.xhp	0	help	par_id3153770	19			0	lt	".*" is a regular expression that designates the contents of the current cell.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_by_formula.xhp	0	help	par_id3153143	20			0	lt	Enter the following formula in the \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Replace with\</item\> field: \<item type=\"literal\"\>=&+STYLE(IF(CURRENT()>3;"Red";"Green"))\</item\>            				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_by_formula.xhp	0	help	par_id3146975	21			0	lt	The "&" symbol designates the current contents of the \<emph\>Search for\</emph\> field. The line must begin with an equal sign, since it is a formula. It is assumed that the cell styles "Red" and "Green" already exist.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_by_formula.xhp	0	help	par_id3149262	22			0	lt	Mark the fields \<link href=\"text/shared/01/02100000.xhp\" name=\"Regular expressions\"\>\<emph\>Regular expressions\</emph\>\</link\> and \<emph\>Current selection only\</emph\>. Click \<emph\>Find All\</emph\>.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_by_formula.xhp	0	help	par_id3144767	24			0	lt	All cells with contents that were included in the selection are now highlighted.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_by_formula.xhp	0	help	par_id3147127	23			0	lt	Click\<emph/\>               \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Replace all\</item\>.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	tit				0	lt	Applying Conditional Formatting				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	bm_id3149263				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>conditional formatting; cells\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>cells; conditional formatting\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>formatting; conditional formatting\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>styles;conditional styles\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>cell formats; conditional\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>random numbers;examples\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>cell styles; copying\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>copying; cell styles\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>tables; copying cell styles\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149263	24			0	lt	\<variable id=\"cellstyle_conditional\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/cellstyle_conditional.xhp\" name=\"Applying Conditional Formatting\"\>Applying Conditional Formatting\</link\>\</variable\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	par_id3159156	25			0	lt	Using the menu command \<emph\>Format - Conditional formatting\</emph\>, the dialog allows you to define up to three conditions per cell, which must be met in order for the selected cells to have a particular format.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	par_id8039796				0	lt	To apply conditional formatting, AutoCalculate must be enabled. Choose \<emph\>Tools - Cell Contents - AutoCalculate\</emph\> (you see a check mark next to the command when AutoCalculate is enabled).				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	par_id3154944	26			0	lt	With conditional formatting, you can, for example, highlight the totals that exceed the average value of all totals. If the totals change, the formatting changes correspondingly, without having to apply other styles manually.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	hd_id4480727				0	lt	To Define the Conditions				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	par_id3154490	27			0	lt	Select the cells to which you want to apply a conditional style.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	par_id3155603	28			0	lt	Choose \<emph\>Format - Conditional Formatting\</emph\>.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	par_id3146969	29			0	lt	Enter the condition(s) into the dialog box. The dialog is described in detail in \<link href=\"text/scalc/01/05120000.xhp\" name=\"$[officename] Help\"\>$[officename] Help\</link\>, and an example is provided below:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155766	38			0	lt	Example of Conditional Formatting: Highlighting Totals Above/Under the Average Value				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	hd_id4341868				0	lt	Step1: Generate Number Values				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	par_id3150043	39			0	lt	You want to give certain values in your tables particular emphasis. For example, in a table of turnovers, you can show all the values above the average in green and all those below the average in red. This is possible with conditional formatting.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	par_id3155337	40			0	lt	First of all, write a table in which a few different values occur. For your test you can create tables with any random numbers:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	par_id3149565	41			0	lt	In one of the cells enter the formula =RAND(), and you will obtain a random number between 0 and 1. If you want integers of between 0 and 50, enter the formula =INT(RAND()*50).				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	par_id3149258	42			0	lt	Copy the formula to create a row of random numbers. Click the bottom right corner of the selected cell, and drag to the right until the desired cell range is selected.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	par_id3159236	43			0	lt	In the same way as described above, drag down the corner of the rightmost cell in order to create more rows of random numbers.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149211	44			0	lt	Step 2: Define Cell Styles				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	par_id3154659	45			0	lt	The next step is to apply a cell style to all values that represent above-average turnover, and one to those that are below the average. Ensure that the Styles and Formatting window is visible before proceeding.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	par_id3150883	46			0	lt	Click in a blank cell and select the command \<emph\>Format Cells\</emph\> in the context menu.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	par_id3155529	47			0	lt	In the \<emph\>Format Cells\</emph\> dialog on the \<emph\>Background\</emph\> tab, select a background color. Click \<emph\>OK\</emph\>.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	par_id3154484	48			0	lt	In the Styles and Formatting window, click the \<emph\>New Style from Selection\</emph\> icon. Enter the name of the new style. For this example, name the style "Above".				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	par_id3152889	49			0	lt	To define a second style, click again in a blank cell and proceed as described above. Assign a different background color for the cell and assign a name (for this example, "Below").				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148704	60			0	lt	Step 3: Calculate Average				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	par_id3148837	51			0	lt	In our particular example, we are calculating the average of the random values. The result is placed in a cell:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	par_id3144768	52			0	lt	Set the cursor in a blank cell, for example, J14, and choose \<emph\>Insert - Function\</emph\>.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	par_id3156016	53			0	lt	Select the AVERAGE function. Use the mouse to select all your random numbers. If you cannot see the entire range, because the Function Wizard is obscuring it, you can temporarily shrink the dialog using the \<link href=\"text/shared/00/00000001.xhp#eingabesymbol\" name=\"Shrink or Maximize\"\>\<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Shrink / Maximize\</item\>\</link\> icon.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	par_id3153246	54			0	lt	Close the Function Wizard with \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>OK\</item\>.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149898	50			0	lt	Step 4: Apply Cell Styles				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	par_id3149126	55			0	lt	Now you can apply the conditional formatting to the sheet:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	par_id3150049	56			0	lt	Select all cells with the random numbers.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	par_id3153801	57			0	lt	Choose the \<emph\>Format - Conditional Formatting\</emph\> command to open the corresponding dialog.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	par_id3153013	58			0	lt	Define the condition as follows: If cell value is less than J14, format with cell style "Below", and if cell value is greater than or equal to J14, format with cell style "Above".				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155761	61			0	lt	Step 5: Copy Cell Style				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	par_id3145320	62			0	lt	To apply the conditional formatting to other cells later:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	par_id3153074	63			0	lt	Click one of the cells that has been assigned conditional formatting.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	par_id3149051	64			0	lt	Copy the cell to the clipboard.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	par_id3150436	65			0	lt	Select the cells that are to receive this same formatting.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	par_id3147298	66			0	lt	Choose \<emph\>Edit - Paste Special\</emph\>. The \<emph\>Paste Special\</emph\> dialog appears.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	par_id3166465	67			0	lt	In the \<emph\>Selection\</emph\> area, check only the \<emph\>Formats\</emph\> box. All other boxes must be unchecked. Click \<emph\>OK\</emph\>.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	par_id3159123	68			0	lt	\<link href=\"text/scalc/01/05120000.xhp\" name=\"Format - Conditional formatting\"\>Format - Conditional formatting\</link\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_minusvalue.xhp	0	help	tit				0	lt	Highlighting Negative Numbers				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_minusvalue.xhp	0	help	bm_id3147434				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>negative numbers\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>numbers; highlighting negative numbers\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>highlighting;negative numbers\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>colors;negative numbers\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>number formats;colors for negative numbers\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_minusvalue.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147434	31			0	lt	\<variable id=\"cellstyle_minusvalue\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/cellstyle_minusvalue.xhp\" name=\"Highlighting Negative Numbers\"\>Highlighting Negative Numbers\</link\>\</variable\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_minusvalue.xhp	0	help	par_id3153878	33			0	lt	You can format cells with a number format that highlights negative numbers in red. Alternatively, you can define your own number format in which negative numbers are highlighted in other colors.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_minusvalue.xhp	0	help	par_id3155600	34			0	lt	Select the cells and choose \<emph\>Format - Cells\</emph\>.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_minusvalue.xhp	0	help	par_id3146969	35			0	lt	On the \<emph\>Numbers\</emph\> tab, select a number format and mark \<emph\>Negative numbers red\</emph\> check box. Click \<emph\>OK\</emph\>.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_minusvalue.xhp	0	help	par_id3145640	36			0	lt	The cell number format is defined in two parts. The format for positive numbers and zero is defined in front of the semicolon; after the semicolon the formula for negative numbers is defined. You can change the code (RED) under \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Format code\</item\>. For example, instead of RED, enter \<item type=\"literal\"\>YELLOW\</item\>. If the new code appears in the list after clicking the \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Add\</item\> icon, this is a valid entry.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\consolidate.xhp	0	help	tit				0	lt	Consolidating Data				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\consolidate.xhp	0	help	bm_id3150791				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>consolidating data\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>ranges; combining\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>combining;cell ranges\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>tables; combining\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>data; merging cell ranges\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>merging;data ranges\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\consolidate.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150791	5			0	lt	\<variable id=\"consolidate\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/consolidate.xhp\" name=\"Consolidating Data\"\>Consolidating Data\</link\>\</variable\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\consolidate.xhp	0	help	par_id3153191	34			0	lt	During consolidation, the contents of the cells from several sheets will be combined in one place.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\consolidate.xhp	0	help	hd_id892056				0	lt	To Combine Cell Contents				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\consolidate.xhp	0	help	par_id3151073	6			0	lt	Open the document that contains the cell ranges to be consolidated.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\consolidate.xhp	0	help	par_id3154513	7			0	lt	Choose \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Data - Consolidate\</item\> to open the \<emph\>Consolidate\</emph\> dialog.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\consolidate.xhp	0	help	par_id3147345	8			0	lt	From the \<emph\>Source data area\</emph\> box select a source cell range to consolidate with other areas.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\consolidate.xhp	0	help	par_id3149209	9			0	lt	If the range is not named, click in the field next to the \<emph\>Source data area\</emph\>. A blinking text cursor appears. Type a reference for the first source data range or select the range with the mouse.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\consolidate.xhp	0	help	par_id3155529	10			0	lt	Click \<emph\>Add\</emph\> to insert the selected range in the \<emph\>Consolidation areas\</emph\> field.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\consolidate.xhp	0	help	par_id3153816	11			0	lt	Select additional ranges and click \<emph\>Add\</emph\> after each selection.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\consolidate.xhp	0	help	par_id3157983	12			0	lt	Specify where you want to display the result by selecting a target range from the \<emph\>Copy results to\</emph\> box.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\consolidate.xhp	0	help	par_id3150215	13			0	lt	If the target range is not named, click in the field next to \<emph\>Copy results to\</emph\> and enter the reference of the target range. Alternatively, you can select the range using the mouse or position the cursor in the top left cell of the target range.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\consolidate.xhp	0	help	par_id3153813	14			0	lt	Select a function from the \<emph\>Function\</emph\> box. The function specifies how the values of the consolidation ranges are linked. The "Sum" function is the default setting.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\consolidate.xhp	0	help	par_id3149315	15			0	lt	Click \<emph\>OK\</emph\> to consolidate the ranges.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\consolidate.xhp	0	help	par_idN107DE				0	lt	Additional Settings				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\consolidate.xhp	0	help	par_id3147250	16			0	lt	Click \<emph\>More\</emph\> in the \<emph\>Consolidate\</emph\> dialog to display additional settings:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\consolidate.xhp	0	help	par_id3156400	17			0	lt	Select \<emph\>Link to source data\</emph\> to insert the formulas that generate the results in the target range, rather than the actual results. If you link the data, any values modified in the source range are automatically updated in the target range.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\consolidate.xhp	0	help	par_id3150538	18			0	lt	The corresponding cell references in the target range are inserted in consecutive rows, which are automatically ordered and then hidden from view. Only the final result, based on the selected function, is displayed.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\consolidate.xhp	0	help	par_id3149945	19			0	lt	Under \<emph\>Consolidate by\</emph\>, select either \<emph\>Row labels\</emph\> or \<emph\>Column labels\</emph\> if the cells of the source data range are not to be consolidated corresponding to the identical position of the cell in the range, but instead according to a matching row label or column label.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\consolidate.xhp	0	help	par_id3157871	20			0	lt	To consolidate by row labels or column labels, the label must be contained in the selected source ranges.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\consolidate.xhp	0	help	par_id3150478	21			0	lt	The text in the labels must be identical, so that rows or columns can be accurately matched. If the row or column label does not match any that exist in the target range, it will be appended as a new row or column.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\consolidate.xhp	0	help	par_id3147468	22			0	lt	The data from the consolidation ranges and target range will be saved when you save the document. If you later open a document in which consolidation has been defined, this data will again be available.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\consolidate.xhp	0	help	par_id3153039	33			0	lt	\<link href=\"text/scalc/01/12070000.xhp\" name=\"Data - Consolidate\"\>Data - Consolidate\</link\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\csv_files.xhp	0	help	tit				0	lt	 Importing and Exporting CSV Files 				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\csv_files.xhp	0	help	bm_id892361				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>number series import\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>data series import\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>exporting; tables as text\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>importing; tables as text\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>delimited values and files\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>comma separated files and values\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>text file import and export\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>csv files;importing and exporting\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>tables; importing/exporting as text\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>text documents; importing to spreadsheets\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>opening;text csv files\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>saving;as text csv\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\csv_files.xhp	0	help	par_idN10862				0	lt	\<variable id=\"csv_files\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/csv_files.xhp\"\>Opening and Saving Text CSV Files\</link\>\</variable\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\csv_files.xhp	0	help	par_idN10880				0	lt	Comma Separated Values (CSV) is a text file format that you can use to exchange data from a database or a spreadsheet between applications. Each line in a Text CSV file represents a record in the database, or a row in a spreadsheet. Each field in a database record or cell in a spreadsheet row is usually separated by a comma. However, you can use other characters to delimit a field, such as a tabulator character.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\csv_files.xhp	0	help	par_idN10886				0	lt	If the field or cell contains a comma, the field or cell \<emph\>must\</emph\> be enclosed by single quotes (') or double quotes (").				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\csv_files.xhp	0	help	par_idN10890				0	lt	To Open a Text CSV File in Calc				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\csv_files.xhp	0	help	par_idN10897				0	lt	Choose \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>File - Open\</item\>.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\csv_files.xhp	0	help	par_idN1089F				0	lt	Locate the CSV file that you want to open.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\csv_files.xhp	0	help	par_idN108A2				0	lt	If the file has a *.csv extension, select the file.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\csv_files.xhp	0	help	par_idN108A5				0	lt	If the CSV file has another extension, select the file, and then select "Text CSV" in the \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>File type\</item\> box				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\csv_files.xhp	0	help	par_idN1082D				0	lt	Click \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Open\</item\>.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\csv_files.xhp	0	help	par_idN10834				0	lt	The \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Text Import\</item\> dialog opens.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\csv_files.xhp	0	help	par_idN108B1				0	lt	Specify the options to divide the text in the file into columns.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\csv_files.xhp	0	help	par_idN108BB				0	lt	You can preview the layout of the imported data at the bottom of the \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Text Import\</item\> dialog. 				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\csv_files.xhp	0	help	par_id8444166				0	lt	Right-click a column in the preview to set the format or to hide the column.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\csv_files.xhp	0	help	par_idN108E2				0	lt	Check the text delimiter box that matches the character used as text delimiter in the file. In case of an unlisted delimiter, type the character into the input box.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\csv_files.xhp	0	help	par_idN108C5				0	lt	Click \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>OK\</item\>.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\csv_files.xhp	0	help	par_idN108FA				0	lt	To Save a Sheet as a Text CSV File				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\csv_files.xhp	0	help	par_idN106FC				0	lt	When you export a spreadsheet to CSV format, only the data on the current sheet is saved. All other information, including formulas and formatting, is lost.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\csv_files.xhp	0	help	par_idN10901				0	lt	Open the Calc sheet that you want to save as a Text CSV file.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\csv_files.xhp	0	help	par_idN107AF				0	lt	Only the current sheet can be exported.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\csv_files.xhp	0	help	par_idN10905				0	lt	Choose \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>File - Save as\</item\>.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\csv_files.xhp	0	help	par_idN10915				0	lt	In the \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>File name\</item\> box, enter a name for the file.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\csv_files.xhp	0	help	par_idN1090D				0	lt	In the \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>File type\</item\> box, select "Text CSV".				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\csv_files.xhp	0	help	par_idN107DD				0	lt	(Optional) Set the field options for the Text CSV file.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\csv_files.xhp	0	help	par_idN1091C				0	lt	Select \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Edit filter settings\</item\>.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\csv_files.xhp	0	help	par_idN107ED				0	lt	In the \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Export of text files\</item\> dialog, select the options that you want.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\csv_files.xhp	0	help	par_idN107F4				0	lt	Click \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>OK\</item\>.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\csv_files.xhp	0	help	par_idN107FC				0	lt	Click \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Save\</item\>.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\csv_files.xhp	0	help	par_id3153487	20			0	lt	\<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>%PRODUCTNAME - Preferences\</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Tools - Options\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\> - \<link href=\"text/shared/optionen/01060100.xhp\" name=\"Spreadsheet - View\"\>%PRODUCTNAME Calc - View\</link\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\csv_files.xhp	0	help	par_id3153008	21			0	lt	\<link href=\"text/shared/00/00000207.xhp\" name=\"Export text files\"\>Export text files\</link\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\csv_files.xhp	0	help	par_id3155595	22			0	lt	\<link href=\"text/shared/00/00000208.xhp\" name=\"Import text files\"\>Import text files\</link\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\csv_formula.xhp	0	help	tit				0	lt	Importing and Exporting Text Files				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\csv_formula.xhp	0	help	bm_id3153726				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>csv files;formulas\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>formulas; importing/exporting as csv files\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>exporting;formulas as csv files\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>importing;csv files with formulas\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\csv_formula.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153726	1			0	lt	\<variable id=\"csv_formula\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/csv_formula.xhp\" name=\"Importing and Exporting Text Files\"\>Importing and Exporting CSV Text Files with Formulas\</link\>\</variable\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\csv_formula.xhp	0	help	par_id3149402	2			0	lt	Comma separated values (CSV) files are text files that contain the cell contents of a single sheet. Commas, semicolons, or other characters can be used as the field delimiters between the cells. Text strings are put in quotation marks, numbers are written without quotation marks.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\csv_formula.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150715	15			0	lt	To Import a CSV File				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\csv_formula.xhp	0	help	par_id3153709	16			0	lt	Choose \<emph\>File - Open\</emph\>.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\csv_formula.xhp	0	help	par_id3155445	17			0	lt	In the \<emph\>File type\</emph\> field, select the format "Text CSV". Select the file and click \<emph\>Open\</emph\>. When a file has the .csv extension, the file type is automatically recognized.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\csv_formula.xhp	0	help	par_id3149565	18			0	lt	You will see the \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Text Import\</item\> dialog. Click \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>OK\</item\>.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\csv_formula.xhp	0	help	par_id3149255	19			0	lt	If the csv file contains formulas, but you want to import the results of those formulas, then choose \<emph\>\<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>%PRODUCTNAME - Preferences\</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Tools - Options\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\> - %PRODUCTNAME Calc - View\</emph\> and clear the \<emph\>Formulas\</emph\> check box.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\csv_formula.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154022	3			0	lt	To Export Formulas and Values as CSV Files				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\csv_formula.xhp	0	help	par_id3150342	4			0	lt	Click the sheet to be written as a csv file.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\csv_formula.xhp	0	help	par_id3166423	5			0	lt	If you want to export the formulas as formulas, for example, in the form =SUM(A1:B5), proceed as follows:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\csv_formula.xhp	0	help	par_id3155111	6			0	lt	Choose \<emph\>\<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>%PRODUCTNAME - Preferences\</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Tools - Options\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\> - %PRODUCTNAME Calc - View\</emph\>.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\csv_formula.xhp	0	help	par_id3150200	7			0	lt	Under \<emph\>Display\</emph\>, mark the \<emph\>Formulas\</emph\> check box. Click \<emph\>OK\</emph\>.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\csv_formula.xhp	0	help	par_id3154484	8			0	lt	If you want to export the calculation results instead of the formulas, do not mark \<emph\>Formulas\</emph\>.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\csv_formula.xhp	0	help	par_id3148702	9			0	lt	Choose \<emph\>File - Save as\</emph\>. You will see the \<emph\>Save as\</emph\> dialog.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\csv_formula.xhp	0	help	par_id3153912	10			0	lt	In the\<emph/\>               \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>File type\</item\> field select the format "Text CSV".				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\csv_formula.xhp	0	help	par_id3157978	13			0	lt	Enter a name and click \<emph\>Save\</emph\>.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\csv_formula.xhp	0	help	par_id3152869	23			0	lt	From the \<emph\>Export of text files\</emph\> dialog that appears, select the character set and the field and text delimiters for the data to be exported, and confirm with \<emph\>OK\</emph\>.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\csv_formula.xhp	0	help	par_id3146781	12			0	lt	If the numbers use commas as decimal separators or thousands separators, do not select the comma as the field delimiter. If the text contains double quotation marks, you must select the single quotation mark as separator.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\csv_formula.xhp	0	help	par_id3150050	14			0	lt	If necessary, after you have saved, clear the \<emph\>Formulas\</emph\> check box to see the calculated results in the table again.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\csv_formula.xhp	0	help	par_id3153487	20			0	lt	\<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>%PRODUCTNAME - Preferences\</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Tools - Options\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\> - \<link href=\"text/shared/optionen/01060100.xhp\" name=\"Spreadsheet - View\"\>%PRODUCTNAME Calc - View\</link\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\csv_formula.xhp	0	help	par_id3153008	21			0	lt	\<link href=\"text/shared/00/00000207.xhp\" name=\"Export text files\"\>Export text files\</link\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\csv_formula.xhp	0	help	par_id3155595	22			0	lt	\<link href=\"text/shared/00/00000208.xhp\" name=\"Import text files\"\>Import text files\</link\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\currency_format.xhp	0	help	tit				0	lt	Cells in Currency Format				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\currency_format.xhp	0	help	bm_id3156329				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>currency formats; spreadsheets\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>cells; currency formats\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>international currency formats\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>formats; currency formats in cells\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>currencies; default currencies\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>defaults;currency formats\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>changing;currency formats\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\currency_format.xhp	0	help	hd_id3156329	46			0	lt	\<variable id=\"currency_format\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/currency_format.xhp\" name=\"Cells in Currency Format\"\>Cells in Currency Format\</link\>\</variable\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\currency_format.xhp	0	help	par_id3153968	47			0	lt	In \<item type=\"productname\"\>%PRODUCTNAME\</item\> Calc you can give numbers any currency format. When you click the \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Currency\</item\> icon \<image id=\"img_id3150791\" src=\"res/commandimagelist/sc_currencyfield.png\" width=\"0.1665in\" height=\"0.1665in\"\>\<alt id=\"alt_id3150791\"\>Icon\</alt\>\</image\> in the \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Formatting\</item\> bar to format a number, the cell is given the default currency format set under \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>\<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>%PRODUCTNAME - Preferences\</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Tools - Options\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\> - Language Settings - Languages\</item\>.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\currency_format.xhp	0	help	par_id3150010	48			0	lt	Exchanging of \<item type=\"productname\"\>%PRODUCTNAME\</item\> Calc documents can lead to misunderstandings, if your \<item type=\"productname\"\>%PRODUCTNAME\</item\> Calc document is loaded by a user who uses a different default currency format.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\currency_format.xhp	0	help	par_id3156442	52			0	lt	In \<item type=\"productname\"\>%PRODUCTNAME\</item\> Calc you can define that a number that you have formatted as "1,234.50 €", still remains in euros in another country and does not become dollars.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\currency_format.xhp	0	help	par_id3151075	49			0	lt	You can change the currency format in the \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Format Cells\</item\> dialog (choose \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Format - Cells - Numbers\</item\> tab) by two country settings. In the \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Language\</item\> combo box select the basic setting for decimal and thousands separators. In the \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Format\</item\> list box you can select the currency symbol and its position.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\currency_format.xhp	0	help	par_id3150749	50			0	lt	For example, if the language is set to "Default" and you are using a german locale setting, the currency format will be "1.234,00 €". A point is used before the thousand digits and a comma before the decimal places. If you now select the subordinate currency format "$ English (US)" from the \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Format\</item\> list box , you will get the following format: "$ 1.234,00". As you can see, the separators have remained the same. Only the currency symbol has been changed and converted, but the underlying format of the notation remains the same as in the locale setting.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\currency_format.xhp	0	help	par_id3145640	51			0	lt	If, under \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Language\</item\>, you convert the cells to "English (US)", the English-language locale setting is also transferred and the default currency format is now "$ 1,234.00".				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\currency_format.xhp	0	help	par_id3154255	53			0	lt	\<link href=\"text/shared/01/05020300.xhp\" name=\"Format - Cells - Numbers\"\>Format - Cells - Numbers\</link\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_define.xhp	0	help	tit				0	lt	Defining Database Ranges				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_define.xhp	0	help	bm_id3154758				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>tables; database ranges\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>database ranges; defining\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>ranges; defining database ranges\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>defining;database ranges\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_define.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154758	31			0	lt	\<variable id=\"database_define\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/database_define.xhp\" name=\"Defining Database Ranges\"\>Defining a Database Range\</link\>\</variable\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_define.xhp	0	help	par_id3153768	81			0	lt	You can define a range of cells in a spreadsheet to use as a database. Each row in this database range corresponds to a database record and each cell in a row corresponds to a database field. You can sort, group, search, and perform calculations on the range as you would in a database.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_define.xhp	0	help	par_id3145801	82			0	lt	You can only edit and access a database range in the spreadsheet that contains the range. You cannot access the database range in the %PRODUCTNAME Data Sources view. 				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_define.xhp	0	help	par_idN10648				0	lt	To define a database range				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_define.xhp	0	help	par_id3155064	41			0	lt	Select the range of cells that you want to define as a database range.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_define.xhp	0	help	par_idN10654				0	lt	Choose \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Data - Define Range\</item\>.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_define.xhp	0	help	par_id3153715	72			0	lt	In the \<emph\>Name\</emph\> box, enter a name for the database range.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_define.xhp	0	help	par_idN1066A				0	lt	Click \<emph\>More\</emph\>.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_define.xhp	0	help	par_id3154253	42			0	lt	Specify the options for the database range.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_define.xhp	0	help	par_idN10675				0	lt	Click \<emph\>OK\</emph\>.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_filter.xhp	0	help	tit				0	lt	Filtering Cell Ranges 				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_filter.xhp	0	help	bm_id3153541				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>cell ranges;applying/removing filters\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>filtering;cell ranges/database ranges\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>database ranges;applying/removing filters\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>removing;cell range filters\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_filter.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153541	47			0	lt	\<variable id=\"database_filter\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/database_filter.xhp\" name=\"Filtering Cell Ranges\"\>Filtering Cell Ranges\</link\>\</variable\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_filter.xhp	0	help	par_id3145069	48			0	lt	You can use several filters to filter cell ranges in spreadsheets. A standard filter uses the options that you specify to filter the data. An AutoFilter filters data according to a specific value or string. An advanced filter uses filter criteria from specified cells.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_filter.xhp	0	help	par_idN10682				0	lt	To Apply a Standard Filter to a Cell Range				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_filter.xhp	0	help	par_id3150398	50			0	lt	Click in a cell range.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_filter.xhp	0	help	par_idN10693				0	lt	Choose \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Data - Filter - Standard Filter\</item\>.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_filter.xhp	0	help	par_id3156422	51			0	lt	In the \<emph\>Standard Filter\</emph\> dialog, specify the filter options that you want.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_filter.xhp	0	help	par_idN106A5				0	lt	Click \<emph\>OK\</emph\>.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_filter.xhp	0	help	par_id3153143	52			0	lt	The records that match the filter options that you specified are shown.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_filter.xhp	0	help	par_id3153728	53			0	lt	To Apply an AutoFilter to a Cell Range				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_filter.xhp	0	help	par_id3144764	54			0	lt	Click in a cell range or a database range.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_filter.xhp	0	help	par_id9303872				0	lt	If you want to apply multiple AutoFilters to the same sheet, you must first define database ranges, then apply the AutoFilters to the database ranges.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_filter.xhp	0	help	par_id3154944	55			0	lt	Choose \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Data - Filter - AutoFilter\</item\>.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_filter.xhp	0	help	par_idN106DB				0	lt	An arrow button is added to the head of each column in the database range.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_filter.xhp	0	help	par_id3153878	56			0	lt	Click the arrow button in the column that contains the value or string that you want to set as the filter criteria.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_filter.xhp	0	help	par_idN10749				0	lt	Select the value or string that you want to use as the filter criteria.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_filter.xhp	0	help	par_idN1074C				0	lt	The records that match the filter criteria that you selected are shown.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_filter.xhp	0	help	par_idN106E8				0	lt	To Remove a Filter From a Cell Range				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_filter.xhp	0	help	par_idN1075C				0	lt	Click in a filtered cell range.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_filter.xhp	0	help	par_idN106EC				0	lt	Choose \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Data - Filter - Remove Filter\</item\>.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_filter.xhp	0	help	par_id4525284				0	lt	\<link href=\"http://wiki.services.openoffice.org/wiki/Documentation/How_Tos/Defining_a_Data_Range\"\>Wiki page about defining a data range\</link\>				20130618 17:22:18
