helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3150312	29			0	lt	Select a single column range in which to enter the frequency according to the class limits. You must select one field more than the class ceiling. In this example, select the range C1:C6. Call up the FREQUENCY function in the \<emph\>Function Wizard\</emph\>. Select the \<emph\>Data\</emph\> range in (A1:A11), and then the \<emph\>Classes\</emph\> range in which you entered the class limits (B1:B6). Select the \<emph\>Array\</emph\> check box and click \<emph\>OK\</emph\>. You will see the frequency count in the range C1:C6.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_idN11269				0	lt	\<embedvar href=\"text/scalc/00/00000004.xhp#moreontop\"/\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	bm_id3151030				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>MDETERM function\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>determinants\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	hd_id3151030	31			0	lt	MDETERM				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3154073	32			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_MDET\"\>Returns the array determinant of an array.\</ahelp\> This function returns a value in the current cell; it is not necessary to define a range for the results.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	hd_id3156366	33			0	lt	Syntax				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3156380	34			0	lt	MDETERM(Array)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3150290	35			0	lt	\<emph\>Array\</emph\> represents a square array in which the determinants are defined.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_idN11035				0	lt	You can find a general introduction to using Array functions on top of this page.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_idN11333				0	lt	\<embedvar href=\"text/scalc/00/00000004.xhp#moreontop\"/\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	bm_id3151348				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>MINVERSE function\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>inverse arrays\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	hd_id3151348	39			0	lt	MINVERSE				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3145569	40			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_MINV\"\>Returns the inverse array.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	hd_id3156072	41			0	lt	Syntax				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3156085	42			0	lt	MINVERSE(Array)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3157849	43			0	lt	\<emph\>Array\</emph\> represents a square array that is to be inverted.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_idN113EE				0	lt	\<embedvar href=\"text/scalc/00/00000004.xhp#moreontop\"/\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	hd_id3157868	44			0	lt	Example				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3149638	45			0	lt	Select a square range and select MINVERSE. Select the output array, select the \<emph\>Array\</emph\> field and click \<emph\>OK\</emph\>.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	bm_id3148546				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>MMULT function\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148546	47			0	lt	MMULT				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3148518	48			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_MMULT\"\>Calculates the array product of two arrays.\</ahelp\> The number of columns for array 1 must match the number of rows for array 2. The square array has an equal number of rows and columns.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	hd_id3146767	49			0	lt	Syntax				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3150798	50			0	lt	MMULT(Array; Array)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3150812	51			0	lt	\<emph\>Array\</emph\> at first place represents the first array used in the array product.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3152553	52			0	lt	\<emph\>Array\</emph\> at second place represents the second array with the same number of rows.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_idN114C3				0	lt	\<embedvar href=\"text/scalc/00/00000004.xhp#moreontop\"/\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	hd_id3152574	53			0	lt	Example				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3146826	54			0	lt	Select a square range. Choose the MMULT function. Select the first \<emph\>Array\</emph\>, then select the second \<emph\>Array\</emph\>. Using \<emph\>Function Wizard\</emph\>, mark the \<emph\>Array\</emph\> check box. Click \<emph\>OK\</emph\>. The output array will appear in the first selected range.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	bm_id3154970				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>TRANSPOSE function\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154970	56			0	lt	TRANSPOSE				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3155276	57			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_MTRANS\"\>Transposes the rows and columns of an array.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155294	58			0	lt	Syntax				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3153843	59			0	lt	TRANSPOSE(Array)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3153857	60			0	lt	\<emph\>Array\</emph\> represents the array in the spreadsheet that is to be transposed.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_idN115A5				0	lt	\<embedvar href=\"text/scalc/00/00000004.xhp#moreontop\"/\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	hd_id3159352	61			0	lt	Example				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3159366	62			0	lt	In the spreadsheet, select the range in which the transposed array can appear. If the original array has n rows and m columns, your selected range must have at least m rows and n columns. Then enter the formula directly, select the original array and press \<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>Shift+Command+Enter\</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Shift+Ctrl+Enter\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\>. Or, if you are using the \<emph\>Function Wizard\</emph\>, mark the \<emph\>Array\</emph\> check box. The transposed array appears in the selected target range and is protected automatically against changes.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	bm_id3109846				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>LINEST function\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	hd_id3109846	64			0	lt	LINEST				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3144733	65			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_RGP\"\>Returns a table of statistics for a straight line that best fits a data set.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	hd_id3152825	66			0	lt	Syntax				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3152839	67			0	lt	LINEST(data_Y; data_X; linearType; stats)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3152853	68			0	lt	\<emph\>data_Y\</emph\> is a single row or column range specifying the y coordinates in a set of data points.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3154428	69			0	lt	\<emph\>data_X\</emph\> is a corresponding single row or column range specifying the x coordinates. If \<emph\>data_X\</emph\> is omitted it defaults to \<item type=\"literal\"\>1, 2, 3, ..., n\</item\>. If there is more than one set of variables \<emph\>data_X\</emph\> may be a range with corresponding multiple rows or columns.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id0811200804502119				0	lt	LINEST finds a straight line \<item type=\"literal\"\>y = a + bx\</item\> that best fits the data, using linear regression (the "least squares" method). With more than one set of variables the straight line is of the form \<item type=\"literal\"\>y = a + b1x1 + b2x2 ... + bnxn\</item\>.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3154448	70			0	lt	 if\<emph\>linearType\</emph\> is FALSE the straight line found is forced to pass through the origin (the constant a is zero; y = bx). If omitted, \<emph\>linearType\</emph\> defaults to TRUE (the line is not forced through the origin).				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3154142	71			0	lt	if\<emph\>stats\</emph\> is omitted or FALSE only the top line of the statistics table is returned. If TRUE the entire table is returned.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id0811200804502261				0	lt	LINEST returns a table (array) of statistics as below and must be entered as an array formula (for example by using \<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>Command\</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Ctrl\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\>+Shift+Return rather than just Return).				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_idN11416				0	lt	\<embedvar href=\"text/scalc/00/00000004.xhp#optional\"/\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_idN116C6				0	lt	\<embedvar href=\"text/scalc/00/00000004.xhp#moreontop\"/\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154162	72			0	lt	Example				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3154176	73			0	lt	This function returns an array and is handled in the same way as the other array functions. Select a range for the answers and then the function. Select \<emph\>data_Y\</emph\>. If you want, you can enter other parameters. Select \<emph\>Array\</emph\> and click \<emph\>OK\</emph\>.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3155468	74			0	lt	The results returned by the system (if \<emph\>stats\</emph\> = 0), will at least show the slope of the regression line and its intersection with the Y axis. If \<emph\>stats\</emph\> does not equal 0, other results are to be displayed.				20130618 17:22:18
