helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060118.xhp	0	help	par_id3153790	186			0	lt	COUPPCD(Settlement; Maturity; Frequency; Basis)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060118.xhp	0	help	par_id3150989	187			0	lt	\<emph\>Settlement\</emph\> is the date of purchase of the security.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060118.xhp	0	help	par_id3154667	188			0	lt	\<emph\>Maturity\</emph\> is the date on which the security matures (expires).				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060118.xhp	0	help	par_id3154569	189			0	lt	\<emph\>Frequency\</emph\> is the number of interest payments per year (1, 2 or 4).				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060118.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150826	190			0	lt	Example				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060118.xhp	0	help	par_id3148968	191			0	lt	A security is purchased on 2001-01-25; the date of maturity is 2001-11-15. Interest is paid half-yearly (frequency is 2). Using daily balance interest calculation (basis 3) what was the interest date prior to purchase?				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060118.xhp	0	help	par_id3149992	192			0	lt	=COUPPCD("2001-01-25"; "2001-11-15"; 2; 3) returns 2000-15-11.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060118.xhp	0	help	bm_id3150673				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>COUPNUM function\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>number of coupons\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060118.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150673	173			0	lt	COUPNUM				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060118.xhp	0	help	par_id3154350	174			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_AAI_FUNC_COUPNUM\"\>Returns the number of coupons (interest payments) between the settlement date and the maturity date.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060118.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148400	175			0	lt	Syntax				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060118.xhp	0	help	par_id3153200	176			0	lt	COUPNUM(Settlement; Maturity; Frequency; Basis)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060118.xhp	0	help	par_id3159406	177			0	lt	\<emph\>Settlement\</emph\> is the date of purchase of the security.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060118.xhp	0	help	par_id3155864	178			0	lt	\<emph\>Maturity\</emph\> is the date on which the security matures (expires).				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060118.xhp	0	help	par_id3154720	179			0	lt	\<emph\>Frequency\</emph\> is the number of interest payments per year (1, 2 or 4).				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060118.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149319	180			0	lt	Example				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060118.xhp	0	help	par_id3152460	181			0	lt	A security is purchased on 2001-01-25; the date of maturity is 2001-11-15. Interest is paid half-yearly (frequency is 2). Using daily balance interest calculation (basis 3) how many interest dates are there?				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060118.xhp	0	help	par_id3150640	182			0	lt	=COUPNUM("2001-01-25"; "2001-11-15"; 2; 3) returns 2.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060118.xhp	0	help	bm_id3149339				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>IPMT function\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>periodic amortizement rates\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060118.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149339	263			0	lt	IPMT				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060118.xhp	0	help	par_id3154522	264			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_ZINSZ\"\>Calculates the periodic amortizement for an investment with regular payments and a constant interest rate.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060118.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153266	265			0	lt	Syntax				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060118.xhp	0	help	par_id3151283	266			0	lt	IPMT(Rate; Period; NPer; PV; FV; Type)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060118.xhp	0	help	par_id3147313	267			0	lt	\<emph\>Rate\</emph\> is the periodic interest rate.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060118.xhp	0	help	par_id3145158	268			0	lt	\<emph\>Period\</emph\> is the period, for which the compound interest is calculated. Period=NPER if compound interest for the last period is calculated.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060118.xhp	0	help	par_id3147577	269			0	lt	\<emph\>NPer\</emph\> is the total number of periods, during which annuity is paid.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060118.xhp	0	help	par_id3156211	270			0	lt	\<emph\>PV\</emph\> is the present cash value in sequence of payments.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060118.xhp	0	help	par_id3151213	271			0	lt	\<emph\>FV\</emph\> (optional) is the desired value (future value) at the end of the periods.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060118.xhp	0	help	par_id3154195	272			0	lt	\<emph\>Type\</emph\> is the due date for the periodic payments.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060118.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150102	273			0	lt	Example				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060118.xhp	0	help	par_id3149438	274			0	lt	What is the interest rate during the fifth period (year) if the constant interest rate is 5% and the cash value is 15,000 currency units? The periodic payment is seven years.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060118.xhp	0	help	par_id3150496	275			0	lt	\<item type=\"input\"\>=IPMT(5%;5;7;15000)\</item\> = -352.97 currency units. The compound interest during the fifth period (year) is 352.97 currency units.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060118.xhp	0	help	bm_id3151205				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>calculating;future values\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>future values;constant interest rates\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>FV function\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060118.xhp	0	help	hd_id3151205	277			0	lt	FV				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060118.xhp	0	help	par_id3154140	278			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_ZW\"\>Returns the future value of an investment based on periodic, constant payments and a constant interest rate (Future Value).\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060118.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155178	279			0	lt	Syntax				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060118.xhp	0	help	par_id3145215	280			0	lt	FV(Rate; NPer; Pmt; PV; Type)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060118.xhp	0	help	par_id3155136	281			0	lt	\<emph\>Rate\</emph\> is the periodic interest rate.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060118.xhp	0	help	par_id3156029	282			0	lt	\<emph\>NPer\</emph\> is the total number of periods (payment period).				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060118.xhp	0	help	par_id3151322	283			0	lt	\<emph\>Pmt\</emph\> is the annuity paid regularly per period.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060118.xhp	0	help	par_id3145256	284			0	lt	\<emph\>PV\</emph\> (optional) is the (present) cash value of an investment.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060118.xhp	0	help	par_id3150999	285			0	lt	\<emph\>Type\</emph\> (optional) defines whether the payment is due at the beginning or the end of a period.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060118.xhp	0	help	par_idN114D8				0	lt	\<embedvar href=\"text/scalc/00/00000004.xhp#optional\"/\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060118.xhp	0	help	hd_id3146800	286			0	lt	Example				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060118.xhp	0	help	par_id3146813	287			0	lt	What is the value at the end of an investment if the interest rate is 4% and the payment period is two years, with a periodic payment of 750 currency units. The investment has a present value of 2,500 currency units.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060118.xhp	0	help	par_id3149302	288			0	lt	\<item type=\"input\"\>=FV(4%;2;750;2500) \</item\>= -4234.00 currency units. The value at the end of the investment is 4234.00 currency units.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060118.xhp	0	help	bm_id3155912				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>FVSCHEDULE function\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>future values;varying interest rates\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060118.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155912	51			0	lt	FVSCHEDULE				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060118.xhp	0	help	par_id3163726	52			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_AAI_FUNC_FVSCHEDULE\"\>Calculates the accumulated value of the starting capital for a series of periodically varying interest rates.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060118.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149571	53			0	lt	Syntax				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060118.xhp	0	help	par_id3148891	54			0	lt	FVSCHEDULE(Principal; Schedule)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060118.xhp	0	help	par_id3148904	55			0	lt	\<emph\>Principal\</emph\> is the starting capital.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060118.xhp	0	help	par_id3148562	56			0	lt	\<emph\>Schedule\</emph\> is a series of interest rates, for example, as a range H3:H5 or as a (List) (see example).				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060118.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147288	57			0	lt	Example				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060118.xhp	0	help	par_id3148638	58			0	lt	1000 currency units have been invested in for three years. The interest rates were 3%, 4% and 5% per annum. What is the value after three years?				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060118.xhp	0	help	par_id3156358	59			0	lt	\<item type=\"input\"\>=FVSCHEDULE(1000;{0.03;0.04;0.05})\</item\> returns 1124.76.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060118.xhp	0	help	bm_id3156435				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>calculating;number of payment periods\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>payment periods;number of\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>number of payment periods\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>NPER function\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060118.xhp	0	help	hd_id3156435	290			0	lt	NPER				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060118.xhp	0	help	par_id3152363	291			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_ZZR\"\>Returns the number of periods for an investment based on periodic, constant payments and a constant interest rate.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060118.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147216	292			0	lt	Syntax				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060118.xhp	0	help	par_id3155934	293			0	lt	NPER(Rate; Pmt; PV; FV; Type)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060118.xhp	0	help	par_id3155946	294			0	lt	\<emph\>Rate\</emph\> is the periodic interest rate.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060118.xhp	0	help	par_id3149042	295			0	lt	\<emph\>Pmt\</emph\> is the constant annuity paid in each period.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060118.xhp	0	help	par_id3153134	296			0	lt	\<emph\>PV\</emph\> is the present value (cash value) in a sequence of payments.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060118.xhp	0	help	par_id3154398	297			0	lt	\<emph\>FV\</emph\> (optional) is the future value, which is reached at the end of the last period.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060118.xhp	0	help	par_id3145127	298			0	lt	\<emph\>Type\</emph\> (optional) is the due date of the payment at the beginning or at the end of the period.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060118.xhp	0	help	par_idN1166C				0	lt	\<embedvar href=\"text/scalc/00/00000004.xhp#optional\"/\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060118.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155795	299			0	lt	Example				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060118.xhp	0	help	par_id3147378	300			0	lt	How many payment periods does a payment period cover with a periodic interest rate of 6%, a periodic payment of 153.75 currency units and a present cash value of 2.600 currency units.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060118.xhp	0	help	par_id3156171	301			0	lt	\<item type=\"input\"\>=NPER(6%;153.75;2600)\</item\> = -12,02. The payment period covers 12.02 periods.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060118.xhp	0	help	par_id3150309	314			0	lt	\<link href=\"text/scalc/01/04060103.xhp\" name=\"Back to Financial Functions Part One\"\>Back to Financial Functions Part One\</link\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060118.xhp	0	help	par_id3153163	315			0	lt	\<link href=\"text/scalc/01/04060119.xhp\" name=\"Back to Financial Functions Part Two\"\>Back to Financial Functions Part Two\</link\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	tit				0	lt	Financial Functions Part Two				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149052	1			0	lt	Financial Functions Part Two				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3148742	343			0	lt	\<link href=\"text/scalc/01/04060103.xhp\" name=\"Back to Financial Functions Part One\"\>Back to Financial Functions Part One\</link\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3151341	344			0	lt	\<link href=\"text/scalc/01/04060118.xhp\" name=\"Forward to Financial Functions Part Three\"\>Forward to Financial Functions Part Three\</link\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	bm_id3150026				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>PPMT function\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150026	238			0	lt	PPMT				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3146942	239			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_KAPZ\"\>Returns for a given period the payment on the principal for an investment that is based on periodic and constant payments and a constant interest rate.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150459	240			0	lt	Syntax				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3146878	241			0	lt	PPMT(Rate; Period; NPer; PV; FV; Type)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3151228	242			0	lt	\<emph\>Rate\</emph\> is the periodic interest rate.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3148887	243			0	lt	\<emph\>Period\</emph\> is the amortizement period. P = 1 for the first and P = NPer for the last period.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3148436	244			0	lt	\<emph\>NPer\</emph\> is the total number of periods during which annuity is paid.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3153035	245			0	lt	\<emph\>PV\</emph\> is the present value in the sequence of payments.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3147474	246			0	lt	\<emph\>FV\</emph\> (optional) is the desired (future) value.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3144744	247			0	lt	\<emph\>Type\</emph\> (optional) defines the due date. F = 1 for payment at the beginning of a period and F = 0 for payment at the end of a period.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_idN1067C				0	lt	\<embedvar href=\"text/scalc/00/00000004.xhp#optional\"/\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148582	248			0	lt	Example				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3154811	249			0	lt	How high is the periodic monthly payment at an annual interest rate of 8.75% over a period of 3 years? The cash value is 5,000 currency units and is always paid at the beginning of a period. The future value is 8,000 currency units.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3149246	250			0	lt	\<item type=\"input\"\>=PPMT(8.75%/12;1;36;5000;8000;1)\</item\> = -350.99 currency units.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	bm_id3146139				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>calculating; total amortizement rates\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>total amortizement rates\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>amortization installment\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>repayment installment\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>CUMPRINC function\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	hd_id3146139	252			0	lt	CUMPRINC				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3150140	253			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_KUMKAPITAL\"\>Returns the cumulative interest paid for an investment period with a constant interest rate.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149188	254			0	lt	Syntax				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3148733	255			0	lt	CUMPRINC(Rate; NPer; PV; S; E; Type)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3150864	256			0	lt	\<emph\>Rate\</emph\> is the periodic interest rate.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3166052	257			0	lt	\<emph\>NPer\</emph\> is the payment period with the total number of periods. NPER can also be a non-integer value.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3150007	258			0	lt	\<emph\>PV\</emph\> is the current value in the sequence of payments.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3153112	259			0	lt	\<emph\>S\</emph\> is the first period.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3146847	260			0	lt	\<emph\>E\</emph\> is the last period.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3145167	261			0	lt	\<emph\>Type\</emph\> is the due date of the payment at the beginning or end of each period.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154502	262			0	lt	Example				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3153570	263			0	lt	What are the payoff amounts if the yearly interest rate is 5.5% for 36 months? The cash value is 15,000 currency units. The payoff amount is calculated between the 10th and 18th period. The due date is at the end of the period.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3149884	264			0	lt	\<item type=\"input\"\>=CUMPRINC(5.5%/12;36;15000;10;18;0)\</item\> = -3669.74 currency units. The payoff amount between the 10th and 18th period is 3669.74 currency units.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	bm_id3150019				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>CUMPRINC_ADD function\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150019	182			0	lt	CUMPRINC_ADD				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3145246	183			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_AAI_FUNC_CUMPRINC\"\> Calculates the cumulative redemption of a loan in a period.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153047	184			0	lt	Syntax				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3157970	185			0	lt	CUMPRINC_ADD(Rate; NPer; PV; StartPeriod; EndPeriod; Type)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3145302	186			0	lt	\<emph\>Rate\</emph\> is the interest rate for each period.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3151017	187			0	lt	\<emph\>NPer\</emph\> is the total number of payment periods. The rate and NPER must refer to the same unit, and thus both be calculated annually or monthly.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3155620	188			0	lt	\<emph\>PV\</emph\> is the current value.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3145352	189			0	lt	\<emph\>StartPeriod\</emph\> is the first payment period for the calculation.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3157986	190			0	lt	\<emph\>EndPeriod\</emph\> is the last payment period for the calculation.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3150570	191			0	lt	\<emph\>Type\</emph\> is the maturity of a payment at the end of each period (Type = 0) or at the start of the period (Type = 1).				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150269	192			0	lt	Example				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3148774	193			0	lt	The following mortgage loan is taken out on a house:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3150661	194			0	lt	Rate: 9.00 per cent per annum (9% / 12 = 0.0075), Duration: 30 years (payment periods = 30 * 12 = 360), NPV: 125000 currency units.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3155512	195			0	lt	How much will you repay in the second year of the mortgage (thus from periods 13 to 24)?				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3149394	196			0	lt	\<item type=\"input\"\>=CUMPRINC_ADD(0.0075;360;125000;13;24;0)\</item\> returns -934.1071				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3149026	197			0	lt	In the first month you will be repaying the following amount:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3154636	198			0	lt	\<item type=\"input\"\>=CUMPRINC_ADD(0.0075;360;125000;1;1;0)\</item\> returns -68.27827				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	bm_id3155370				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>calculating; accumulated interests\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>accumulated interests\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>CUMIPMT function\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155370	266			0	lt	CUMIPMT				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3158411	267			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_KUMZINSZ\"\>Calculates the cumulative interest payments, that is, the total interest, for an investment based on a constant interest rate.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155814	268			0	lt	Syntax				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3147536	269			0	lt	CUMIPMT(Rate; NPer; PV; S; E; Type)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3150475	270			0	lt	\<emph\>Rate\</emph\> is the periodic interest rate.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3153921	271			0	lt	\<emph\>NPer\</emph\> is the payment period with the total number of periods. NPER can also be a non-integer value.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3153186	272			0	lt	\<emph\>PV\</emph\> is the current value in the sequence of payments.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3156259	273			0	lt	\<emph\>S\</emph\> is the first period.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3155990	274			0	lt	\<emph\>E\</emph\> is the last period.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3149777	275			0	lt	\<emph\>Type\</emph\> is the due date of the payment at the beginning or end of each period.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153723	276			0	lt	Example				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3147478	277			0	lt	What are the interest payments at a yearly interest rate of 5.5 %, a payment period of monthly payments for 2 years and a current cash value of 5,000 currency units? The start period is the 4th and the end period is the 6th period. The payment is due at the beginning of each period.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3149819	278			0	lt	\<item type=\"input\"\>=CUMIPMT(5.5%/12;24;5000;4;6;1)\</item\> = -57.54 currency units. The interest payments for between the 4th and 6th period are 57.54 currency units.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	bm_id3083280				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>CUMIPMT_ADD function\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	hd_id3083280	165			0	lt	CUMIPMT_ADD				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3152482	166			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_AAI_FUNC_CUMIPMT\"\>Calculates the accumulated interest for a period.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149713	167			0	lt	Syntax				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3145087	168			0	lt	CUMIPMT_ADD(Rate; NPer; PV; StartPeriod; EndPeriod; Type)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3149277	169			0	lt	\<emph\>Rate\</emph\> is the interest rate for each period.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3149270	170			0	lt	\<emph\>NPer\</emph\> is the total number of payment periods. The rate and NPER must refer to the same unit, and thus both be calculated annually or monthly.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3152967	171			0	lt	\<emph\>PV\</emph\> is the current value.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3156308	172			0	lt	\<emph\>StartPeriod\</emph\> is the first payment period for the calculation.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3149453	173			0	lt	\<emph\>EndPeriod\</emph\> is the last payment period for the calculation.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3150962	174			0	lt	\<emph\>Type\</emph\> is the maturity of a payment at the end of each period (Type = 0) or at the start of the period (Type = 1).				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	hd_id3152933	175			0	lt	Example				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3156324	176			0	lt	The following mortgage loan is taken out on a house:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3147566	177			0	lt	Rate: 9.00 per cent per annum (9% / 12 = 0.0075), Duration: 30 years (NPER = 30 * 12 = 360), Pv: 125000 currency units.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3151272	178			0	lt	How much interest must you pay in the second year of the mortgage (thus from periods 13 to 24)?				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3156130	179			0	lt	\<item type=\"input\"\>=CUMIPMT_ADD(0.0075;360;125000;13;24;0)\</item\> returns -11135.23.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3150764	180			0	lt	How much interest must you pay in the first month?				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3146857	181			0	lt	\<item type=\"input\"\>=CUMIPMT_ADD(0.0075;360;125000;1;1;0)\</item\> returns -937.50.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	bm_id3150878				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>PRICE function\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>prices; fixed interest securities\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>sales values;fixed interest securities\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150878	9			0	lt	PRICE				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3153210	10			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_AAI_FUNC_PRICE\"\>Calculates the market value of a fixed interest security with a par value of 100 currency units as a function of the forecast yield.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154646	11			0	lt	Syntax				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3152804	12			0	lt	PRICE(Settlement; Maturity; Rate; Yield; Redemption; Frequency; Basis)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3156121	13			0	lt	\<emph\>Settlement\</emph\> is the date of purchase of the security.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3149983	14			0	lt	\<emph\>Maturity\</emph\> is the date on which the security matures (expires).				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3153755	15			0	lt	\<emph\>Rate\</emph\> is the annual nominal rate of interest (coupon interest rate)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3155999	16			0	lt	\<emph\>Yield\</emph\> is the annual yield of the security.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3156114	17			0	lt	\<emph\>Redemption\</emph\> is the redemption value per 100 currency units of par value.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3155846	18			0	lt	\<emph\>Frequency\</emph\> is the number of interest payments per year (1, 2 or 4).				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153148	19			0	lt	Example				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3150260	20			0	lt	A security is purchased on 1999-02-15; the maturity date is 2007-11-15. The nominal rate of interest is 5.75%. The yield is 6.5%. The redemption value is 100 currency units. Interest is paid half-yearly (frequency is 2). With calculation on basis 0, the price is as follows:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3147273	21			0	lt	=PRICE("1999-02-15"; "2007-11-15"; 0.0575; 0.065; 100; 2; 0) returns 95.04287.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	bm_id3151297				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>PRICEDISC function\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>prices;non-interest-bearing securities\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>sales values;non-interest-bearing securities\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	hd_id3151297	22			0	lt	PRICEDISC				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3155100	23			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_AAI_FUNC_PRICEDISC\"\>Calculates the price per 100 currency units of par value of a non-interest- bearing security.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149294	24			0	lt	Syntax				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3146084	25			0	lt	PRICEDISC(Settlement; Maturity; Discount; Redemption; Basis)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3159179	26			0	lt	\<emph\>Settlement\</emph\> is the date of purchase of the security.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3154304	27			0	lt	\<emph\>Maturity\</emph\> is the date on which the security matures (expires).				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3156014	28			0	lt	\<emph\>Discount\</emph\> is the discount of a security as a percentage.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3147489	29			0	lt	\<emph\>Redemption\</emph\> is the redemption value per 100 currency units of par value.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	hd_id3152794	30			0	lt	Example				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3149198	31			0	lt	A security is purchased on 1999-02-15; the maturity date is 1999-03-01. Discount in per cent is 5.25%. The redemption value is 100. When calculating on basis 2 the price discount is as follows:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3151178	32			0	lt	=PRICEDISC("1999-02-15"; "1999-03-01"; 0.0525; 100; 2) returns 99.79583.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	bm_id3154693				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>PRICEMAT function\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>prices;interest-bearing securities\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154693	33			0	lt	PRICEMAT				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3153906	34			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_AAI_FUNC_PRICEMAT\"\>Calculates the price per 100 currency units of par value of a security, that pays interest on the maturity date.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154933	35			0	lt	Syntax				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3155393	36			0	lt	PRICEMAT(Settlement; Maturity; Issue; Rate; Yield; Basis)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3153102	37			0	lt	\<emph\>Settlement\</emph\> is the date of purchase of the security.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3150530	38			0	lt	\<emph\>Maturity\</emph\> is the date on which the security matures (expires).				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3149903	39			0	lt	\<emph\>Issue\</emph\> is the date of issue of the security.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3148828	40			0	lt	\<emph\>Rate\</emph\> is the interest rate of the security on the issue date.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3146993	41			0	lt	\<emph\>Yield\</emph\> is the annual yield of the security.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150507	42			0	lt	Example				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3154289	43			0	lt	Settlement date: February 15 1999, maturity date: April 13 1999, issue date: November 11 1998. Interest rate: 6.1 per cent, yield: 6.1 per cent, basis: 30/360 = 0.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3154905	44			0	lt	The price is calculated as follows:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3158409	45			0	lt	=PRICEMAT("1999-02-15";"1999-04-13";"1998-11-11"; 0.061; 0.061;0) returns 99.98449888.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	bm_id3148448				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>calculating; durations\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>durations;calculating\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>DURATION function\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148448	280			0	lt	DURATION				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3153056	281			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_LAUFZEIT\"\>Calculates the number of periods required by an investment to attain the desired value.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145421	282			0	lt	Syntax				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3148933	283			0	lt	DURATION(Rate; PV; FV)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3148801	284			0	lt	\<emph\>Rate\</emph\> is a constant. The interest rate is to be calculated for the entire duration (duration period). The interest rate per period is calculated by dividing the interest rate by the calculated duration. The internal rate for an annuity is to be entered as Rate/12.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3147239	285			0	lt	\<emph\>PV\</emph\> is the present (current) value. The cash value is the deposit of cash or the current cash value of an allowance in kind. As a deposit value a positive value must be entered; the deposit must not be 0 or <0.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3147515	286			0	lt	\<emph\>FV\</emph\> is the expected value. The future value determines the desired (future) value of the deposit.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153579	287			0	lt	Example				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3148480	288			0	lt	At an interest rate of 4.75%, a cash value of 25,000 currency units and a future value of 1,000,000 currency units, a duration of 79.49 payment periods is returned. The periodic payment is the resulting quotient from the future value and the duration, in this case 1,000,000/79.49=12,850.20.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	bm_id3148912				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>calculating;linear depreciations\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>depreciations;linear\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>linear depreciations\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>straight-line depreciations\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>SLN function\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148912	290			0	lt	SLN				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3149154	291			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_LIA\"\>Returns the straight-line depreciation of an asset for one period.\</ahelp\>The amount of the depreciation is constant during the depreciation period.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153240	292			0	lt	Syntax				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3166456	293			0	lt	SLN(Cost; Salvage; Life)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3146955	294			0	lt	\<emph\>Cost\</emph\> is the initial cost of an asset.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3149796	295			0	lt	\<emph\>Salvage\</emph\> is the value of an asset at the end of the depreciation.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3166444	296			0	lt	\<emph\>Life\</emph\> is the depreciation period determining the number of periods in the depreciation of the asset.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155579	297			0	lt	Example				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3154098	298			0	lt	Office equipment with an initial cost of 50,000 currency units is to be depreciated over 7 years. The value at the end of the depreciation is to be 3,500 currency units.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3153390	299			0	lt	\<item type=\"input\"\>=SLN(50000;3,500;84)\</item\> = 553.57 currency units. The periodic monthly depreciation of the office equipment is 553.57 currency units.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	bm_id3153739				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>MDURATION function\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>Macauley duration\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153739	217			0	lt	MDURATION				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3149923	218			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_AAI_FUNC_MDURATION\"\>Calculates the modified Macauley duration of a fixed interest security in years.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149964	219			0	lt	Syntax				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3148987	220			0	lt	MDURATION(Settlement; Maturity; Coupon; Yield; Frequency; Basis)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3148619	221			0	lt	\<emph\>Settlement\</emph\> is the date of purchase of the security.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3149805	222			0	lt	\<emph\>Maturity\</emph\> is the date on which the security matures (expires).				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3154338	223			0	lt	\<emph\>Coupon\</emph\> is the annual nominal rate of interest (coupon interest rate)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3148466	224			0	lt	\<emph\>Yield\</emph\> is the annual yield of the security.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3149423	225			0	lt	\<emph\>Frequency\</emph\> is the number of interest payments per year (1, 2 or 4).				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154602	226			0	lt	Example				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3148652	227			0	lt	A security is purchased on 2001-01-01; the maturity date is 2006-01-01. The nominal rate of interest is 8%. The yield is 9.0%. Interest is paid half-yearly (frequency is 2). Using daily balance interest calculation (basis 3) how long is the modified duration?				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3145378	228			0	lt	=MDURATION("2001-01-01"; "2006-01-01"; 0.08; 0.09; 2; 3) returns 4.02 years.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	bm_id3149242				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>calculating;net present values\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>net present values\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>NPV function\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149242	301			0	lt	NPV				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3145308	302			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_NBW\"\>Returns the present value of an investment based on a series of periodic cash flows and a discount rate. To get the net present value, subtract the cost of the project (the initial cash flow at time zero) from the returned value.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149937	303			0	lt	Syntax				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3153321	304			0	lt	NPV(Rate; Value1; Value2; ...)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3150630	305			0	lt	\<emph\>Rate\</emph\> is the discount rate for a period.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3150427	306			0	lt	\<emph\>Value1;...\</emph\> are up to 30 values, which represent deposits or withdrawals.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153538	307			0	lt	Example				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3154800	308			0	lt	What is the net present value of periodic payments of 10, 20 and 30 currency units with a discount rate of 8.75%. At time zero the costs were payed as -40 currency units.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3143270	309			0	lt	\<item type=\"input\"\>=NPV(8.75%;10;20;30)\</item\> = 49.43 currency units. The net present value is the returned value minus the initial costs of 40 currency units, therefore 9.43 currency units.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	bm_id3149484				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>calculating;nominal interest rates\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>nominal interest rates\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>NOMINAL function\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149484	311			0	lt	NOMINAL				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3149596	312			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_NOMINAL\"\>Calculates the yearly nominal interest rate, given the effective rate and the number of compounding periods per year.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	hd_id3151252	313			0	lt	Syntax				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3152769	314			0	lt	NOMINAL(EffectiveRate; NPerY)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3147521	315			0	lt	\<emph\>EffectiveRate\</emph\> is the effective interest rate				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3156334	316			0	lt	\<emph\>NPerY\</emph\> is the number of periodic interest payments per year.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154473	317			0	lt	Example				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3147091	318			0	lt	What is the nominal interest per year for an effective interest rate of 13.5% if twelve payments are made per year.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3154831	319			0	lt	\<item type=\"input\"\>=NOMINAL(13.5%;12)\</item\> = 12.73%. The nominal interest rate per year is 12.73%.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	bm_id3155123				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>NOMINAL_ADD function\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155123	229			0	lt	NOMINAL_ADD				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3148671	230			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_AAI_FUNC_NOMINAL\"\>Calculates the annual nominal rate of interest on the basis of the effective rate and the number of interest payments per annum.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155611	231			0	lt	Syntax				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3156157	232			0	lt	NOMINAL_ADD(EffectiveRate; NPerY)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3153777	233			0	lt	\<emph\>EffectiveRate\</emph\> is the effective annual rate of interest.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3150409	234			0	lt	\<emph\>NPerY\</emph\> the number of interest payments per year.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	hd_id3146789	235			0	lt	Example				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3145777	236			0	lt	What is the nominal rate of interest for a 5.3543% effective rate of interest and quarterly payment.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3156146	237			0	lt	\<item type=\"input\"\>=NOMINAL_ADD(5.3543%;4)\</item\> returns 0.0525 or 5.25%.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	bm_id3159087				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>DOLLARFR function\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>converting;decimal fractions, into mixed decimal fractions\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	hd_id3159087	208			0	lt	DOLLARFR				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3150593	209			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_AAI_FUNC_DOLLARFR\"\>Converts a quotation that has been given as a decimal number into a mixed decimal fraction.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	hd_id3151106	210			0	lt	Syntax				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3152959	211			0	lt	DOLLARFR(DecimalDollar; Fraction)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3149558	212			0	lt	\<emph\>DecimalDollar\</emph\> is a decimal number.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3153672	213			0	lt	\<emph\>Fraction\</emph\> is a whole number that is used as the denominator of the decimal fraction.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	hd_id3156274	214			0	lt	Example				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3153795	215			0	lt	\<item type=\"input\"\>=DOLLARFR(1.125;16)\</item\> converts into sixteenths. The result is 1.02 for 1 plus 2/16.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3150995	216			0	lt	\<item type=\"input\"\>=DOLLARFR(1.125;8)\</item\> converts into eighths. The result is 1.1 for 1 plus 1/8.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	bm_id3154671				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>fractions; converting\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>converting;decimal fractions, into decimal numbers\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>DOLLARDE function\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154671	199			0	lt	DOLLARDE				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3154418	200			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_AAI_FUNC_DOLLARDE\"\>Converts a quotation that has been given as a decimal fraction into a decimal number.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	hd_id3146124	201			0	lt	Syntax				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3150348	202			0	lt	DOLLARDE(FractionalDollar; Fraction)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3154111	203			0	lt	\<emph\>FractionalDollar\</emph\> is a number given as a decimal fraction.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3153695	204			0	lt	\<emph\>Fraction\</emph\> is a whole number that is used as the denominator of the decimal fraction.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153884	205			0	lt	Example				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3150941	206			0	lt	\<item type=\"input\"\>=DOLLARDE(1.02;16)\</item\> stands for 1 and 2/16. This returns 1.125.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3150830	207			0	lt	\<item type=\"input\"\>=DOLLARDE(1.1;8)\</item\> stands for 1 and 1/8. This returns 1.125.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	bm_id3148974				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>calculating;modified internal rates of return\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>modified internal rates of return\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>MIRR function\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>internal rates of return;modified\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148974	321			0	lt	MIRR				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3155497	322			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_QIKV\"\>Calculates the modified internal rate of return of a series of investments.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154354	323			0	lt	Syntax				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3148399	324			0	lt	MIRR(Values; Investment; ReinvestRate)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3155896	325			0	lt	\<emph\>Values\</emph\> corresponds to the array or the cell reference for cells whose content corresponds to the payments.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3149998	326			0	lt	\<emph\>Investment\</emph\> is the rate of interest of the investments (the negative values of the array)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3159408	327			0	lt	\<emph\>ReinvestRate\</emph\>:the rate of interest of the reinvestment (the positive values of the array)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154714	328			0	lt	Example				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3147352	329			0	lt	Assuming a cell content of A1 = \<item type=\"input\"\>-5\</item\>, A2 = \<item type=\"input\"\>10\</item\>, A3 = \<item type=\"input\"\>15\</item\>, and A4 = \<item type=\"input\"\>8\</item\>, and an investment value of 0.5 and a reinvestment value of 0.1, the result is 94.16%.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	bm_id3149323				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>YIELD function\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>rates of return;securities\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>yields, see also rates of return\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149323	129			0	lt	YIELD				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3150643	130			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_AAI_FUNC_YIELD\"\>Calculates the yield of a security.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149344	131			0	lt	Syntax				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3149744	132			0	lt	YIELD(Settlement; Maturity; Rate; Price; Redemption; Frequency; Basis)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3154526	133			0	lt	\<emph\>Settlement\</emph\> is the date of purchase of the security.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3153266	134			0	lt	\<emph\>Maturity\</emph\> is the date on which the security matures (expires).				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3151284	135			0	lt	\<emph\>Rate\</emph\> is the annual rate of interest.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3147314	136			0	lt	\<emph\>Price\</emph\> is the price (purchase price) of the security per 100 currency units of par value.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3145156	137			0	lt	\<emph\>Redemption\</emph\> is the redemption value per 100 currency units of par value.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3159218	138			0	lt	\<emph\>Frequency\</emph\> is the number of interest payments per year (1, 2 or 4).				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147547	139			0	lt	Example				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3151214	140			0	lt	A security is purchased on 1999-02-15. It matures on 2007-11-15. The rate of interest is 5.75%. The price is 95.04287 currency units per 100 units of par value, the redemption value is 100 units. Interest is paid half-yearly (frequency = 2) and the basis is 0. How high is the yield?				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3154194	141			0	lt	=YIELD("1999-02-15"; "2007-11-15"; 0.0575 ;95.04287; 100; 2; 0) returns 0.065 or 6.50 per cent.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	bm_id3150100				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>YIELDDISC function\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>rates of return;non-interest-bearing securities\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150100	142			0	lt	YIELDDISC				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3150486	143			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_AAI_FUNC_YIELDDISC\"\>Calculates the annual yield of a non-interest-bearing security.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149171	144			0	lt	Syntax				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3159191	145			0	lt	YIELDDISC(Settlement; Maturity; Price; Redemption; Basis)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3150237	146			0	lt	\<emph\>Settlement\</emph\> is the date of purchase of the security.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3146924	147			0	lt	\<emph\>Maturity\</emph\> is the date on which the security matures (expires).				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3151201	148			0	lt	\<emph\>Price\</emph\> is the price (purchase price) of the security per 100 currency units of par value.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3156049	149			0	lt	\<emph\>Redemption\</emph\> is the redemption value per 100 currency units of par value.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154139	150			0	lt	Example				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3163815	151			0	lt	A non-interest-bearing security is purchased on 1999-02-15. It matures on 1999-03-01. The price is 99.795 currency units per 100 units of par value, the redemption value is 100 units. The basis is 2. How high is the yield?				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3155187	152			0	lt	=YIELDDISC("1999-02-15"; "1999-03-01"; 99.795; 100; 2) returns 0.052823 or 5.2823 per cent.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	bm_id3155140				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>YIELDMAT function\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>rates of return;securities with interest paid on maturity\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155140	153			0	lt	YIELDMAT				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3151332	154			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_AAI_FUNC_YIELDMAT\"\>Calculates the annual yield of a security, the interest of which is paid on the date of maturity.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	hd_id3159100	155			0	lt	Syntax				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3159113	156			0	lt	YIELDMAT(Settlement; Maturity; Issue; Rate; Price; Basis)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3149309	157			0	lt	\<emph\>Settlement\</emph\> is the date of purchase of the security.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3151381	158			0	lt	\<emph\>Maturity\</emph\> is the date on which the security matures (expires).				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3153302	159			0	lt	\<emph\>Issue\</emph\> is the date of issue of the security.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3147140	160			0	lt	\<emph\>Rate\</emph\> is the interest rate of the security on the issue date.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3151067	161			0	lt	\<emph\>Price\</emph\> is the price (purchase price) of the security per 100 currency units of par value.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155342	162			0	lt	Example				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3163717	163			0	lt	A security is purchased on 1999-03-15. It matures on 1999-11-03. The issue date was 1998-11-08. The rate of interest is 6.25%, the price is 100.0123 units. The basis is 0. How high is the yield?				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3155311	164			0	lt	=YIELDMAT("1999-03-15"; "1999-11-03"; "1998-11-08"; 0.0625; 100.0123; 0) returns 0.060954 or 6.0954 per cent.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	bm_id3149577				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>calculating;annuities\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>annuities\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>PMT function\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149577	330			0	lt	PMT				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3148563	331			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_RMZ\"\>Returns the periodic payment for an annuity with constant interest rates.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145257	332			0	lt	Syntax				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3147278	333			0	lt	PMT(Rate; NPer; PV; FV; Type)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3147291	334			0	lt	\<emph\>Rate\</emph\> is the periodic interest rate.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3148641	335			0	lt	\<emph\>NPer\</emph\> is the number of periods in which annuity is paid.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3156360	336			0	lt	\<emph\>PV\</emph\> is the present value (cash value) in a sequence of payments.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3154920	337			0	lt	\<emph\>FV\</emph\> (optional) is the desired value (future value) to be reached at the end of the periodic payments.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3156434	338			0	lt	\<emph\>Type\</emph\> (optional) is the due date for the periodic payments. Type=1 is payment at the beginning and Type=0 is payment at the end of each period.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_idN11645				0	lt	\<embedvar href=\"text/scalc/00/00000004.xhp#optional\"/\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	hd_id3152358	339			0	lt	Example				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3154222	340			0	lt	What are the periodic payments at a yearly interest rate of 1.99% if the payment time is 3 years and the cash value is 25,000 currency units. There are 36 months as 36 payment periods, and the interest rate per payment period is 1.99%/12.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3155943	341			0	lt	\<item type=\"input\"\>=PMT(1.99%/12;36;25000)\</item\> = -715.96 currency units. The periodic monthly payment is therefore 715.96 currency units.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	bm_id3155799				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>TBILLEQ function\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>treasury bills;annual return\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>annual return on treasury bills\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155799	58			0	lt	TBILLEQ				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3154403	59			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_AAI_FUNC_TBILLEQ\"\>Calculates the annual return on a treasury bill ().\</ahelp\> A treasury bill is purchased on the settlement date and sold at the full par value on the maturity date, that must fall within the same year. A discount is deducted from the purchase price.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155080	60			0	lt	Syntax				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3150224	61			0	lt	TBILLEQ(Settlement; Maturity; Discount)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3156190	62			0	lt	\<emph\>Settlement\</emph\> is the date of purchase of the security.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3153827	63			0	lt	\<emph\>Maturity\</emph\> is the date on which the security matures (expires).				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3150310	64			0	lt	\<emph\>Discount\</emph\> is the percentage discount on acquisition of the security.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150324	65			0	lt	Example				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3153173	66			0	lt	Settlement date: March 31 1999, maturity date: June 1 1999, discount: 9.14 per cent.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3153520	67			0	lt	The return on the treasury bill corresponding to a security is worked out as follows:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3154382	68			0	lt	=TBILLEQ("1999-03-31";"1999-06-01"; 0.0914) returns 0.094151 or 9.4151 per cent.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	bm_id3151032				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>TBILLPRICE function\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>treasury bills;prices\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>prices;treasury bills\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	hd_id3151032	69			0	lt	TBILLPRICE				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3157887	70			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_AAI_FUNC_TBILLPRICE\"\>Calculates the price of a treasury bill per 100 currency units.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	hd_id3156374	71			0	lt	Syntax				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3150284	72			0	lt	TBILLPRICE(Settlement; Maturity; Discount)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3154059	73			0	lt	\<emph\>Settlement\</emph\> is the date of purchase of the security.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3154073	74			0	lt	\<emph\>Maturity\</emph\> is the date on which the security matures (expires).				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3145765	75			0	lt	\<emph\>Discount\</emph\> is the percentage discount upon acquisition of the security.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153373	76			0	lt	Example				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3155542	77			0	lt	Settlement date: March 31 1999, maturity date: June 1 1999, discount: 9 per cent.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3154578	78			0	lt	The price of the treasury bill is worked out as follows:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3154592	79			0	lt	=TBILLPRICE("1999-03-31";"1999-06-01"; 0.09) returns 98.45.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	bm_id3152912				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>TBILLYIELD function\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>treasury bills;rates of return\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>rates of return of treasury bills\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	hd_id3152912	80			0	lt	TBILLYIELD				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3145560	81			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_AAI_FUNC_TBILLYIELD\"\>Calculates the yield of a treasury bill.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145578	82			0	lt	Syntax				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3156077	83			0	lt	TBILLYIELD(Settlement; Maturity; Price)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3156091	84			0	lt	\<emph\>Settlement\</emph\> is the date of purchase of the security.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3157856	85			0	lt	\<emph\>Maturity\</emph\> is the date on which the security matures (expires).				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3149627	86			0	lt	\<emph\>Price\</emph\> is the price (purchase price) of the treasury bill per 100 currency units of par value.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149642	87			0	lt	Example				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3145178	88			0	lt	Settlement date: March 31 1999, maturity date: June 1 1999, price: 98.45 currency units.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3145193	89			0	lt	The yield of the treasury bill is worked out as follows:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3148528	90			0	lt	=TBILLYIELD("1999-03-31";"1999-06-01"; 98.45) returns 0.091417 or 9.1417 per cent.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3148546	345			0	lt	\<link href=\"text/scalc/01/04060103.xhp\" name=\"Back to Financial Functions Part One\"\>Back to Financial Functions Part One\</link\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060119.xhp	0	help	par_id3146762	346			0	lt	\<link href=\"text/scalc/01/04060118.xhp\" name=\"Forward to Financial Functions Part Three\"\>Forward to Financial Functions Part Three\</link\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	tit				0	lt	Statistical Functions Part One				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3146320	1			0	lt	\<variable id=\"ae\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/01/04060181.xhp\"\>Statistical Functions Part One\</link\>\</variable\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	bm_id3145632				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>INTERCEPT function\</bookmark_value\>         \<bookmark_value\>points of intersection\</bookmark_value\>         \<bookmark_value\>intersections\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145632	2			0	lt	INTERCEPT				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3146887	3			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_ACHSENABSCHNITT\"\>Calculates the point at which a line will intersect the y-values by using known x-values and y-values.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150374	4			0	lt	Syntax				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3149718	5			0	lt	INTERCEPT(DataY; DataX)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3149947	6			0	lt	            \<emph\>DataY\</emph\> is the dependent set of observations or data.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3147412	7			0	lt	            \<emph\>DataX\</emph\> is the independent set of observations or data.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3152983	8			0	lt	Names, arrays or references containing numbers must be used here. Numbers can also be entered directly.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3157906	9			0	lt	Example				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3148728	10			0	lt	To calculate the intercept, use cells D3:D9 as the y value and C3:C9 as the x value from the example spreadsheet. Input will be as follows:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3149013	11			0	lt	            \<item type=\"input\"\>=INTERCEPT(D3:D9;C3:C9)\</item\> = 2.15.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	bm_id3148437				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>COUNT function\</bookmark_value\>         \<bookmark_value\>numbers;counting\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148437	13			0	lt	COUNT				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3150700	14			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_ANZAHL\"\>Counts how many numbers are in the list of arguments.\</ahelp\> Text entries are ignored.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153930	15			0	lt	Syntax				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3148585	16			0	lt	COUNT(Value1; Value2; ... Value30)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3155827	17			0	lt	            \<emph\>Value1; Value2, ...\</emph\> are 1 to 30 values or ranges representing the values to be counted.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149254	18			0	lt	Example				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3149953	19			0	lt	The entries 2, 4, 6 and eight in the Value 1-4 fields are to be counted.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3154558	20			0	lt	            \<item type=\"input\"\>=COUNT(2;4;6;"eight")\</item\> = 3. The count of numbers is therefore 3.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	bm_id3149729				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>COUNTA function\</bookmark_value\>         \<bookmark_value\>number of entries\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149729	22			0	lt	COUNTA				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3150142	23			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_ANZAHL2\"\>Counts how many values are in the list of arguments.\</ahelp\> Text entries are also counted, even when they contain an empty string of length 0. If an argument is an array or reference, empty cells within the array or reference are ignored.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148573	24			0	lt	Syntax				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3153111	25			0	lt	COUNTA(Value1; Value2; ... Value30)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3150001	26			0	lt	            \<emph\>Value1; Value2, ...\</emph\> are 1 to 30 arguments representing the values to be counted.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150334	27			0	lt	Example				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3154508	28			0	lt	The entries 2, 4, 6 and eight in the Value 1-4 fields are to be counted.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3158000	29			0	lt	            \<item type=\"input\"\>=COUNTA(2;4;6;"eight")\</item\> = 4. The count of values is therefore 4.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	bm_id3150267				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>B function\</bookmark_value\>         \<bookmark_value\>probabilities of samples with binomial distribution\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150267	31			0	lt	B				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3156061	32			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_B\"\>Returns the probability of a sample with binomial distribution.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150659	33			0	lt	Syntax				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3148392	34			0	lt	B(Trials; SP; T1; T2)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3149002	35			0	lt	            \<emph\>Trials\</emph\> is the number of independent trials.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3148875	36			0	lt	            \<emph\>SP\</emph\> is the probability of success on each trial.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3145352	37			0	lt	            \<emph\>T1\</emph\> defines the lower limit for the number of trials.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3149538	38			0	lt	            \<emph\>T2\</emph\> (optional) defines the upper limit for the number of trials.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148768	39			0	lt	Example				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3154633	40			0	lt	What is the probability with ten throws of the dice, that a six will come up exactly twice? The probability of a six (or any other number) is 1/6. The following formula combines these factors:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3149393	41			0	lt	            \<item type=\"input\"\>=B(10;1/6;2)\</item\> returns a probability of 29%.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	bm_id3158416				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>RSQ function\</bookmark_value\>         \<bookmark_value\>determination coefficients\</bookmark_value\>         \<bookmark_value\>regression analysis\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3158416	43			0	lt	RSQ				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3154949	44			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_BESTIMMTHEITSMASS\"\>Returns the square of the Pearson correlation coefficient based on the given values.\</ahelp\> RSQ (also called determination coefficient) is a measure for the accuracy of an adjustment and can be used to produce a regression analysis.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3152820	45			0	lt	Syntax				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3155822	46			0	lt	RSQ(DataY; DataX)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3150470	47			0	lt	            \<emph\>DataY\</emph\> is an array or range of data points.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3153181	48			0	lt	            \<emph\>DataX\</emph\> is an array or range of data points.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3156258	49			0	lt	Example				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3155991	50			0	lt	            \<item type=\"input\"\>=RSQ(A1:A20;B1:B20)\</item\> calculates the determination coefficient for both data sets in columns A and B.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	bm_id3145620				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>BETAINV function\</bookmark_value\>         \<bookmark_value\>cumulative probability density function;inverse of\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145620	52			0	lt	BETAINV				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3149825	53			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_BETAINV\"\>Returns the inverse of the cumulative beta probability density function.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3152479	54			0	lt	Syntax				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3156300	55			0	lt	BETAINV(Number; Alpha; Beta; Start; End)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3149266	56			0	lt	            \<emph\>Number\</emph\> is the value between \<emph\>Start\</emph\> and \<emph\>End\</emph\> at which to evaluate the function.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3149710	57			0	lt	            \<emph\>Alpha\</emph\> is a parameter to the distribution.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3156306	58			0	lt	            \<emph\>Beta\</emph\> is a parameter to the distribution.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3150960	59			0	lt	            \<emph\>Start\</emph\> (optional) is the lower bound for \<emph\>Number\</emph\>.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3151268	60			0	lt	            \<emph\>End\</emph\> (optional) is the upper bound for \<emph\>Number\</emph\>.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_idN109DF				0	lt	            \<embedvar href=\"text/scalc/00/00000004.xhp#optional\"/\>         				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147077	61			0	lt	Example				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3146859	62			0	lt	            \<item type=\"input\"\>=BETAINV(0.5;5;10)\</item\> returns the value 0.33.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	bm_id3156096				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>BETADIST function\</bookmark_value\>         \<bookmark_value\>cumulative probability density function;calculating\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3156096	64			0	lt	BETADIST				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3150880	65			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_BETAVERT\"\>Returns the beta function.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150762	66			0	lt	Syntax				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3147571	67			0	lt	BETADIST(Number; Alpha; Beta; Start; End; Cumulative)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3156317	68			0	lt	            \<emph\>Number\</emph\> is the value between \<emph\>Start\</emph\> and \<emph\>End\</emph\> at which to evaluate the function.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3156107	69			0	lt	            \<emph\>Alpha\</emph\> is a parameter to the distribution.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3153619	70			0	lt	            \<emph\>Beta\</emph\> is a parameter to the distribution.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3150254	71			0	lt	            \<emph\>Start\</emph\> (optional) is the lower bound for \<emph\>Number\</emph\>.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3149138	72			0	lt	            \<emph\>End\</emph\> (optional) is the upper bound for \<emph\>Number\</emph\>.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id012020091254453				0	lt	            \<emph\>Cumulative\</emph\> (optional) can be 0 or False to calculate the probability density function. It can be any other value or True or omitted to calculate the cumulative distribution function.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_idN10AB3				0	lt	            \<embedvar href=\"text/scalc/00/00000004.xhp#optional\"/\>         				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145649	73			0	lt	Example				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3156118	74			0	lt	            \<item type=\"input\"\>=BETADIST(0.75;3;4)\</item\> returns the value 0.96				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	bm_id3143228				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>BINOMDIST function\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3143228	76			0	lt	BINOMDIST				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3146897	77			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_BINOMVERT\"\>Returns the individual term binomial distribution probability.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149289	78			0	lt	Syntax				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3156009	79			0	lt	BINOMDIST(X; Trials; SP; C)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3154304	80			0	lt	            \<emph\>X\</emph\> is the number of successes in a set of trials.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3147492	81			0	lt	            \<emph\>Trials\</emph\> is the number of independent trials.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3146085	82			0	lt	            \<emph\>SP\</emph\> is the probability of success on each trial.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3149760	83			0	lt	            \<emph\>C\</emph\> = 0 calculates the probability of a single event and \<emph\>C\</emph\> = 1 calculates the cumulative probability.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3151171	84			0	lt	Example				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3145666	85			0	lt	            \<item type=\"input\"\>=BINOMDIST(A1;12;0.5;0)\</item\> shows (if the values \<item type=\"input\"\>0\</item\> to \<item type=\"input\"\>12\</item\> are entered in A1) the probabilities for 12 flips of a coin that \<emph\>Heads\</emph\> will come up exactly the number of times entered in A1.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3150120	86			0	lt	            \<item type=\"input\"\>=BINOMDIST(A1;12;0.5;1)\</item\> shows the cumulative probabilities for the same series. For example, if A1 = \<item type=\"input\"\>4\</item\>, the cumulative probability of the series is 0, 1, 2, 3 or 4 times \<emph\>Heads\</emph\> (non-exclusive OR).				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	bm_id0119200902432928				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>CHISQINV function\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id0119200902421451				0	lt	CHISQINV				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id0119200902421449				0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\".\"\>Returns the inverse of CHISQDIST.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id0119200902475241				0	lt	Syntax				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id0119200902475286				0	lt	            \<emph\>Probability\</emph\> is the probability value for which the inverse of the chi-square distribution is to be calculated.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id0119200902475282				0	lt	            \<emph\>Degrees Of Freedom\</emph\> is the degrees of freedom for the chi-square function.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	bm_id3148835				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>CHIINV function\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148835	88			0	lt	CHIINV				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3149906	89			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_CHIINV\"\>Returns the inverse of the one-tailed probability of the chi-squared distribution.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3159157	90			0	lt	Syntax				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3150504	91			0	lt	CHIINV(Number; DegreesFreedom)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3154898	92			0	lt	            \<emph\>Number\</emph\> is the value of the error probability.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3154294	93			0	lt	            \<emph\>DegreesFreedom\</emph\> is the degrees of freedom of the experiment.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154208	94			0	lt	Example				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3150777	130			0	lt	A die is thrown 1020 times. The numbers on the die 1 through 6 come up 195, 151, 148, 189, 183 and 154 times (observation values). The hypothesis that the die is not fixed is to be tested.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3153062	131			0	lt	The Chi square distribution of the random sample is determined by the formula given above. Since the expected value for a given number on the die for n throws is n times 1/6, thus 1020/6 = 170, the formula returns a Chi square value of 13.27.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3148806	132			0	lt	If the (observed) Chi square is greater than or equal to the (theoretical) Chi square CHIINV, the hypothesis will be discarded, since the deviation between theory and experiment is too great. If the observed Chi square is less that CHIINV, the hypothesis is confirmed with the indicated probability of error.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3149763	95			0	lt	            \<item type=\"input\"\>=CHIINV(0.05;5)\</item\> returns 11.07.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3159142	133			0	lt	            \<item type=\"input\"\>=CHIINV(0.02;5)\</item\> returns 13.39.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3158401	134			0	lt	If the probability of error is 5%, the die is not true. If the probability of error is 2%, there is no reason to believe it is fixed.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	bm_id3154260				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>CHITEST function\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154260	97			0	lt	CHITEST				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3151052	98			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_CHITEST\"\>Returns the probability of a deviance from a random distribution of two test series based on the chi-squared test for independence.\</ahelp\> CHITEST returns the chi-squared distribution of the data.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3148925	135			0	lt	The probability determined by CHITEST can also be determined with CHIDIST, in which case the Chi square of the random sample must then be passed as a parameter instead of the data row.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154280	99			0	lt	Syntax				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3149162	100			0	lt	CHITEST(DataB; DataE)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3158421	101			0	lt	            \<emph\>DataB\</emph\> is the array of the observations.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3166453	102			0	lt	            \<emph\>DataE\</emph\> is the range of the expected values.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3146946	103			0	lt	Example				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3154096	136			0	lt	Data_B (observed)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3152948	137			0	lt	Data_E (expected)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3152876	138			0	lt	1				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3159279	139			0	lt	                     \<item type=\"input\"\>195\</item\>                  				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3149105	140			0	lt	                     \<item type=\"input\"\>170\</item\>                  				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3149922	141			0	lt	2				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3148621	142			0	lt	                     \<item type=\"input\"\>151\</item\>                  				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3148987	143			0	lt	                     \<item type=\"input\"\>170\</item\>                  				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3149417	144			0	lt	3				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3148661	145			0	lt	                     \<item type=\"input\"\>148\</item\>                  				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3151128	146			0	lt	                     \<item type=\"input\"\>170\</item\>                  				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3148467	147			0	lt	4				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3149237	148			0	lt	                     \<item type=\"input\"\>189\</item\>                  				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3145304	149			0	lt	                     \<item type=\"input\"\>170\</item\>                  				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3149927	150			0	lt	5				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3150630	151			0	lt	                     \<item type=\"input\"\>183\</item\>                  				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3150423	152			0	lt	                     \<item type=\"input\"\>170\</item\>                  				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3143275	153			0	lt	6				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3144750	154			0	lt	                     \<item type=\"input\"\>154\</item\>                  				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3153947	155			0	lt	                     \<item type=\"input\"\>170\</item\>                  				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3149481	104			0	lt	            \<item type=\"input\"\>=CHITEST(A1:A6;B1:B6)\</item\> equals 0.02. This is the probability which suffices the observed data of the theoretical Chi-square distribution.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	bm_id3148690				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>CHIDIST function\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148690	106			0	lt	CHIDIST				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3156338	156			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_CHIVERT\"\>Returns the probability value from the indicated Chi square that a hypothesis is confirmed.\</ahelp\> CHIDIST compares the Chi square value to be given for a random sample that is calculated from the sum of (observed value-expected value)^2/expected value for all values with the theoretical Chi square distribution and determines from this the probability of error for the hypothesis to be tested.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3151316	157			0	lt	The probability determined by CHIDIST can also be determined by CHITEST.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155123	108			0	lt	Syntax				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3158439	109			0	lt	CHIDIST(Number; DegreesFreedom)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3148675	110			0	lt	            \<emph\>Number\</emph\> is the chi-square value of the random sample used to determine the error probability.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3155615	111			0	lt	            \<emph\>DegreesFreedom\</emph\> are the degrees of freedom of the experiment.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3146787	112			0	lt	Example				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3145774	113			0	lt	            \<item type=\"input\"\>=CHIDIST(13.27; 5)\</item\> equals 0.02.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3156141	158			0	lt	If the Chi square value of the random sample is 13.27 and if the experiment has 5 degrees of freedom, then the hypothesis is assured with a probability of error of 2%.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	bm_id0119200902231887				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>CHISQDIST function\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>chi-square distribution\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id0119200901583452				0	lt	CHISQDIST				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id0119200901583471				0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\".\"\>Returns the value of the probability density function or the cumulative distribution function for the chi-square distribution.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id0119200902395520				0	lt	Syntax				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id0119200902395679				0	lt	CHISQDIST(Number; Degrees Of Freedom; Cumulative)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id011920090239564				0	lt	            \<emph\>Number\</emph\> is the number for which the function is to be calculated.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id0119200902395660				0	lt	            \<emph\>Degrees Of Freedom\</emph\> is the degrees of freedom for the chi-square function.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id0119200902395623				0	lt	            \<emph\>Cumulative\</emph\> (optional): 0 or False calculates the probability density function. Other values or True or omitted calculates the cumulative distribution function.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	bm_id3150603				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>EXPONDIST function\</bookmark_value\>         \<bookmark_value\>exponential distributions\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150603	115			0	lt	EXPONDIST				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3149563	116			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_EXPONVERT\"\>Returns the exponential distribution.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153789	117			0	lt	Syntax				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3150987	118			0	lt	EXPONDIST(Number; Lambda; C)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3154663	119			0	lt	            \<emph\>Number\</emph\> is the value of the function.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3154569	120			0	lt	            \<emph\>Lambda\</emph\> is the parameter value.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3147332	121			0	lt	            \<emph\>C\</emph\> is a logical value that determines the form of the function. \<emph\>C = 0\</emph\> calculates the density function, and \<emph\>C = 1\</emph\> calculates the distribution.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3146133	122			0	lt	Example				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3150357	123			0	lt	            \<item type=\"input\"\>=EXPONDIST(3;0.5;1)\</item\> returns 0.78.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	tit				0	lt	Statistical Functions Part Two				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154372	1			0	lt	\<variable id=\"fh\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/01/04060182.xhp\" name=\"Statistical Functions Part Two\"\>Statistical Functions Part Two\</link\>\</variable\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	bm_id3145388				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>FINV function\</bookmark_value\>         \<bookmark_value\>inverse F probability distribution\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145388	2			0	lt	FINV				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	par_id3155089	3			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_FINV\"\>Returns the inverse of the F probability distribution.\</ahelp\> The F distribution is used for F tests in order to set the relation between two differing data sets.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153816	4			0	lt	Syntax				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	par_id3153068	5			0	lt	FINV(Number; DegreesFreedom1; DegreesFreedom2)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	par_id3146866	6			0	lt	            \<emph\>Number\</emph\> is probability value for which the inverse F distribution is to be calculated.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	par_id3153914	7			0	lt	            \<emph\>DegreesFreedom1\</emph\> is the number of degrees of freedom in the numerator of the F distribution.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	par_id3148607	8			0	lt	            \<emph\>DegreesFreedom2\</emph\> is the number of degrees of freedom in the denominator of the F distribution.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	hd_id3156021	9			0	lt	Example				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	par_id3145073	10			0	lt	            \<item type=\"input\"\>=FINV(0.5;5;10)\</item\> yields 0.93.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	bm_id3150888				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>FISHER function\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150888	12			0	lt	FISHER				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	par_id3155384	13			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_FISHER\"\>Returns the Fisher transformation for x and creates a function close to a normal distribution.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149898	14			0	lt	Syntax				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	par_id3143220	15			0	lt	FISHER(Number)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	par_id3159228	16			0	lt	            \<emph\>Number\</emph\> is the value to be transformed.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154763	17			0	lt	Example				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	par_id3149383	18			0	lt	            \<item type=\"input\"\>=FISHER(0.5)\</item\> yields 0.55.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	bm_id3155758				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>FISHERINV function\</bookmark_value\>         \<bookmark_value\>inverse of Fisher transformation\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155758	20			0	lt	FISHERINV				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	par_id3154734	21			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_FISHERINV\"\>Returns the inverse of the Fisher transformation for x and creates a function close to a normal distribution.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155755	22			0	lt	Syntax				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	par_id3146108	23			0	lt	FISHERINV(Number)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	par_id3145115	24			0	lt	            \<emph\>Number\</emph\> is the value that is to undergo reverse-transformation.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155744	25			0	lt	Example				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	par_id3150432	26			0	lt	            \<item type=\"input\"\>=FISHERINV(0.5)\</item\> yields 0.46.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	bm_id3151390				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>FTEST function\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	hd_id3151390	28			0	lt	FTEST				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	par_id3150534	29			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_FTEST\"\>Returns the result of an F test.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	hd_id3166466	30			0	lt	Syntax				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	par_id3153024	31			0	lt	FTEST(Data1; Data2)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	par_id3150032	32			0	lt	            \<emph\>Data1\</emph\> is the first record array.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	par_id3153018	33			0	lt	            \<emph\>Data2\</emph\> is the second record array.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153123	34			0	lt	Example				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	par_id3159126	35			0	lt	            \<item type=\"input\"\>=FTEST(A1:A30;B1:B12)\</item\> calculates whether the two data sets are different in their variance and returns the probability that both sets could have come from the same total population.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	bm_id3150372				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>FDIST function\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150372	37			0	lt	FDIST				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	par_id3152981	38			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_FVERT\"\>Calculates the values of an F distribution.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150484	39			0	lt	Syntax				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	par_id3145826	40			0	lt	FDIST(Number; DegreesFreedom1; DegreesFreedom2)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	par_id3150461	41			0	lt	            \<emph\>Number\</emph\> is the value for which the F distribution is to be calculated.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	par_id3150029	42			0	lt	            \<emph\>degreesFreedom1\</emph\> is the degrees of freedom in the numerator in the F distribution.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	par_id3146877	43			0	lt	            \<emph\>degreesFreedom2\</emph\> is the degrees of freedom in the denominator in the F distribution.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147423	44			0	lt	Example				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	par_id3150696	45			0	lt	            \<item type=\"input\"\>=FDIST(0.8;8;12)\</item\> yields 0.61.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	bm_id0119200903223192				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>GAMMA function\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	hd_id0119200903205393				0	lt	GAMMA				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	par_id0119200903205379				0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\".\"\>Returns the Gamma function value.\</ahelp\> Note that GAMMAINV is not the inverse of GAMMA, but of GAMMADIST.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	hd_id0119200903271613				0	lt	Syntax				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	par_id0119200903271614				0	lt	            \<emph\>Number\</emph\> is the number for which the Gamma function value is to be calculated.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	bm_id3154841				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>GAMMAINV function\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154841	47			0	lt	GAMMAINV				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	par_id3153932	48			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_GAMMAINV\"\>Returns the inverse of the Gamma cumulative distribution GAMMADIST.\</ahelp\> This function allows you to search for variables with different distribution.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149949	49			0	lt	Syntax				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	par_id3155828	50			0	lt	GAMMAINV(Number; Alpha; Beta)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	par_id3145138	51			0	lt	            \<emph\>Number\</emph\> is the probability value for which the inverse Gamma distribution is to be calculated.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	par_id3152785	52			0	lt	            \<emph\>Alpha\</emph\> is the parameter Alpha of the Gamma distribution.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	par_id3154561	53			0	lt	            \<emph\>Beta\</emph\> is the parameter Beta of the Gamma distribution.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148734	54			0	lt	Example				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	par_id3153331	55			0	lt	            \<item type=\"input\"\>=GAMMAINV(0.8;1;1)\</item\> yields 1.61.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	bm_id3154806				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>GAMMALN function\</bookmark_value\>         \<bookmark_value\>natural logarithm of Gamma function\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154806	57			0	lt	GAMMALN				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	par_id3148572	58			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_GAMMALN\"\>Returns the natural logarithm of the Gamma function: G(x).\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	hd_id3152999	59			0	lt	Syntax				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	par_id3153112	60			0	lt	GAMMALN(Number)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	par_id3154502	61			0	lt	            \<emph\>Number\</emph\> is the value for which the natural logarithm of the Gamma function is to be calculated.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153568	62			0	lt	Example				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	par_id3153730	63			0	lt	            \<item type=\"input\"\>=GAMMALN(2)\</item\> yields 0.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	bm_id3150132				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>GAMMADIST function\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150132	65			0	lt	GAMMADIST				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	par_id3155931	66			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_GAMMAVERT\"\>Returns the values of a Gamma distribution.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	par_id0119200903333675				0	lt	The inverse function is GAMMAINV.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147373	67			0	lt	Syntax				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	par_id3155436	68			0	lt	GAMMADIST(Number; Alpha; Beta; C)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	par_id3150571	69			0	lt	            \<emph\>Number\</emph\> is the value for which the Gamma distribution is to be calculated.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	par_id3145295	70			0	lt	            \<emph\>Alpha\</emph\> is the parameter Alpha of the Gamma distribution.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	par_id3151015	71			0	lt	            \<emph\>Beta\</emph\> is the parameter Beta of the Gamma distribution				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	par_id3157972	72			0	lt	            \<emph\>C\</emph\> (optional) = 0 or False calculates the density function \<emph\>C\</emph\> = 1 or True calculates the distribution.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149535	73			0	lt	Example				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	par_id3145354	74			0	lt	            \<item type=\"input\"\>=GAMMADIST(2;1;1;1)\</item\> yields 0.86.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	bm_id3150272				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>GAUSS function\</bookmark_value\>         \<bookmark_value\>normal distribution; standard\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150272	76			0	lt	GAUSS				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	par_id3149030	77			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_GAUSS\"\>Returns the standard normal cumulative distribution.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	par_id2059694				0	lt	It is GAUSS(x)=NORMSDIST(x)-0.5				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153551	78			0	lt	Syntax				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	par_id3155368	79			0	lt	GAUSS(Number)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	par_id3153228	80			0	lt	            \<emph\>Number\</emph\> is the value for which the value of the standard normal distribution is to be calculated.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150691	81			0	lt	Example				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	par_id3154867	82			0	lt	            \<item type=\"input\"\>=GAUSS(0.19)\</item\> = 0.08				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	par_id3148594	83			0	lt	            \<item type=\"input\"\>=GAUSS(0.0375)\</item\> = 0.01				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	bm_id3148425				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>GEOMEAN function\</bookmark_value\>         \<bookmark_value\>means;geometric\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148425	85			0	lt	GEOMEAN				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	par_id3156257	86			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_GEOMITTEL\"\>Returns the geometric mean of a sample.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147167	87			0	lt	Syntax				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	par_id3153720	88			0	lt	GEOMEAN(Number1; Number2; ...Number30)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	par_id3152585	89			0	lt	            \<emph\>Number1, Number2,...Number30\</emph\> are numeric arguments or ranges that represent a random sample.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	hd_id3146146	90			0	lt	Example				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	par_id3149819	92			0	lt	            \<item type=\"input\"\>=GEOMEAN(23;46;69)\</item\> = 41.79. The geometric mean value of this random sample is therefore 41.79.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	bm_id3152966				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>TRIMMEAN function\</bookmark_value\>         \<bookmark_value\>means;of data set without margin data\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	hd_id3152966	94			0	lt	TRIMMEAN				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	par_id3149716	95			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_GESTUTZTMITTEL\"\>Returns the mean of a data set without the Alpha percent of data at the margins.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149281	96			0	lt	Syntax				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	par_id3154821	97			0	lt	TRIMMEAN(Data; Alpha)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	par_id3155834	98			0	lt	            \<emph\>Data\</emph\> is the array of data in the sample.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	par_id3156304	99			0	lt	            \<emph\>Alpha\</emph\> is the percentage of the marginal data that will not be taken into consideration.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	hd_id3151180	100			0	lt	Example				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	par_id3156130	101			0	lt	            \<item type=\"input\"\>=TRIMMEAN(A1:A50; 0.1)\</item\> calculates the mean value of numbers in A1:A50, without taking into consideration the 5 percent of the values representing the highest values and the 5 percent of the values representing the lowest ones. The percentage numbers refer to the amount of the untrimmed mean value, not to the number of summands.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	bm_id3153216				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>ZTEST function\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153216	103			0	lt	ZTEST				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	par_id3150758	104			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_GTEST\"\>Calculates the probability of observing a z-statistic greater than the one computed based on a sample.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150872	105			0	lt	Syntax				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	par_id3153274	106			0	lt	ZTEST(Data; mu; Sigma)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	par_id3156109	107			0	lt	            \<emph\>Data\</emph\> is the given sample, drawn from a normally distributed population.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	par_id3149977	108			0	lt	            \<emph\>mu\</emph\> is the known mean of the population.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	par_id3154740	109			0	lt	            \<emph\>Sigma\</emph\> (optional) is the known standard deviation of the population. If omitted, the standard deviation of the given sample is used.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	par_id0305200911372999				0	lt	See also the \<link href=\"http://wiki.services.openoffice.org/wiki/Documentation/How_Tos/Calc:_ZTEST_function\"\>Wiki page\</link\>.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	bm_id3153623				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>HARMEAN function\</bookmark_value\>         \<bookmark_value\>means;harmonic\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153623	113			0	lt	HARMEAN				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	par_id3155102	114			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_HARMITTEL\"\>Returns the harmonic mean of a data set.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	hd_id3146900	115			0	lt	Syntax				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	par_id3149287	116			0	lt	HARMEAN(Number1; Number2; ...Number30)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	par_id3154303	117			0	lt	            \<emph\>Number1,Number2,...Number30\</emph\> are up to 30 values or ranges, that can be used to calculate the harmonic mean.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	hd_id3159179	118			0	lt	Example				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	par_id3146093	120			0	lt	            \<item type=\"input\"\>=HARMEAN(23;46;69)\</item\> = 37.64. The harmonic mean of this random sample is thus 37.64				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	bm_id3152801				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>HYPGEOMDIST function\</bookmark_value\>         \<bookmark_value\>sampling without replacement\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	hd_id3152801	122			0	lt	HYPGEOMDIST				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	par_id3159341	123			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_HYPGEOMVERT\"\>Returns the hypergeometric distribution.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154697	124			0	lt	Syntax				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	par_id3155388	125			0	lt	HYPGEOMDIST(X; NSample; Successes; NPopulation)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	par_id3154933	126			0	lt	            \<emph\>X\</emph\> is the number of results achieved in the random sample.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	par_id3153106	127			0	lt	            \<emph\>NSample\</emph\> is the size of the random sample.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	par_id3146992	128			0	lt	            \<emph\>Successes\</emph\> is the number of possible results in the total population.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	par_id3148826	129			0	lt	            \<emph\>NPopulation \</emph\>is the size of the total population.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150529	130			0	lt	Example				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	par_id3154904	131			0	lt	            \<item type=\"input\"\>=HYPGEOMDIST(2;2;90;100)\</item\> yields 0.81. If 90 out of 100 pieces of buttered toast fall from the table and hit the floor with the buttered side first, then if 2 pieces of buttered toast are dropped from the table, the probability is 81%, that both will strike buttered side first.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060183.xhp	0	help	tit				0	lt	Statistical Functions Part Three				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060183.xhp	0	help	hd_id3166425	1			0	lt	\<variable id=\"kl\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/01/04060183.xhp\" name=\"Statistical Functions Part Three\"\>Statistical Functions Part Three\</link\>\</variable\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060183.xhp	0	help	bm_id3149530				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>LARGE function\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060183.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149530	2			0	lt	LARGE				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060183.xhp	0	help	par_id3150518	3			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_KGROESSTE\"\>Returns the Rank_c-th largest value in a data set.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060183.xhp	0	help	hd_id3152990	4			0	lt	Syntax				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060183.xhp	0	help	par_id3154372	5			0	lt	LARGE(Data; RankC)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060183.xhp	0	help	par_id3152986	6			0	lt	\<emph\>Data\</emph\> is the cell range of data.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060183.xhp	0	help	par_id3156448	7			0	lt	\<emph\>RankC\</emph\> is the ranking of the value.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060183.xhp	0	help	hd_id3152889	8			0	lt	Example				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060183.xhp	0	help	par_id3148702	9			0	lt	\<item type=\"input\"\>=LARGE(A1:C50;2)\</item\> gives the second largest value in A1:C50.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060183.xhp	0	help	bm_id3154532				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>SMALL function\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060183.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154532	11			0	lt	SMALL				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060183.xhp	0	help	par_id3157981	12			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_KKLEINSTE\"\>Returns the Rank_c-th smallest value in a data set.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060183.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154957	13			0	lt	Syntax				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060183.xhp	0	help	par_id3153974	14			0	lt	SMALL(Data; RankC)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060183.xhp	0	help	par_id3154540	15			0	lt	\<emph\>Data\</emph\> is the cell range of data.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060183.xhp	0	help	par_id3155094	16			0	lt	\<emph\>RankC\</emph\> is the rank of the value.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060183.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153247	17			0	lt	Example				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060183.xhp	0	help	par_id3149897	18			0	lt	\<item type=\"input\"\>=SMALL(A1:C50;2)\</item\> gives the second smallest value in A1:C50.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060183.xhp	0	help	bm_id3153559				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>CONFIDENCE function\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060183.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153559	20			0	lt	CONFIDENCE				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060183.xhp	0	help	par_id3153814	21			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_KONFIDENZ\"\>Returns the (1-alpha) confidence interval for a normal distribution.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060183.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149315	22			0	lt	Syntax				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060183.xhp	0	help	par_id3147501	23			0	lt	CONFIDENCE(Alpha; StDev; Size)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060183.xhp	0	help	par_id3149872	24			0	lt	\<emph\>Alpha\</emph\> is the level of the confidence interval.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060183.xhp	0	help	par_id3145324	25			0	lt	\<emph\>StDev\</emph\> is the standard deviation for the total population.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060183.xhp	0	help	par_id3153075	26			0	lt	\<emph\>Size\</emph\> is the size of the total population.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060183.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150435	27			0	lt	Example				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060183.xhp	0	help	par_id3153335	28			0	lt	\<item type=\"input\"\>=CONFIDENCE(0.05;1.5;100)\</item\> gives 0.29.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060183.xhp	0	help	bm_id3148746				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>CORREL function\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>coefficient of correlation\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060183.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148746	30			0	lt	CORREL				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060183.xhp	0	help	par_id3147299	31			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_KORREL\"\>Returns the correlation coefficient between two data sets.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060183.xhp	0	help	hd_id3156397	32			0	lt	Syntax				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060183.xhp	0	help	par_id3153023	33			0	lt	CORREL(Data1; Data2)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060183.xhp	0	help	par_id3150036	34			0	lt	\<emph\>Data1\</emph\> is the first data set.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060183.xhp	0	help	par_id3153021	35			0	lt	\<emph\>Data2\</emph\> is the second data set.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060183.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149720	36			0	lt	Example				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060183.xhp	0	help	par_id3149941	37			0	lt	\<item type=\"input\"\>=CORREL(A1:A50;B1:B50)\</item\> calculates the correlation coefficient as a measure of the linear correlation of the two data sets.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060183.xhp	0	help	bm_id3150652				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>COVAR function\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060183.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150652	39			0	lt	COVAR				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060183.xhp	0	help	par_id3146875	40			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_KOVAR\"\>Returns the covariance of the product of paired deviations.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060183.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149013	41			0	lt	Syntax				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060183.xhp	0	help	par_id3150740	42			0	lt	COVAR(Data1; Data2)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060183.xhp	0	help	par_id3145827	43			0	lt	\<emph\>Data1\</emph\> is the first data set.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060183.xhp	0	help	par_id3150465	44			0	lt	\<emph\>Data2\</emph\> is the second data set.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060183.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154677	45			0	lt	Example				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060183.xhp	0	help	par_id3144748	46			0	lt	\<item type=\"input\"\>=COVAR(A1:A30;B1:B30)\</item\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060183.xhp	0	help	bm_id3147472				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>CRITBINOM function\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060183.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147472	48			0	lt	CRITBINOM				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060183.xhp	0	help	par_id3149254	49			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_KRITBINOM\"\>Returns the smallest value for which the cumulative binomial distribution is less than or equal to a criterion value.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060183.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153930	50			0	lt	Syntax				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060183.xhp	0	help	par_id3148586	51			0	lt	CRITBINOM(Trials; SP; Alpha)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060183.xhp	0	help	par_id3145593	52			0	lt	\<emph\>Trials\</emph\> is the total number of trials.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060183.xhp	0	help	par_id3153084	53			0	lt	\<emph\>SP\</emph\> is the probability of success for one trial.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060183.xhp	0	help	par_id3149726	54			0	lt	\<emph\>Alpha\</emph\> is the threshold probability to be reached or exceeded.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060183.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148752	55			0	lt	Example				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060183.xhp	0	help	par_id3148740	56			0	lt	\<item type=\"input\"\>=CRITBINOM(100;0.5;0.1)\</item\> yields 44.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060183.xhp	0	help	bm_id3155956				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>KURT function\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060183.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155956	58			0	lt	KURT				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060183.xhp	0	help	par_id3153108	59			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_KURT\"\>Returns the kurtosis of a data set (at least 4 values required).\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060183.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150334	60			0	lt	Syntax				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060183.xhp	0	help	par_id3154508	61			0	lt	KURT(Number1; Number2; ...Number30)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060183.xhp	0	help	par_id3145167	62			0	lt	\<emph\>Number1,Number2,...Number30\</emph\> are numeric arguments or ranges representing a random sample of distribution.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060183.xhp	0	help	hd_id3158000	63			0	lt	Example				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060183.xhp	0	help	par_id3150016	64			0	lt	\<item type=\"input\"\>=KURT(A1;A2;A3;A4;A5;A6)\</item\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060183.xhp	0	help	bm_id3150928				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>LOGINV function\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>inverse of lognormal distribution\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060183.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150928	66			0	lt	LOGINV				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060183.xhp	0	help	par_id3145297	67			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_LOGINV\"\>Returns the inverse of the lognormal distribution.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060183.xhp	0	help	hd_id3151016	68			0	lt	Syntax				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060183.xhp	0	help	par_id3153049	69			0	lt	LOGINV(Number; Mean; StDev)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060183.xhp	0	help	par_id3148390	70			0	lt	\<emph\>Number\</emph\> is the probability value for which the inverse standard logarithmic distribution is to be calculated.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060183.xhp	0	help	par_id3149538	71			0	lt	\<emph\>Mean\</emph\> is the arithmetic mean of the standard logarithmic distribution.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060183.xhp	0	help	par_id3145355	72			0	lt	\<emph\>StDev\</emph\> is the standard deviation of the standard logarithmic distribution.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060183.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148768	73			0	lt	Example				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060183.xhp	0	help	par_id3155623	74			0	lt	\<item type=\"input\"\>=LOGINV(0.05;0;1)\</item\> returns 0.19.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060183.xhp	0	help	bm_id3158417				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>LOGNORMDIST function\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>cumulative lognormal distribution\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060183.xhp	0	help	hd_id3158417	76			0	lt	LOGNORMDIST				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060183.xhp	0	help	par_id3154953	77			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_LOGNORMVERT\"\>Returns the cumulative lognormal distribution.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060183.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150474	78			0	lt	Syntax				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060183.xhp	0	help	par_id3150686	79			0	lt	LOGNORMDIST(Number; Mean; StDev; Cumulative)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060183.xhp	0	help	par_id3154871	80			0	lt	\<emph\>Number\</emph\> is the probability value for which the standard logarithmic distribution is to be calculated.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060183.xhp	0	help	par_id3155820	81			0	lt	\<emph\>Mean\</emph\> (optional) is the mean value of the standard logarithmic distribution.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060183.xhp	0	help	par_id3155991	82			0	lt	\<emph\>StDev\</emph\> (optional) is the standard deviation of the standard logarithmic distribution.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060183.xhp	0	help	par_id3155992				0	lt	\<emph\>Cumulative\</emph\> (optional) = 0 calculates the density function, Cumulative = 1 calculates the distribution.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060183.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153178	83			0	lt	Example				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060183.xhp	0	help	par_id3149778	84			0	lt	\<item type=\"input\"\>=LOGNORMDIST(0.1;0;1)\</item\> returns 0.01.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	tit				0	lt	Statistical Functions Part Four				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153415	1			0	lt	\<variable id=\"mq\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/01/04060184.xhp\" name=\"Statistical Functions Part Four\"\>Statistical Functions Part Four\</link\>\</variable\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	bm_id3154511				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>MAX function\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154511	2			0	lt	MAX				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	par_id3153709	3			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_MAX\"\>Returns the maximum value in a list of arguments.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	par_id9282509				0	lt	Returns 0 if no numeric value and no error was encountered in the cell range(s) passed as cell reference(s). Text cells are ignored by MIN() and MAX(). The functions MINA() and MAXA() return 0 if no value (numeric or text) and no error was encountered. Passing a literal string argument to MIN() or MAX(), e.g. MIN("string"), still results in an error.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154256	4			0	lt	Syntax				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	par_id3147340	5			0	lt	MAX(Number1; Number2; ...Number30)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	par_id3149568	6			0	lt	\<emph\>Number1; Number2;...Number30\</emph\> are numerical values or ranges. 				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153963	7			0	lt	Example				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	par_id3147343	8			0	lt	\<item type=\"input\"\>=MAX(A1;A2;A3;50;100;200)\</item\> returns the largest value from the list.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	par_id3148485	9			0	lt	\<item type=\"input\"\>=MAX(A1:B100)\</item\> returns the largest value from the list.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	bm_id3166426				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>MAXA function\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	hd_id3166426	139			0	lt	MAXA				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	par_id3150363	140			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_MAXA\"\>Returns the maximum value in a list of arguments. In opposite to MAX, here you can enter text. The value of the text is 0.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	par_id7689443				0	lt	The functions MINA() and MAXA() return 0 if no value (numeric or text) and no error was encountered.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150516	141			0	lt	Syntax				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	par_id3166431	142			0	lt	MAXA(Value1; Value2; ... Value30)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	par_id3150202	143			0	lt	\<emph\>Value1; Value2;...Value30\</emph\> are values or ranges. Text has the value of 0.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	hd_id3156290	144			0	lt	Example				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	par_id3156446	145			0	lt	\<item type=\"input\"\>=MAXA(A1;A2;A3;50;100;200;"Text")\</item\> returns the largest value from the list.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	par_id3149404	146			0	lt	\<item type=\"input\"\>=MAXA(A1:B100)\</item\> returns the largest value from the list.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	bm_id3153820				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>MEDIAN function\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153820	11			0	lt	MEDIAN				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	par_id3151241	12			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_MEDIAN\"\>Returns the median of a set of numbers. In a set containing an uneven number of values, the median will be the number in the middle of the set and in a set containing an even number of values, it will be the mean of the two values in the middle of the set.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148871	13			0	lt	Syntax				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	par_id3155264	14			0	lt	MEDIAN(Number1; Number2; ...Number30)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	par_id3150109	15			0	lt	\<emph\>Number1; Number2;...Number30\</emph\> are values or ranges, which represent a sample. Each number can also be replaced by a reference.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	hd_id3144506	16			0	lt	Example				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	par_id3145078	17			0	lt	for an odd number: \<item type=\"input\"\>=MEDIAN(1;5;9;20;21)\</item\> returns 9 as the median value.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	par_id3149126	165			0	lt	for an even number: \<item type=\"input\"\>=MEDIAN(1;5;9;20)\</item\> returns the average of the two middle values 5 and 9, thus 7.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	bm_id3154541				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>MIN function\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154541	19			0	lt	MIN				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	par_id3143222	20			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_MIN\"\>Returns the minimum value in a list of arguments.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	par_id2301400				0	lt	Returns 0 if no numeric value and no error was encountered in the cell range(s) passed as cell reference(s). Text cells are ignored by MIN() and MAX(). The functions MINA() and MAXA() return 0 if no value (numeric or text) and no error was encountered. Passing a literal string argument to MIN() or MAX(), e.g. MIN("string"), still results in an error.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154651	21			0	lt	Syntax				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	par_id3146964	22			0	lt	MIN(Number1; Number2; ...Number30)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	par_id3153486	23			0	lt	\<emph\>Number1; Number2;...Number30\</emph\> are numerical values or ranges.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155523	24			0	lt	Example				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	par_id3154734	25			0	lt	\<item type=\"input\"\>=MIN(A1:B100)\</item\> returns the smallest value in the list.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	bm_id3147504				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>MINA function\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147504	148			0	lt	MINA				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	par_id3147249	149			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_MINA\"\>Returns the minimum value in a list of arguments. Here you can also enter text. The value of the text is 0.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	par_id4294564				0	lt	The functions MINA() and MAXA() return 0 if no value (numeric or text) and no error was encountered.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150435	150			0	lt	Syntax				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	par_id3153336	151			0	lt	MINA(Value1; Value2; ... Value30)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	par_id3146098	152			0	lt	\<emph\>Value1; Value2;...Value30\</emph\> are values or ranges. Text has the value of 0.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148743	153			0	lt	Example				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	par_id3147401	154			0	lt	\<item type=\"input\"\>=MINA(1;"Text";20)\</item\> returns 0.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	par_id3147295	155			0	lt	\<item type=\"input\"\>=MINA(A1:B100)\</item\> returns the smallest value in the list.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	bm_id3166465				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>AVEDEV function\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>averages;statistical functions\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	hd_id3166465	27			0	lt	AVEDEV				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	par_id3150373	28			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_MITTELABW\"\>Returns the average of the absolute deviations of data points from their mean.\</ahelp\> Displays the diffusion in a data set.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150038	29			0	lt	Syntax				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	par_id3145636	30			0	lt	AVEDEV(Number1; Number2; ...Number30)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	par_id3157871	31			0	lt	\<emph\>Number1, Number2,...Number30\</emph\> are values or ranges that represent a sample. Each number can also be replaced by a reference.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149725	32			0	lt	Example				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	par_id3153122	33			0	lt	            \<item type=\"input\"\>=AVEDEV(A1:A50)\</item\>         				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	bm_id3145824				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>AVERAGE function\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145824	35			0	lt	AVERAGE				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	par_id3150482	36			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_MITTELWERT\"\>Returns the average of the arguments.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	hd_id3146943	37			0	lt	Syntax				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	par_id3154679	38			0	lt	AVERAGE(Number1; Number2; ...Number30)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	par_id3150741	39			0	lt	\<emph\>Number1; Number2;...Number 0\</emph\> are numerical values or ranges.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153039	40			0	lt	Example				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	par_id3151232	41			0	lt	\<item type=\"input\"\>=AVERAGE(A1:A50)\</item\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	bm_id3148754				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>AVERAGEA function\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148754	157			0	lt	AVERAGEA				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	par_id3145138	158			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_MITTELWERTA\"\>Returns the average of the arguments. The value of a text is 0.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153326	159			0	lt	Syntax				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	par_id3149734	160			0	lt	AVERAGEA(Value1; Value2; ... Value30)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	par_id3155260	161			0	lt	\<emph\>Value1; Value2;...Value30\</emph\> are values or ranges. Text has the value of 0.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149504	162			0	lt	Example				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	par_id3150864	163			0	lt	\<item type=\"input\"\>=AVERAGEA(A1:A50)\</item\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	bm_id3153933				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>MODE function\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>most common value\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153933	43			0	lt	MODE				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	par_id3153085	44			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_MODALWERT\"\>Returns the most common value in a data set.\</ahelp\> If there are several values with the same frequency, it returns the smallest value. An error occurs when a value doesn't appear twice.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153003	45			0	lt	Syntax				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	par_id3155950	46			0	lt	MODE(Number1; Number2; ...Number30)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	par_id3150337	47			0	lt	\<emph\>Number1; Number2;...Number30\</emph\> are numerical values or ranges.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153571	48			0	lt	Example				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	par_id3153733	49			0	lt	\<item type=\"input\"\>=MODE(A1:A50)\</item\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	bm_id3149879				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>NEGBINOMDIST function\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>negative binomial distribution\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149879	51			0	lt	NEGBINOMDIST				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	par_id3155437	52			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_NEGBINOMVERT\"\>Returns the negative binomial distribution.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145351	53			0	lt	Syntax				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	par_id3150935	54			0	lt	NEGBINOMDIST(X; R; SP)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	par_id3153044	55			0	lt	\<emph\>X\</emph\> represents the value returned for unsuccessful tests.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	par_id3151018	56			0	lt	\<emph\>R\</emph\> represents the value returned for successful tests.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	par_id3148878	57			0	lt	\<emph\>SP\</emph\> is the probability of the success of an attempt.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149539	58			0	lt	Example				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	par_id3148770	59			0	lt	\<item type=\"input\"\>=NEGBINOMDIST(1;1;0.5)\</item\> returns 0.25.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	bm_id3155516				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>NORMINV function\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>normal distribution;inverse of\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155516	61			0	lt	NORMINV				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	par_id3154634	62			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_NORMINV\"\>Returns the inverse of the normal cumulative distribution.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153227	63			0	lt	Syntax				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	par_id3147534	64			0	lt	NORMINV(Number; Mean; StDev)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	par_id3154950	65			0	lt	\<emph\>Number\</emph\> represents the probability value used to determine the inverse normal distribution.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	par_id3150690	66			0	lt	\<emph\>Mean\</emph\> represents the mean value in the normal distribution.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	par_id3148594	67			0	lt	\<emph\>StDev\</emph\> represents the standard deviation of the normal distribution.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155822	68			0	lt	Example				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	par_id3153921	69			0	lt	\<item type=\"input\"\>=NORMINV(0.9;63;5)\</item\> returns 69.41. If the average egg weighs 63 grams with a standard deviation of 5, then there will be 90% probability that the egg will not be heavier than 69.41g grams.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	bm_id3153722				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>NORMDIST function\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>density function\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153722	71			0	lt	NORMDIST				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	par_id3150386	72			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_NORMVERT\"\>Returns the density function or the normal cumulative distribution.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	hd_id3083282	73			0	lt	Syntax				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	par_id3150613	74			0	lt	NORMDIST(Number; Mean; StDev; C)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	par_id3149820	75			0	lt	\<emph\>Number\</emph\> is the value of the distribution based on which the normal distribution is to be calculated.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	par_id3146063	76			0	lt	\<emph\>Mean\</emph\> is the mean value of the distribution.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	par_id3156295	77			0	lt	\<emph\>StDev\</emph\> is the standard deviation of the distribution.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	par_id3145080	78			0	lt	\<emph\>C\</emph\> is optional. \<emph\>C\</emph\> = 0 calculates the density function, \<emph\>C\</emph\> = 1 calculates the distribution.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	hd_id3152972	79			0	lt	Example				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	par_id3149283	80			0	lt	\<item type=\"input\"\>=NORMDIST(70;63;5;0)\</item\> returns 0.03.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	par_id3149448	81			0	lt	\<item type=\"input\"\>=NORMDIST(70;63;5;1)\</item\> returns 0.92.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	bm_id3152934				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>PEARSON function\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	hd_id3152934	83			0	lt	PEARSON				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	par_id3153216	84			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_PEARSON\"\>Returns the Pearson product moment correlation coefficient r.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147081	85			0	lt	Syntax				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	par_id3156133	86			0	lt	PEARSON(Data1; Data2)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	par_id3151272	87			0	lt	\<emph\>Data1\</emph\> represents the array of the first data set.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	par_id3153279	88			0	lt	\<emph\>Data2\</emph\> represents the array of the second data set.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147567	89			0	lt	Example				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	par_id3151187	90			0	lt	\<item type=\"input\"\>=PEARSON(A1:A30;B1:B30)\</item\> returns the Pearson correlation coefficient of both data sets.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	bm_id3152806				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>PHI function\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	hd_id3152806	92			0	lt	PHI				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	par_id3150254	93			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_PHI\"\>Returns the values of the distribution function for a standard normal distribution.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154748	94			0	lt	Syntax				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	par_id3149976	95			0	lt	PHI(Number)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	par_id3156108	96			0	lt	\<emph\>Number\</emph\> represents the value based on which the standard normal distribution is calculated.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153621	97			0	lt	Example				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	par_id3155849	98			0	lt	\<item type=\"input\"\>=PHI(2.25) \</item\>= 0.03				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	par_id3143236	99			0	lt	\<item type=\"input\"\>=PHI(-2.25)\</item\> = 0.03				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	par_id3149286	100			0	lt	\<item type=\"input\"\>=PHI(0)\</item\> = 0.4				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	bm_id3153985				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>POISSON function\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153985	102			0	lt	POISSON				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	par_id3154298	103			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_POISSON\"\>Returns the Poisson distribution.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	hd_id3159183	104			0	lt	Syntax				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	par_id3146093	105			0	lt	POISSON(Number; Mean; C)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	par_id3147253	106			0	lt	\<emph\>Number\</emph\> represents the value based on which the Poisson distribution is calculated.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	par_id3151177	107			0	lt	\<emph\>Mean\</emph\> represents the middle value of the Poisson distribution.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	par_id3149200	108			0	lt	\<emph\>C\</emph\> (optional) = 0 or False calculates the density function; \<emph\>C\</emph\> = 1 or True calculates the distribution. When omitted, the default value True is inserted when you save the document, for best compatibility with other programs and older versions of %PRODUCTNAME.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	hd_id3159347	109			0	lt	Example				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	par_id3150113	110			0	lt	\<item type=\"input\"\>=POISSON(60;50;1)\</item\> returns 0.93.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	bm_id3153100				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>PERCENTILE function\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153100	112			0	lt	PERCENTILE				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	par_id3154940	113			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_QUANTIL\"\>Returns the alpha-percentile of data values in an array.\</ahelp\> A percentile returns the scale value for a data series which goes from the smallest (Alpha=0) to the largest value (alpha=1) of a data series. For \<item type=\"literal\"\>Alpha\</item\> = 25%, the percentile means the first quartile; \<item type=\"literal\"\>Alpha\</item\> = 50% is the MEDIAN.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150531	114			0	lt	Syntax				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	par_id3148813	115			0	lt	PERCENTILE(Data; Alpha)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	par_id3153054	116			0	lt	\<emph\>Data\</emph\> represents the array of data.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	par_id3154212	117			0	lt	\<emph\>Alpha\</emph\> represents the percentage of the scale between 0 and 1.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154290	118			0	lt	Example				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	par_id3159147	119			0	lt	\<item type=\"input\"\>=PERCENTILE(A1:A50;0.1)\</item\> represents the value in the data set, which equals 10% of the total data scale in A1:A50.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	bm_id3148807				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>PERCENTRANK function\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148807	121			0	lt	PERCENTRANK				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	par_id3153573	122			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_QUANTILSRANG\"\>Returns the percentage rank of a value in a sample.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147512	123			0	lt	Syntax				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	par_id3147238	124			0	lt	PERCENTRANK(Data; Value)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	par_id3154266	125			0	lt	\<emph\>Data\</emph\> represents the array of data in the sample.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	par_id3148475	126			0	lt	\<emph\>Value\</emph\> represents the value whose percentile rank must be determined.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155364	127			0	lt	Example				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	par_id3149163	128			0	lt	\<item type=\"input\"\>=PERCENTRANK(A1:A50;50)\</item\> returns the percentage rank of the value 50 from the total range of all values found in A1:A50. If 50 falls outside the total range, an error message will appear.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	bm_id3166442				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>QUARTILE function\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	hd_id3166442	130			0	lt	QUARTILE				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	par_id3146958	131			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_QUARTILE\"\>Returns the quartile of a data set.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	hd_id3152942	132			0	lt	Syntax				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	par_id3153684	133			0	lt	QUARTILE(Data; Type)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	par_id3153387	134			0	lt	\<emph\>Data\</emph\> represents the array of data in the sample.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	par_id3155589	135			0	lt	\<emph\>Type\</emph\> represents the type of quartile. (0 = MIN, 1 = 25%, 2 = 50% (MEDIAN), 3 = 75% and 4 = MAX.)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149103	136			0	lt	Example				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060184.xhp	0	help	par_id3159276	137			0	lt	\<item type=\"input\"\>=QUARTILE(A1:A50;2)\</item\> returns the value of which 50% of the scale corresponds to the lowest to highest values in the range A1:A50.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060185.xhp	0	help	tit				0	lt	Statistical Functions Part Five				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060185.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147072	1			0	lt	\<variable id=\"rz\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/01/04060185.xhp\" name=\"Statistical Functions Part Five\"\>Statistical Functions Part Five\</link\>\</variable\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060185.xhp	0	help	bm_id3155071				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>RANK function\</bookmark_value\>         \<bookmark_value\>numbers;determining ranks\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060185.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155071	2			0	lt	RANK				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060185.xhp	0	help	par_id3153976	3			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_RANG\"\>Returns the rank of a number in a sample.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060185.xhp	0	help	hd_id3159206	4			0	lt	Syntax				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060185.xhp	0	help	par_id3153250	5			0	lt	RANK(Value; Data; Type)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060185.xhp	0	help	par_id3154543	6			0	lt	            \<emph\>Value\</emph\> is the value, whose rank is to be determined.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060185.xhp	0	help	par_id3149130	7			0	lt	            \<emph\>Data\</emph\> is the array or range of data in the sample.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060185.xhp	0	help	par_id3150215	8			0	lt	            \<emph\>Type\</emph\> (optional) is the sequence order.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060185.xhp	0	help	par_id9305398				0	lt	Type = 0 means descending from the last item of the array to the first (this is the default), 				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060185.xhp	0	help	par_id9996948				0	lt	Type = 1 means ascending from the first item of the range to the last.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060185.xhp	0	help	hd_id3143223	9			0	lt	Example				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060185.xhp	0	help	par_id3155919	10			0	lt	            \<item type=\"input\"\>=RANK(A10;A1:A50)\</item\> returns the ranking of the value in A10 in value range A1:A50. If \<item type=\"literal\"\>Value\</item\> does not exist within the range an error message is displayed.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060185.xhp	0	help	bm_id3153556				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>SKEW function\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060185.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153556	12			0	lt	SKEW				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060185.xhp	0	help	par_id3153485	13			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_SCHIEFE\"\>Returns the skewness of a distribution.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060185.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154733	14			0	lt	Syntax				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060185.xhp	0	help	par_id3151191	15			0	lt	SKEW(Number1; Number2; ...Number30)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060185.xhp	0	help	par_id3155757	16			0	lt	            \<emph\>Number1, Number2...Number30\</emph\> are numerical values or ranges.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060185.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153297	17			0	lt	Example				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060185.xhp	0	help	par_id3145118	18			0	lt	            \<item type=\"input\"\>=SKEW(A1:A50)\</item\> calculates the value of skew for the data referenced.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060185.xhp	0	help	bm_id3149051				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>regression lines;FORECAST function\</bookmark_value\>         \<bookmark_value\>extrapolations\</bookmark_value\>         \<bookmark_value\>FORECAST function\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060185.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149051	20			0	lt	FORECAST				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060185.xhp	0	help	par_id3153290	21			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_SCHAETZER\"\>Extrapolates future values based on existing x and y values.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060185.xhp	0	help	hd_id3151343	22			0	lt	Syntax				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060185.xhp	0	help	par_id3147404	23			0	lt	FORECAST(Value; DataY; DataX)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060185.xhp	0	help	par_id3148743	24			0	lt	            \<emph\>Value\</emph\> is the x value, for which the y value on the linear regression is to be returned.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060185.xhp	0	help	par_id3146325	25			0	lt	            \<emph\>DataY\</emph\> is the array or range of known y's.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060185.xhp	0	help	par_id3150536	26			0	lt	            \<emph\>DataX\</emph\> is the array or range of known x's.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060185.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147416	27			0	lt	Example				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060185.xhp	0	help	par_id3157874	28			0	lt	            \<item type=\"input\"\>=FORECAST(50;A1:A50;B1;B50)\</item\> returns the Y value expected for the X value of 50 if the X and Y values in both references are linked by a linear trend.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060185.xhp	0	help	bm_id3149143				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>STDEV function\</bookmark_value\>         \<bookmark_value\>standard deviations in statistics;based on a sample\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060185.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149143	30			0	lt	STDEV				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060185.xhp	0	help	par_id3146888	31			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_STABW\"\>Estimates the standard deviation based on a sample.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060185.xhp	0	help	hd_id3146815	32			0	lt	Syntax				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060185.xhp	0	help	par_id3149946	33			0	lt	STDEV(Number1; Number2; ...Number30)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060185.xhp	0	help	par_id3157904	34			0	lt	            \<emph\>Number1, Number2, ... Number30\</emph\> are numerical values or ranges representing a sample based on an entire population.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060185.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150650	35			0	lt	Example				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060185.xhp	0	help	par_id3149434	36			0	lt	            \<item type=\"input\"\>=STDEV(A1:A50)\</item\> returns the estimated standard deviation based on the data referenced.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060185.xhp	0	help	bm_id3144745				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>STDEVA function\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060185.xhp	0	help	hd_id3144745	186			0	lt	STDEVA				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060185.xhp	0	help	par_id3151234	187			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_STABWA\"\>Calculates the standard deviation of an estimation based on a sample.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060185.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148884	188			0	lt	Syntax				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060185.xhp	0	help	par_id3147422	189			0	lt	STDEVA(Value1;Value2;...Value30)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060185.xhp	0	help	par_id3154547	190			0	lt	            \<emph\>Value1, Value2, ...Value30\</emph\> are values or ranges representing a sample derived from an entire population. Text has the value 0.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060185.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155829	191			0	lt	Example				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060185.xhp	0	help	par_id3148581	192			0	lt	            \<item type=\"input\"\>=STDEVA(A1:A50)\</item\> returns the estimated standard deviation based on the data referenced.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060185.xhp	0	help	bm_id3149734				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>STDEVP function\</bookmark_value\>         \<bookmark_value\>standard deviations in statistics;based on a population\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060185.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149734	38			0	lt	STDEVP				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060185.xhp	0	help	par_id3149187	39			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_STABWN\"\>Calculates the standard deviation based on the entire population.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060185.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154387	40			0	lt	Syntax				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060185.xhp	0	help	par_id3154392	41			0	lt	STDEVP(Number1;Number2;...Number30)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060185.xhp	0	help	par_id3155261	42			0	lt	            \<emph\>Number 1,Number 2,...Number 30\</emph\> are numerical values or ranges representing a sample based on an entire population.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060185.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145591	43			0	lt	Example				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060185.xhp	0	help	par_id3153933	44			0	lt	            \<item type=\"input\"\>=STDEVP(A1:A50)\</item\> returns a standard deviation of the data referenced.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060185.xhp	0	help	bm_id3154522				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>STDEVPA function\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060185.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154522	194			0	lt	STDEVPA				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060185.xhp	0	help	par_id3149549	195			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_STABWNA\"\>Calculates the standard deviation based on the entire population.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060185.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155950	196			0	lt	Syntax				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060185.xhp	0	help	par_id3146851	197			0	lt	STDEVPA(Value1;Value2;...Value30)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060185.xhp	0	help	par_id3153109	198			0	lt	            \<emph\>Value1,value2,...value30\</emph\> are values or ranges representing a sample derived from an entire population. Text has the value 0.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060185.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154506	199			0	lt	Example				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060185.xhp	0	help	par_id3145163	200			0	lt	            \<item type=\"input\"\>=STDEVPA(A1:A50)\</item\> returns the standard deviation of the data referenced.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060185.xhp	0	help	bm_id3155928				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>STANDARDIZE function\</bookmark_value\>         \<bookmark_value\>converting;random variables, into normalized values\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060185.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155928	46			0	lt	STANDARDIZE				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060185.xhp	0	help	par_id3149883	47			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_STANDARDISIERUNG\"\>Converts a random variable to a normalized value.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060185.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154330	48			0	lt	Syntax				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060185.xhp	0	help	par_id3150132	49			0	lt	STANDARDIZE(Number; Mean; StDev)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060185.xhp	0	help	par_id3159139	50			0	lt	            \<emph\>Number\</emph\> is the value to be standardized.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060185.xhp	0	help	par_id3145241	51			0	lt	            \<emph\>Mean\</emph\> is the arithmetic mean of the distribution.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060185.xhp	0	help	par_id3148874	52			0	lt	            \<emph\>StDev\</emph\> is the standard deviation of the distribution.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060185.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145351	53			0	lt	Example				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060185.xhp	0	help	par_id3156067	54			0	lt	            \<item type=\"input\"\>=STANDARDIZE(11;10;1)\</item\> returns 1. The value 11 in a normal distribution with a mean of 10 and a standard deviation of 1 is as much above the mean of 10, as the value 1 is above the mean of the standard normal distribution.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060185.xhp	0	help	bm_id3157986				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>NORMSINV function\</bookmark_value\>         \<bookmark_value\>normal distribution;inverse of standard\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060185.xhp	0	help	hd_id3157986	56			0	lt	NORMSINV				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060185.xhp	0	help	par_id3151282	57			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_STANDNORMINV\"\>Returns the inverse of the standard normal cumulative distribution.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060185.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153261	58			0	lt	Syntax				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060185.xhp	0	help	par_id3154195	59			0	lt	NORMINV(Number)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060185.xhp	0	help	par_id3148772	60			0	lt	            \<emph\>Number\</emph\> is the probability to which the inverse standard normal distribution is calculated.				20130618 17:22:18
