helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\solver.xhp	0	help	par_id8531449				0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\".\" visibility=\"hidden\"\>Enter the cell range that can be changed.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\solver.xhp	0	help	par_id9183935				0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\".\" visibility=\"hidden\"\>Enter a cell reference.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\solver.xhp	0	help	par_id946684				0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\".\" visibility=\"hidden\"\>Select an operator from the list.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\solver.xhp	0	help	par_id9607226				0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\".\" visibility=\"hidden\"\>Enter a value or a cell reference.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\solver.xhp	0	help	par_id1939451				0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\".\" visibility=\"hidden\"\>Click to shrink or restore the dialog. You can click or select cells in the sheet. You can enter a cell reference manually in the input box.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\solver.xhp	0	help	par_id9038972				0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\".\" visibility=\"hidden\"\>Click to remove the row from the list. Any rows from below this row move up.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\solver.xhp	0	help	par_id2423780				0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\".\" visibility=\"hidden\"\>Opens the Options dialog.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\solver.xhp	0	help	par_id2569658				0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\".\" visibility=\"hidden\"\>Click to solve the equation with the current settings. The dialog settings are retained until you close the current document.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\solver.xhp	0	help	par_id5474410				0	lt	To solve equations with the solver				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\solver.xhp	0	help	par_id2216559				0	lt	The goal of the solver process is to find those variable values of an equation that result in an optimized value in the \<emph\>target cell\</emph\>, also named the "objective". You can choose whether the value in the target cell should be a maximum, a minimum, or approaching a given value.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\solver.xhp	0	help	par_id7869502				0	lt	The initial variable values are inserted in a rectangular cell range that you enter in the \<emph\>By changing cells\</emph\> box. 				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\solver.xhp	0	help	par_id9852900				0	lt	You can define a series of limiting conditions that set constraints for some cells. For example, you can set the constraint that one of the variables or cells must not be bigger than another variable, or not bigger than a given value. You can also define the constraint that one or more variables must be integers (values without decimals), or binary values (where only 0 and 1 are allowed).				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\solver.xhp	0	help	par_id5323953				0	lt	The default solver engine supports only linear equations.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\solver_options.xhp	0	help	tit				0	lt	Options				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\solver_options.xhp	0	help	hd_id2794274				0	lt	Options				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\solver_options.xhp	0	help	par_id6776940				0	lt	The Options dialog for the \<link href=\"text/scalc/01/solver.xhp\"\>Solver\</link\> is used to set some options.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\solver_options.xhp	0	help	par_id393993				0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\".\" visibility=\"hidden\"\>Select a solver engine. The listbox is disabled if only one solver engine is installed. Solver engines can be installed as extensions.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\solver_options.xhp	0	help	par_id5871761				0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\".\" visibility=\"hidden\"\>Configure the current solver.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\solver_options.xhp	0	help	par_id6531266				0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\".\" visibility=\"hidden\"\>If the current entry in the Settings listbox allows to edit a value, you can click the Edit button. A dialog opens where you can change the value.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\solver_options.xhp	0	help	par_id3912778				0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\".\" visibility=\"hidden\"\>Enter or change the value.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\solver_options.xhp	0	help	par_id3163853				0	lt	Use the Options dialog to configure the current solver engine.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\solver_options.xhp	0	help	par_id121158				0	lt	You can install more solver engines as extensions, if available. Open Tools - Extension Manager and browse to the Extensions web site to search for extensions.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\solver_options.xhp	0	help	par_id3806878				0	lt	Select the solver engine to use and to configure from the listbox. The listbox is disabled if onle one solver engine is installed.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\solver_options.xhp	0	help	par_id130619				0	lt	In the Settings box, check all settings that you want to use for the current goal seeking operation. If the current option offers different values, the Edit button is enabled. Click Edit to open a dialog where you can change the value.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\solver_options.xhp	0	help	par_id9999694				0	lt	Click OK to accept the changes and to go back to the \<link href=\"text/scalc/01/solver.xhp\"\>Solver\</link\> dialog.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\text2columns.xhp	0	help	tit				0	lt	Text to Columns				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\text2columns.xhp	0	help	bm_id8004394				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>text to columns\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\text2columns.xhp	0	help	hd_id2300180				0	lt	\<link href=\"text/scalc/01/text2columns.xhp\"\>Text to Columns\</link\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\text2columns.xhp	0	help	par_id655232				0	lt	\<variable id=\"text2columns\"\>Opens the Text to Columns dialog, where you enter settings to expand the contents of selected cells to multiple cells. \</variable\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\text2columns.xhp	0	help	hd_id9599597				0	lt	To expand cell contents to multiple cells				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\text2columns.xhp	0	help	par_id2021546				0	lt	You can expand cells that contain comma separated values (CSV) into multiple cells in the same row. 				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\text2columns.xhp	0	help	par_id2623981				0	lt	For example, cell A1 contains the comma separated values \<item type=\"literal\"\>1,2,3,4\</item\>, and cell A2 contains the text \<item type=\"literal\"\>A,B,C,D\</item\>. 				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\text2columns.xhp	0	help	par_id7242042				0	lt	Select the cell or cells that you want to expand.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\text2columns.xhp	0	help	par_id6999420				0	lt	Choose \<emph\>Data - Text to Columns\</emph\>.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\text2columns.xhp	0	help	par_id6334116				0	lt	You see the Text to Columns dialog.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\text2columns.xhp	0	help	par_id9276406				0	lt	Select the separator options. The preview shows how the current cell contents will be transformed into multiple cells.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\text2columns.xhp	0	help	par_id8523819				0	lt	You can select a fixed width and then click the ruler on the preview to set cell breakup positions.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\text2columns.xhp	0	help	par_id1517380				0	lt	You can select or enter separator characters to define the positions of breaking points. The separator characters are removed from the resulting cell contents.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\text2columns.xhp	0	help	par_id7110812				0	lt	In the example, you select the comma as a delimiter character. Cells A1 and A2 will be expanded to four columns each. A1 contains 1, B1 contains 2, and so on.				20130618 17:22:18
