helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153514	1			0	lt	\<variable id=\"zahlenformatcodes\"\>\<link href=\"text/shared/01/05020301.xhp\" name=\"Number Format Codes\"\>Number Format Codes\</link\>\</variable\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3150467	88			0	lt	Number format codes can consist of up to three sections separated by a semicolon (;).				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3150146	108			0	lt	In a number format code with two sections, the first section applies to positive values and zero, and the second section applies to negative values.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3158442	109			0	lt	In a number format code with three sections, the first section applies to positive values, the second section to negative values, and the third section to the value zero.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3155069	110			0	lt	You can also assign conditions to the three sections, so that the format is only applied if a condition is met.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	hd_id3151262	229			0	lt	Decimal Places and Significant Digits				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3153624	3			0	lt	Use zero (0) or the number sign (#) as placeholders in your number format code to represent numbers. The (#) only displays significant digits, while the (0) displays zeroes if there are fewer digits in the number than in the number format.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3153323	107			0	lt	Use question marks (?) to represent the number of digits to include in the numerator and the denominator of a fraction. Fractions that do not fit the pattern that you define are displayed as floating point numbers.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3148440	4			0	lt	If a number contains more digits to the right of the decimal delimiter than there are placeholders in the format, the number is rounded accordingly. If a number contains more digits to the left of the decimal delimiter than there are placeholders in the format, the entire number is displayed. Use the following list as a guide for using placeholders when you create a number format code:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3150902	86			0	lt	Placeholders				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3157896	87			0	lt	Explanation				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3152801	5			0	lt	#				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3145090	6			0	lt	Does not display extra zeros.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3147088	7			0	lt	0 (Zero)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3150774	8			0	lt	Displays extra zeros if the number has less places than zeros in the format.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_idN1087E				0	lt	Examples				20130618 17:22:18
