helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150948	119			0	lt	returns field names with the field content "Ms."				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150333	120			0	lt	LIKE 'g?ve'				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3147332	121			0	lt	returns field names with field content such as "give" and "gave".				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3146062	122			0	lt	LIKE 'S*'				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3155350	123			0	lt	returns data fields with field contents such as "Sun".				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3152883	124			0	lt	BETWEEN 10 AND 20				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3159406	125			0	lt	returns field names with field content between the values 10 and 20. (The fields can be either text fields or number fields).				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3148765	126			0	lt	IN (1; 3; 5; 7)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149712	127			0	lt	returns field names with the values 1, 3, 5, 7. If the field name contains an item number, for example, you can create a query that returns the item having the specified number.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3152948	128			0	lt	NOT IN ('Smith')				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3147279	129			0	lt	returns field names that do not contain "Smith".				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3157998	252			0	lt	\<emph\>Date fields\</emph\> are represented as #Date# to clearly identify them as dates. The date condition will be reproduced in the resulting SQL statement in the following ODBC - compliant way:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153734	253			0	lt	Date				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3159131	254			0	lt	{D'YYYY-MM-DD'}				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153937	255			0	lt	Date time				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3146090	256			0	lt	{D'YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS'}				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3155947	257			0	lt	Time				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150964	258			0	lt	{D'HH:MM:SS'}				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3151220	260			0	lt	$[officename] also supports the following \<emph\>Escape sequences\</emph\> known from ODBC and JDBC:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3157975	261			0	lt	Date				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149753	262			0	lt	{d 'YYYY-MM-DD'}				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3156318	263			0	lt	Time				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3151280	264			0	lt	{t 'HH:MI:SS[.SS]'} - [ ] optional				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153264	265			0	lt	DateTime				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153981	266			0	lt	{ts 'YYYY-MM-DD HH:MI:SS[.SS]'} - [ ] optional				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149539	267			0	lt	Example: select {d '1999-12-31'} from world.years				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3146073	268			0	lt	\<emph\>Like \</emph\>Escape Sequence: {escape 'escape-character'}				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150661	269			0	lt	Example: select * from Item where ItemName like 'The *%' {escape '*'}				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3148541	270			0	lt	The example will give you all of the entries where the item name begins with 'The *'. This means that you can also search for characters that would otherwise be interpreted as placeholders, such as *, ?, _, % or the period.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150572	271			0	lt	\<emph\>Outer Join\</emph\> Escape Sequence: {oj outer-join}				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3156052	272			0	lt	Example: select Article.* from {oj item LEFT OUTER JOIN orders ON item.no=orders.ANR}				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153674	109			0	lt	Querying text fields				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149134	110			0	lt	To query the content of a text field, you must put the expression between single quotes. The distinction between uppercase and lowercase letters depends on the database in use. LIKE, by definition, is case-sensitive (though some databases don't see it that strict).				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149302	111			0	lt	Querying date fields				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150510	112			0	lt	Even if you want to filter by a date, you must place the expression between single quotation marks. The following formats are valid: YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS and YYYY/MM/DD HH:MM:SS as well as YYYY.MM.DD HH:MM:SS				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150427	228			0	lt	Querying Yes/No fields				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149523	229			0	lt	To query Yes/No fields, use the following syntax for dBASE tables:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153180	230			0	lt	Status				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3147481	231			0	lt	Query criterion				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3155187	232			0	lt	Example				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3156092	233			0	lt	Yes				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3152414	234			0	lt	for dBASE tables: not equal to any given value				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3151265	236			0	lt	=1 returns all records where the Yes/No field has the status "Yes" or "On" (selected in black),				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3152450	238			0	lt	No				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150997	239			0	lt	.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3155331	240			0	lt	=0 returns all records for which the Yes/No field has the status "No" or "Off" (no selection).				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154179	241			0	lt	Null				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3147035	242			0	lt	IS NULL				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3159385	243			0	lt	IS NULL returns all records for which the Yes/No field has neither of the states Yes or No (selected in gray).				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3157888	244			0	lt	The syntax depends on the database system used. You should also note that Yes/No fields can be defined differently (only 2 states instead of 3).				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145772	113			0	lt	Parameter queries				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3157312	114			0	lt	You must place the variable between square brackets (=[x]) to create a query with variable parameters. Alternatively, you can use an equal sign followed by a colon (=:x). When the query is executed, the program will display a dialog asking you for the expression to which the variable x should be assigned.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150818	273			0	lt	If you query several parameters at the same time, you will see a list field in the dialog containing all of the parameters and an input line alongside each one. Enter the values, preferably from top to bottom, and press the Enter key after each line.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3157855	115			0	lt	Parameter queries with placeholders (*, _) or special characters (for example, ?) are not possible.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3157537	116			0	lt	If you formulate a parameter query and you save it with the variables, you can later create a query in which only the variables have to be replaced by the expressions that you want. $[officename] asks for these variables in a dialog as soon as you open the query.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3151035	139			0	lt	Parameter Input				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153596	140			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_QRYDGN_ROW_FUNCTION\"\>The \<emph\>Parameter Input\</emph\> dialog asks you which variables you defined in the query. Enter a value for each query variable and confirm by clicking \<emph\>OK\</emph\>.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150585	138			0	lt	Parameter queries are also used for \<link href=\"text/shared/02/01170203.xhp\" name=\"subforms\"\>subforms\</link\>, since they work exclusively with queries for which the values to be invoked are read internally from a variable.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153645	274			0	lt	A parameter query can have the following form in an SQL statement:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3156731	275			0	lt	select * from 'addresses' where 'name' = :placeholder				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145181	135			0	lt	SQL Mode				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3147013	220			0	lt	SQL stands for "Structured Query Language" and describes instructions for updating and administering relational databases.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3152570	136			0	lt	In $[officename] you do not need any knowledge of SQL for most queries, since you do not have to enter the SQL code. If you create a query in the query design, $[officename] automatically converts your instructions into the corresponding SQL syntax. If, with the help of the \<emph\>Switch Design View On/Off \</emph\>button, you change to the SQL view, you can see the SQL commands for a query that has been created previously.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3152412	226			0	lt	You can formulate your query directly in the SQL code. Note, however, that the special syntax is dependent upon the database system that you use.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3146842	227			0	lt	If you enter the SQL code manually, you can create SQL-specific queries that are not supported by the graphical interface in \<emph\>Query design\</emph\>. These queries must be executed in native SQL mode.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149632	223			0	lt	By clicking the \<link href=\"text/shared/02/14030000.xhp\" name=\"Run SQL command directly\"\>\<emph\>Run SQL command directly\</emph\>\</link\> icon in the SQL view, you can formulate a query that is not processed by $[officename].				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010101.xhp	0	help	tit				0	lt	Join Properties				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010101.xhp	0	help	bm_id3154015				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>links;relational databases (Base)\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>inner joins (Base)\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>joins in databases (Base)\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>left joins (Base)\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>right joins (Base)\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>full joins (Base)\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010101.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154015	1			0	lt	Join Properties				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010101.xhp	0	help	par_id3151205	2			0	lt	If you double-click a connection between two linked fields in the query design, or if you choose \<emph\>Insert - New Relation\</emph\>, the \<emph\>Join Properties\</emph\> dialog appears. These properties will be used in all queries created in the future.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010101.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155066	17			0	lt	Tables involved				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010101.xhp	0	help	par_id3153924	16			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_DLG_QRY_RIGHT_TABLE\"\>Specifies two different tables that you want to join.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010101.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155766	15			0	lt	Fields involved				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010101.xhp	0	help	par_id3148994	14			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\".\"\>Specifies two data fields that will be joined by a relation.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010101.xhp	0	help	hd_id3159267	3			0	lt	Options				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010101.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147340	13			0	lt	Type				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010101.xhp	0	help	par_id3152482	4			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_DLG_QRY_JOINTYPE\"\>Specifies the link type of the selected link.\</ahelp\> Some databases support only a subset of the possible types.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010101.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155334	5			0	lt	Inner Join				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010101.xhp	0	help	par_id3155936	6			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_DLG_QRY_JOINTYPE\"\>With the internal join, the results table contains only the records for which the content of the linked fields is the same.\</ahelp\> In $[officename] SQL this type of link is created by a corresponding WHERE clause.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010101.xhp	0	help	hd_id3156372	7			0	lt	Left Join				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010101.xhp	0	help	par_id3166450	8			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_DLG_QRY_JOINTYPE\"\>With the left join, the results table contains all fields of the left table and only those fields of the right table for which the content of the linked fields is the same.\</ahelp\> In $[officename] SQL this type of link corresponds to the LEFT OUTER JOIN command.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010101.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155607	9			0	lt	Right Join				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010101.xhp	0	help	par_id3150647	10			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_DLG_QRY_JOINTYPE\"\>With the right join, the results table contains all fields of the right table and only those fields of the left table for which the content of the linked fields is the same.\</ahelp\> In $[officename] SQL this type of link corresponds to the RIGHT OUTER JOIN command.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010101.xhp	0	help	hd_id3158215	11			0	lt	Full Join				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010101.xhp	0	help	par_id3163665	12			0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_DLG_QRY_JOINTYPE\"\>For a full join, the results table contains all fields of the left and right tables.\</ahelp\> In the SQL of $[officename] this type of link corresponds to the FULL OUTER JOIN command.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010101.xhp	0	help	hd_id0305200912031976				0	lt	Natural				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010101.xhp	0	help	par_id0305200912031977				0	lt	\<ahelp hid=\".\"\>Inserts the keyword NATURAL into the SQL statement that defines the relation. The relation joins all columns that have the same column name in both tables. The resulting joined table contains only one column for each pair of equally named columns.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\04000000.xhp	0	help	tit				0	lt	Forms				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\04000000.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150476	1			0	lt	\<link href=\"text/shared/explorer/database/04000000.xhp\" name=\"Forms\"\>Forms\</link\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\04000000.xhp	0	help	bm_id3156136				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>forms; general information (Base)\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\04000000.xhp	0	help	par_id3156136	2			0	lt	Forms can be used to enter or to edit existing database contents easily.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\04000000.xhp	0	help	hd_id3157910	12			0	lt	\<link href=\"text/shared/autopi/01090000.xhp\" name=\"Form Wizard\"\>FormWizard\</link\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\04000000.xhp	0	help	hd_id3156003	14			0	lt	\<link href=\"text/shared/02/01170000.xhp\" name=\"Form Controls\"\>Form Controls\</link\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\04000000.xhp	0	help	par_id3156156	23			0	lt	The Form Controls toolbar offers the tools required to create a form in a text, table, drawing, or presentation document.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\04000000.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155388	24			0	lt	\<link href=\"text/shared/explorer/database/04030000.xhp\" name=\"Form in Design Mode\"\>Form in Design Mode\</link\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\04000000.xhp	0	help	par_id3150504	25			0	lt	In design mode, the form is designed and the properties of the form and the controls contained in it are defined.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\04000000.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149784	30			0	lt	\<link href=\"text/shared/main0213.xhp\" name=\"Sorting and Filtering Data\"\>Sorting and Filtering Data\</link\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\04000000.xhp	0	help	par_id3151384	28			0	lt	You will find the sorting and filter functions in the toolbar when you open a form in user mode.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\04000000.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148944	29			0	lt	\<link href=\"text/shared/02/01170203.xhp\" name=\"Subforms\"\>Subforms\</link\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\04030000.xhp	0	help	tit				0	lt	Form Design				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\04030000.xhp	0	help	bm_id3148668				0	lt	\<bookmark_value\>forms; designing (Base)\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\04030000.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148668	1			0	lt	\<variable id=\"formularentwurf\"\>\<link href=\"text/shared/explorer/database/04030000.xhp\" name=\"Form Design\"\>Form Design\</link\>\</variable\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\04030000.xhp	0	help	par_id3154230	2			0	lt	Any $[officename] document can be expanded into a form. Simply add one or more form controls.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\04030000.xhp	0	help	par_id3145382	3			0	lt	Open the Form Controls toolbar. The Form Controls toolbar contains the \<link href=\"text/shared/02/01170000.xhp\" name=\"functions\"\>functions\</link\> needed to edit a form. More functions can be found in the \<emph\>Form Design\</emph\> bar and \<emph\>More Controls\</emph\> bar.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\04030000.xhp	0	help	par_id3153146	5			0	lt	In the form design you can \<link href=\"text/shared/02/01170000.xhp\" name=\"include controls\"\>include controls\</link\>, \<link href=\"text/shared/02/01170100.xhp\" name=\"apply properties\"\>apply properties\</link\> to them, define \<link href=\"text/shared/02/01170200.xhp\" name=\"Form properties\"\>Form properties\</link\>, and \<link href=\"text/shared/02/01170203.xhp\" name=\"define subforms\"\>define subforms\</link\>.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\04030000.xhp	0	help	par_id3154924	9			0	lt	The\<emph\> Form Navigator\</emph\> icon \<image id=\"img_id3156002\" src=\"res/commandimagelist/sc_showfmexplorer.png\" width=\"4.23mm\" height=\"4.23mm\"\>\<alt id=\"alt_id3156002\"\>Icon\</alt\>\</image\> on the Form Design bar opens the \<link href=\"text/shared/02/01170600.xhp\" name=\"Form Navigator\"\>\<emph\>Form Navigator\</emph\>\</link\>.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\04030000.xhp	0	help	par_id3153968	6			0	lt	The \<link href=\"text/shared/02/01171000.xhp\" name=\"Open in Design Mode\"\>\<emph\>Open in Design Mode\</emph\>\</link\> icon \<image id=\"img_id1871395\" src=\"res/commandimagelist/sc_openreadonly.png\" width=\"5.59mm\" height=\"5.59mm\"\>\<alt id=\"alt_id1871395\"\>Icon\</alt\>\</image\> allows you to save a form document so that it always opens in editing mode.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\04030000.xhp	0	help	par_id3154948	8			0	lt	If there is an error when assigning properties to the objects contained in the form (for example, when assigning a non-existent database table to an object), a corresponding error message appears. This error message may contain a \<emph\>More\</emph\> button. \<ahelp hid=\"dummy\"\>If you click \<emph\>More\</emph\>, a dialog displaying more information about the current problem appears.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
